(window.__DP=window.__DP||[])[2]="in nulliparous women.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[22,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F181\"},{\"id\":\"F182\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":9,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"The most common cause of Post-menopausal bleeding (PMB) is:\",\"o\":[\"Endometrial polyp\",\"Endometrial hyperplasia\",\"Uterine leiomyoma\",\"Endometrial carcinoma\",\"Endometrial or vaginal atrophy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometrial polyps can cause postmenopausal spotting and are important focal lesions, but atrophic change is more common overall.\",\"Endometrial hyperplasia is associated with unopposed estrogen and may cause bleeding, but it is less frequent than atrophic bleeding.\",\"Leiomyomas usually regress after menopause as estrogen levels fall; new or enlarging masses merit evaluation but are not the commonest PMB cause.\",\"Endometrial carcinoma must be excluded in any postmenopausal bleeding because of its clinical importance, although it accounts for a minority of presentations.\",\"Endometrial and vaginal atrophy from estrogen deficiency are the most common benign causes of postmenopausal bleeding.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmenopausal bleeding is common and is most often caused by atrophic endometrium or vaginal epithelium.\",\"Even though atrophy is statistically most likely, every episode of PMB requires assessment because endometrial cancer may present with only light spotting.\",\"Estrogen deficiency produces a thin, fragile epithelium with exposed superficial vessels, making minor trauma sufficient to cause bleeding.\"],\"tk\":\"Atrophic endometrium or vaginal epithelium is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding, but malignancy must still be excluded.\",\"pr\":\"A thin endometrial echo on transvaginal ultrasound has a high negative predictive value for endometrial carcinoma in appropriately selected women.\",\"tp\":\"Document whether bleeding is truly postmenopausal, review tamoxifen or estrogen exposure, and arrange guideline-based endometrial evaluation rather than empiric treatment alone.\",\"cn\":\"Endometrial or vaginal atrophy is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding (PMB), though malignancy must be excluded.\",\"sl\":\"Causes of vaginal bleeding in postmenopausal women: endometrial atrophy 30%, polyps 30%, submucosal fibroids 20%, hyperplasia 8-15%, endometrial carcinoma 8-10%, ovarian, tubal or cervical malignancy 2%.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[48]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F182\"},{\"id\":\"F183\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":0,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Screening\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Rubella vaccination in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 9-week pregnant woman has no evidence of rubella immunity. Which counseling statement is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Administer MMR vaccine now because organogenesis is incomplete\",\"Delay MMR vaccination until breastfeeding has ended\",\"Give MMR vaccine after delivery; breastfeeding is not a contraindication\",\"Administer rubella vaccine at 16 weeks if serology remains negative\",\"No postpartum vaccination is needed if the infant is vaccinated\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"MMR is a live attenuated vaccine and is contraindicated during pregnancy, including the first trimester.\",\"Breastfeeding does not contraindicate MMR vaccination and does not meaningfully impair the maternal immune response.\",\"A rubella-nonimmune pregnant woman should receive MMR after delivery, and Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) specifically states that it is safe during breastfeeding.\",\"There is no gestational age at which routine MMR vaccination becomes acceptable during pregnancy; vaccination is deferred until after birth.\",\"Infant vaccination does not create maternal immunity; a susceptible mother still requires postpartum MMR to protect future pregnancies.\"],\"e\":[\"Rubella infection early in pregnancy can cause congenital rubella syndrome, but the live attenuated MMR vaccine is contraindicated during gestation.\",\"A pregnant woman who lacks rubella immunity should therefore be vaccinated immediately postpartum, not during pregnancy.\",\"Postpartum vaccination creates maternal IgG immunity before a future conception, and breastfeeding does not transmit clinically important vaccine infection to the infant.\"],\"tk\":\"Rubella-nonimmune pregnant women should receive MMR after delivery, and breastfeeding is not a contraindication.\",\"pr\":\"After MMR vaccination, patients capable of pregnancy should avoid conception for 28 days.\",\"tp\":\"Record rubella susceptibility in the antenatal chart and ensure the postpartum MMR dose is actually administered before discharge or promptly thereafter.\",\"cn\":\"Rubella vaccine is live attenuated (measles-mumps-rubella, MMR), so non-immune women are vaccinated postnatally; breastfeeding is not a contraindication.\",\"sl\":\"Prevention is by vaccination (childhood or post-natal). Rubella vaccine is live attenuated as part of the measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine, so 3 months contraception is advised after vaccination.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F183\"},{\"id\":\"F184\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Placental abruption recurrence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant woman with a previous placental abruption now has preeclampsia. Which factor most strongly explains her increased risk of another abruption?\",\"o\":[\"Maternal age under 25 years\",\"Nulliparity\",\"Fetal cephalic presentation\",\"Previous placental abruption\",\"Routine low-dose aspirin use\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Young maternal age is not the dominant recurrence predictor; advanced age may contribute modestly to placental complications.\",\"Nulliparity is not a stronger abruption risk factor than a documented previous abruption.\",\"A cephalic presentation has no causal relationship with placental separation.\",\"A history of placental abruption is one of the strongest predictors of recurrence in a later pregnancy.\",\"Appropriately prescribed low-dose aspirin is used for preeclampsia prevention in high-risk women and is not the principal explanation for recurrent abruption.\"],\"e\":[\"Placental abruption is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta and is associated with hypertension, smoking, cocaine exposure, trauma, and prior abruption.\",\"Although this patient also has preeclampsia, her previous abruption specifically confers a substantial recurrence risk in a subsequent pregnancy.\",\"Chronic placental vascular disease and decidual arteriopathy can predispose to retroplacental hemorrhage, linking hypertensive disease and recurrent abruption.\"],\"tk\":\"Previous placental abruption is a major risk factor for recurrence in a subsequent pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Abruption can be largely concealed, so the amount of visible vaginal bleeding may underestimate maternal blood loss.\",\"tp\":\"In a patient with suspected abruption, prioritize maternal hemodynamic status and fetal monitoring; ultrasound cannot reliably exclude the diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Previous placental abruption is the most predictive risk factor for abruption in a subsequent pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"The most predictive risk factor is abruption in a previous pregnancy: 4.4% recurrence, and 19-25% after two previous pregnancies complicated by abruption.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F184\"},{\"id\":\"F185\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hysteroscopy for HMB with intermenstrual bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 48-year-old patient presents with heavy menstrual bleeding and occasional intermenstrual spotting. Her transvaginal ultrasound (TVS) is normal, and her complete blood count (CBC) shows a hemoglobin level of 11.2 g/dL. The most appropriate next investigation is:\",\"o\":[\"Repeat transvaginal ultrasound in 12 months\",\"Outpatient hysteroscopy\",\"Routine coagulation profile only\",\"Diagnostic laparoscopy\",\"Serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125)\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A normal transvaginal ultrasound does not exclude small endometrial polyps or focal cavity pathology when persistent intermenstrual bleeding continues.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends outpatient hysteroscopy when heavy menstrual bleeding is accompanied by persistent intermenstrual bleeding because focal endometrial pathology may be present.\",\"A coagulation profile is targeted to a lifelong bleeding history or suspected hemostatic disorder; new intermenstrual spotting at age 48 calls for cavity assessment.\",\"Laparoscopy evaluates peritoneal and adnexal disease rather than the endometrial cavity and is not the next investigation for this bleeding pattern.\",\"Cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) is not a screening test for endometrial polyps or hyperplasia and is not indicated solely for HMB with intermenstrual bleeding.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent intermenstrual bleeding raises concern for polyps, submucosal fibroids, or endometrial pathology even when pelvic ultrasound is unrevealing.\",\"NICE therefore recommends outpatient hysteroscopy as the first-line cavity investigation for HMB with persistent intermenstrual bleeding.\",\"Direct visualization improves detection of focal intracavitary lesions that may be missed by ultrasound and permits targeted biopsy when clinically indicated.\"],\"tk\":\"Persistent intermenstrual bleeding with HMB is an indication for outpatient hysteroscopy to assess the uterine cavity.\",\"pr\":\"NICE advises that endometrial sampling for HMB should generally be obtained in the context of hysteroscopy, not as a blind biopsy.\",\"tp\":\"Clarify whether spotting is truly persistent and intermenstrual; that history changes the investigation pathway even when the ultrasound report is normal.\",\"cn\":\"Heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) with intermenstrual bleeding over 40 and normal ultrasound needs hysteroscopy to assess and sample the cavity.\",\"sl\":\"Endometrial sampling methods are dilatation and curettage (D&C), hysteroscopy and Pipelle; polyps are diagnosed by ultrasound, saline infusion sonography and hysteroscopy. Sample the endometrium at 40+ or with risk factors for unopposed oestrogen.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[50,60]],[\"L33_1\",[13,14,27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F185\"},{\"id\":\"F186\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Maternal circulatory adaptations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement is false regarding normal maternal circulatory adaptations in pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Blood flow to the skin increases\",\"Renal blood flow increases during early and mid-pregnancy\",\"Arteriovenous oxygen difference narrows as cardiac output rises\",\"Arterial blood flow to the lower extremities normally decreases as a primary physiologic adaptation\",\"Uterine blood flow rises markedly as pregnancy advances\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cutaneous blood flow increases during pregnancy, contributing to heat dissipation and warm skin.\",\"Renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration increase substantially in early pregnancy as systemic vasodilation and cardiac output rise.\",\"The arteriovenous oxygen difference decreases because cardiac output increases proportionally more than whole-body oxygen consumption.\",\"Pregnancy causes venous stasis in the legs from uterine compression, but a generalized physiologic decrease in arterial limb perfusion is not a normal adaptive feature.\",\"Uterine blood flow increases dramatically to meet placental and fetal metabolic demands.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy produces marked systemic vasodilation and increased cardiac output, redistributing flow toward the uterus, kidneys, skin, and breasts.\",\"Lower-limb symptoms largely reflect venous stasis and reduced venous return from caval and pelvic compression, not a normal reduction in arterial perfusion.\",\"Increased flow with reduced systemic vascular resistance helps meet the rising maternal-fetal oxygen demand while blood pressure typically falls modestly in mid-pregnancy.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal pregnancy increases cardiac output and perfusion of the uterus, kidneys, and skin; lower-limb venous stasis should not be confused with reduced arterial flow.\",\"pr\":\"Glomerular filtration rises by roughly 40–50%, which lowers normal serum creatinine in pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"When reviewing renal tests in pregnancy, treat a “normal nonpregnant” creatinine with caution because pregnancy normally lowers it.\",\"cn\":\"Pregnancy increases blood flow to the uterus, kidneys and skin; lower-limb symptoms reflect venous compression, not reduced arterial flow.\",\"sl\":\"Renal plasma flow rises 50-80% by the second trimester. Uterine blood flow rises from 50 to 500-700 mL/min, about 10-15% of cardiac output at term.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[37,54]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F186\"},{\"id\":\"F187\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"LNG-IUS for HRT endometrial protection and contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 42-year-old perimenopausal woman with an intact uterus wants estrogen therapy for menopausal symptoms and effective contraception for the next 5 years. Which option is the best progestogen/contraceptive component?\",\"o\":[\"Cyclic oral micronized progesterone without contraception\",\"52-mg levonorgestrel intrauterine system\",\"Copper intrauterine device\",\"Progestogen-only pill with estrogen omitted\",\"Mefenamic acid\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oral micronized progesterone can protect the endometrium during estrogen therapy but does not itself provide reliable contraception.\",\"A 52-mg LNG-IUS provides highly effective contraception and local progestogenic endometrial protection when systemic estrogen is used.\",\"A copper intrauterine device (IUD) is effective contraception but contains no progestogen and therefore does not protect the endometrium from systemic estrogen.\",\"A progestogen-only pill can provide contraception, but omitting desired systemic estrogen would not address the stated vasomotor-treatment goal.\",\"Mefenamic acid can reduce menstrual blood loss and dysmenorrhea but offers neither contraception nor endometrial opposition to estrogen.\"],\"e\":[\"Systemic estrogen in a woman with an intact uterus requires progestogenic endometrial protection to reduce hyperplasia and cancer risk.\",\"The 52-mg LNG-IUS can supply that local progestogen effect while also providing long-acting reversible contraception during perimenopause.\",\"Levonorgestrel induces endometrial decidualization and atrophy, counteracting estrogen-driven proliferation with low systemic progestogen exposure.\"],\"tk\":\"A 52-mg LNG-IUS can provide both contraception and endometrial protection for a woman using systemic estrogen.\",\"pr\":\"Menopausal hormone therapy is not contraceptive, so pregnancy prevention is still needed until contraception can safely be stopped by age and menstrual-history criteria.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm the exact LNG-IUS product and duration licensed for contraception/endometrial protection in the local formulary before relying on it for both purposes.\",\"cn\":\"A levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) gives both endometrial protection with estrogen therapy and contraception in perimenopause.\",\"sl\":\"The Mirena intrauterine system (IUS) can be used in peri- and postmenopausal women as the progestogen component of hormone replacement therapy (HRT), licensed for 5 years; the levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) provides both contraception and endometrial protection. HRT is not contraception.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_1\",[11,13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F187\"},{\"id\":\"F188\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Low-transverse versus classical cesarean incision\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about a low-transverse cesarean uterine incision, compared with a classical incision, is false?\",\"o\":[\"It is generally easier to repair\",\"It is associated with less blood loss\",\"It has a lower risk of rupture in a subsequent pregnancy\",\"It is the preferred uterine incision when the lower segment is accessible\",\"It carries a higher subsequent uterine rupture risk than a classical incision\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A low-transverse incision is generally easier to close because it lies in the thinner, less vascular lower uterine segment.\",\"Lower-segment transverse cesarean is associated with less operative blood loss than a vertical incision through the contractile corpus.\",\"The healed low-transverse scar has a lower future rupture risk than a classical scar.\",\"When feasible, the low-transverse approach is preferred because of lower bleeding and better future-pregnancy scar behavior.\",\"A classical uterine incision carries the greater subsequent rupture risk because it traverses the active upper uterine segment.\"],\"e\":[\"Cesarean type is defined by the uterine incision, and the low-transverse incision is preferred whenever access and obstetric circumstances permit.\",\"Compared with a classical incision, it generally causes less bleeding, is easier to repair, and has a much lower future rupture risk.\",\"The upper uterine corpus contracts forcefully in later pregnancy and labour, so a classical scar is exposed to greater tensile stress in future pregnancies.\"],\"tk\":\"Low-transverse cesarean scars have a substantially lower future uterine rupture risk than classical scars.\",\"pr\":\"A prior classical cesarean is generally a contraindication to labour after cesarean because rupture can occur even before active labour.\",\"tp\":\"Always verify the operative report when the previous uterine incision is uncertain; the abdominal skin scar does not reliably reveal the uterine incision type.\",\"cn\":\"A low-transverse cesarean scar has much lower future uterine rupture risk (about 0.5%) than a classical scar (about 5%).\",\"sl\":\"Uterine rupture incidence: unscarred uterus 0.5-2.0 per 10,000; vaginal birth after cesarean (VBAC) depends on the previous scar; classical cesarean 5%; lower uterine segment cesarean section (LUSCS) 0.5%.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[55]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F188\"},{\"id\":\"F189\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Prevention of postoperative wound infection\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which measure most directly reduces the risk of postoperative wound infection after cesarean or gynecologic surgery?\",\"o\":[\"Routine prolonged postoperative antibiotics for every patient\",\"Appropriate perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis\",\"Increasing perioperative glucose concentration\",\"Delaying skin preparation until after incision\",\"Withholding venous thromboembolism prophylaxis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Prolonged postoperative antibiotics are not routinely needed after clean-contaminated surgery and increase adverse effects and antimicrobial resistance.\",\"Timely perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis lowers surgical-site infection risk by ensuring effective tissue concentrations when bacterial contamination occurs.\",\"Hyperglycemia impairs neutrophil function and is associated with higher surgical-site infection risk rather than protection.\",\"Proper pre-incision skin antisepsis reduces microbial burden; delaying preparation until after incision defeats its preventive purpose.\",\"Withholding venous thromboembolism (VTE) prophylaxis does not prevent wound infection and may expose high-risk patients to avoidable thromboembolism.\"],\"e\":[\"Surgical-site infection prevention depends on reducing bacterial inoculation and preserving host defenses.\",\"The most direct evidence-based measure is appropriate antibiotic prophylaxis before incision, together with skin antisepsis and good glycemic control.\",\"Antibiotic concentrations must be present in tissue at the time of initial contamination, which is why timing around incision matters more than routine prolonged postoperative dosing.\"],\"tk\":\"Correctly timed perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis reduces surgical-site infection after cesarean and gynecologic surgery.\",\"pr\":\"Obesity and diabetes increase wound complications partly through poor tissue oxygenation, dead space, and impaired immune function.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm the prophylactic antibiotic has been administered before incision and redose when prolonged surgery or major blood loss requires it.\",\"cn\":\"Correctly timed perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis reduces surgical site infection after cesarean and gynaecologic surgery.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F189\"},{\"id\":\"F190\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Epidural-induced maternal hypotension\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which maternal effect can occur rapidly after initiation of effective epidural analgesia in labour?\",\"o\":[\"Hyperglycemia from pancreatic stimulation\",\"Severe hypertension from parasympathetic blockade\",\"Maternal hyperthermia within seconds\",\"Polycythemia from hemoconcentration\",\"Hypotension from sympathetic blockade\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Epidural local anesthetic does not acutely stimulate pancreatic glucose release; hyperglycemia is not an immediate neuraxial effect.\",\"Neuraxial blockade reduces sympathetic vascular tone, so hypertension is not the expected immediate hemodynamic response.\",\"Maternal temperature may rise during prolonged epidural use, but fever is not the characteristic immediate effect after dosing.\",\"Epidural analgesia does not acutely increase red-cell mass; polycythemia cannot develop as a rapid pharmacologic effect.\",\"Blockade of sympathetic fibers causes vasodilation and reduced venous return, producing maternal hypotension that can also reduce uteroplacental perfusion.\"],\"e\":[\"Labour epidural analgesia blocks sensory and sympathetic nerve transmission below the level of the block.\",\"Sympathetic vasodilation can rapidly reduce systemic vascular resistance and venous return, making hypotension the key early maternal hemodynamic effect.\",\"A fall in maternal arterial pressure can decrease uteroplacental perfusion, so blood pressure and fetal status are monitored around neuraxial dosing.\"],\"tk\":\"Maternal hypotension is an important early epidural effect caused by sympathetic vasodilation.\",\"pr\":\"An excessively high neuraxial block can cause respiratory compromise and profound hypotension, which requires immediate anesthetic management.\",\"tp\":\"Check baseline blood pressure, establish appropriate intravenous (IV) access, and treat significant post-epidural hypotension promptly while reassessing the fetal heart rate.\",\"cn\":\"Epidural analgesia causes sympathetic blockade and vasodilatation, so maternal hypotension can follow quickly.\",\"sl\":\"Great care must be taken to prevent hypotension caused by regional anesthesia; spinal and epidural anesthesia are discouraged in aortic stenosis because of their vasodilatory effects.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F190\"},{\"id\":\"F191\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Anemia threshold at 28 weeks\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"At 28 weeks of gestation, which hemoglobin concentration is the standard threshold below which anemia is diagnosed according to current World Health Organization (WHO) trimester-specific criteria?\",\"o\":[\"9.0 g/dL\",\"10.0 g/dL\",\"10.5 g/dL\",\"11.0 g/dL\",\"12.0 g/dL\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A hemoglobin of 9.0 g/dL represents established moderate anemia rather than the diagnostic threshold.\",\"A 10.0 g/dL cutoff is lower than current WHO criteria and would miss milder anemia.\",\"The 10.5 g/dL threshold is used for the second trimester because physiologic hemodilution is greatest then.\",\"At 28 weeks, the third trimester has begun; WHO defines anemia below 11.0 g/dL in the first and third trimesters.\",\"A 12.0 g/dL threshold would overdiagnose anemia in normal pregnancy because plasma-volume expansion physiologically lowers hemoglobin concentration.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy causes physiologic hemodilution because plasma volume expands more than red-cell mass.\",\"WHO trimester-specific thresholds define anemia as Hb below 11.0 g/dL in the first and third trimesters and below 10.5 g/dL in the second trimester.\",\"At 28 weeks, the patient is entering the third trimester, so the 11.0-g/dL threshold applies despite maximal dilution occurring near this time.\"],\"tk\":\"Use trimester-specific hemoglobin thresholds in pregnancy: <11.0 g/dL in the first/third trimesters and <10.5 g/dL in the second.\",\"pr\":\"Ferritin is the most useful routine marker of iron stores, but inflammation can elevate ferritin and mask iron deficiency.\",\"tp\":\"At the routine 28-week blood count, interpret Hb together with mean corpuscular volume (MCV) and ferritin when iron deficiency is suspected, rather than treating every low value as iron deficiency automatically.\",\"cn\":\"World Health Organization (WHO) defines anaemia in pregnancy as haemoglobin (Hb) below 11.0 g/dL; UK uses 10.5 g/dL after the first trimester.\",\"sl\":\"True anaemia: haemoglobin (Hb) below 110 g/L in the 1st trimester and below 105 g/L in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters (British Society for Haematology [BSH]); the World Health Organization (WHO) uses Hb below 110 g/L throughout pregnancy.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F191\"},{\"id\":\"F192\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Electrolyte effects of persistent vomiting\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant woman has persistent severe vomiting with volume depletion. Which acid-base and electrolyte pattern is most typical?\",\"o\":[\"Metabolic acidosis with hyperkalemia and hypernatremia\",\"Respiratory alkalosis with hyperkalemia and hyponatremia\",\"Metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia and hyperchloremia\",\"Metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia and hyponatremia\",\"Respiratory acidosis with hypokalemia and hypernatremia\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Loss of gastric hydrochloric acid produces alkalosis, not a primary metabolic acidosis; potassium usually falls rather than rises.\",\"Pregnancy itself causes mild respiratory alkalosis, but severe vomiting primarily creates a metabolic alkalosis with extracellular-volume depletion.\",\"A hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis follows bicarbonate loss, such as diarrhea, rather than repeated loss of gastric acid.\",\"Persistent vomiting causes hydrogen and chloride loss, volume contraction, renal potassium wasting, and often hyponatremia, yielding hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia.\",\"Respiratory acidosis reflects hypoventilation and is not the expected acid-base consequence of hyperemesis.\"],\"e\":[\"Repeated vomiting causes loss of gastric hydrochloric acid, producing hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis.\",\"Volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, increasing renal potassium and hydrogen loss, while reduced intake and free-water handling can contribute to hyponatremia.\",\"Severe hyperemesis also depletes thiamine, so prolonged vomiting requires vitamin replacement before carbohydrate loading to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy.\"],\"tk\":\"Persistent vomiting classically causes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia and volume depletion.\",\"pr\":\"Give thiamine before dextrose in patients with prolonged severe vomiting or malnutrition to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy.\",\"tp\":\"Assess orthostatic symptoms, urine ketones, electrolytes, and renal function, then replace volume and potassium while controlling nausea.\",\"cn\":\"Persistent vomiting in hyperemesis gravidarum causes metabolic alkalosis with hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia.\",\"sl\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is characterised by intractable vomiting, dehydration, alkalosis, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia and weight loss usually more than 5% of pre-pregnant body weight, with plasma volume depletion and raised haematocrit.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F020\"},{\"id\":\"F193\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Oxytocin for third-stage PPH prevention\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which uterotonic regimen is recommended for routine prevention of postpartum hemorrhage during the third stage of labour?\",\"o\":[\"Oxytocin 5 IU as a rapid intravenous (IV) bolus\",\"Ergometrine 500 micrograms IV for every woman\",\"Oxytocin 40 IU as a rapid IV bolus\",\"Misoprostol 200 micrograms vaginally\",\"Oxytocin 10 IU intramuscular (IM) or slow IV\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A rapid IV bolus of oxytocin can cause marked hypotension and is not the standard preventive regimen described by World Health Organization (WHO).\",\"Ergometrine can cause hypertension and is not preferred for routine use in women in whom hypertensive disease has not been excluded.\",\"A 40-IU rapid bolus is excessive for routine prophylaxis and carries avoidable cardiovascular adverse effects.\",\"When injectable uterotonics are unavailable, oral misoprostol 400–600 micrograms is an alternative; 200 micrograms vaginally is not the standard prophylactic dose.\",\"WHO recommends oxytocin 10 IU IM or IV for prevention of postpartum haemorrhage (PPH), with slow IV administration preferred when IV access is already present after vaginal birth.\"],\"e\":[\"An effective uterotonic should be given during the third stage of labour to reduce postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"WHO recommends oxytocin 10 IU IM or IV for all births; when IV access is already present after vaginal birth, slow IV administration is preferred.\",\"Oxytocin activates myometrial receptors and raises intracellular calcium, producing sustained uterine contraction that compresses spiral vessels after placental separation.\"],\"tk\":\"Oxytocin 10 IU IM or slow IV is the preferred uterotonic for routine prevention of postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"pr\":\"Ergometrine-containing regimens should be avoided when hypertension or preeclampsia is present because they can markedly elevate blood pressure.\",\"tp\":\"After birth, verify uterotonic administration and immediately assess uterine tone and quantified blood loss rather than assuming prophylaxis eliminates PPH risk.\",\"cn\":\"Oxytocin 10 IU intramuscularly (IM) is the routine uterotonic for active management of the third stage after vaginal birth.\",\"sl\":\"Uterotonics: 10 IU oxytocin intramuscularly (IM) after vaginal delivery, 5 IU oxytocin intravenously (IV) after cesarean section; carbetocin 100 micrograms IV for cesarean section.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[6]],[\"L15_0\",[40]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F193\"},{\"id\":\"F194\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pregnancy venous thromboembolism risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor is not by itself a recognized Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) antenatal risk factor for venous thromboembolism?\",\"o\":[\"Previous venous thromboembolism\",\"Prolonged immobility\",\"Parity of two previous births\",\"Maternal age over 35 years\",\"Obesity with body mass index (BMI) at least 30 kg/m²\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A previous VTE is one of the strongest predictors of thrombosis in pregnancy and may warrant antenatal and postpartum low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) depending on circumstances.\",\"Immobility, including prolonged hospitalization or reduced mobility, increases venous stasis and is a recognized VTE risk factor.\",\"Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) lists parity of 3 or more as a risk factor; having exactly two previous births does not meet that specified parity threshold by itself.\",\"Maternal age over 35 years is included in RCOG obstetric VTE risk assessment.\",\"BMI at least 30 kg/m² is a recognized pre-existing VTE risk factor in pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy is a hypercoagulable state, and obstetric VTE risk rises further with previous thrombosis, thrombophilia, obesity, age, immobility, and obstetric complications.\",\"RCOG specifically includes parity of at least 3, not parity of two, among its scored pre-existing factors.\",\"Venous stasis, increased procoagulant factors, and reduced fibrinolysis together fulfill Virchow’s triad during pregnancy and the puerperium.\"],\"tk\":\"RCOG VTE assessment uses defined thresholds, including parity ≥3, age >35 years, and BMI ≥30 kg/m².\",\"pr\":\"The daily risk of VTE is especially high in the postpartum period, so risk assessment must be repeated after delivery.\",\"tp\":\"Recalculate VTE risk after new events such as cesarean birth, hemorrhage, infection, or prolonged admission, because prophylaxis needs can change during the same pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"The parity threshold for venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk in pregnancy is parity 3 or more, not 2.\",\"sl\":\"Pre-existing venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk factors: previous VTE, age over 35, body mass index (BMI) 30 kg/m2 or more, parity 3 or more, smoking, medical conditions (sickle cell, heart disease, anaemia, systemic lupus erythematosus [SLE]), thrombophilia; pregnancy-related include immobility. Previous VTE is the strongest risk factor.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[5,6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F194\"},{\"id\":\"F195\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"HRT-associated venous thromboembolism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman starts oral menopausal hormone therapy and continues it for 5 years. During which period is the relative risk of venous thromboembolism greatest?\",\"o\":[\"During the first year after starting therapy\",\"During the second year only\",\"During the third year only\",\"During the fourth year only\",\"The risk rises progressively and is highest in the fifth year\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The excess VTE risk with oral HRT is greatest soon after treatment is initiated, particularly during the first year.\",\"Risk persists with oral therapy but is not confined to the second year; the early initiation period carries the greatest relative increase.\",\"There is no special third-year peak that exceeds the early treatment-associated risk.\",\"A unique fourth-year maximum is not expected; duration does not produce a simple year-by-year rise.\",\"Oral HRT does not show a steadily increasing VTE hazard culminating in year 5; transdermal estrogen has substantially less thrombotic effect.\"],\"e\":[\"Oral menopausal hormone therapy increases venous thromboembolism risk, with the greatest relative excess occurring early after initiation.\",\"The first year is therefore the highest-risk period among the choices, particularly in women with additional factors such as obesity, older age, or previous thrombosis.\",\"Oral estrogen undergoes first-pass hepatic metabolism and increases procoagulant protein synthesis, whereas standard-dose transdermal estrogen has little or no measurable increase in VTE risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Oral HRT increases VTE risk most prominently soon after initiation, especially during the first year.\",\"pr\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends considering transdermal rather than oral HRT in patients with increased baseline VTE risk, including body mass index (BMI) over 30 kg/m².\",\"tp\":\"Before prescribing systemic HRT, document personal and family VTE history and choose route and progestogen with the patient’s thrombotic risk in mind.\",\"cn\":\"The venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk of oral hormone replacement therapy (HRT) is highest in the first year of use.\",\"sl\":\"Increased risk of venous thromboembolism (VTE): highest in the 1st year, combined estrogen-progestogen greater than estrogen only, no increase with non-oral estrogen. Oral hormone replacement therapy (HRT) adds 1.5 events per 1000 women per year, highest in year 1.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_0\",[31]],[\"L34_1\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F195\"},{\"id\":\"F196\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Cervical cancer risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following are risk factors for Cervical CA except:\",\"o\":[\"Nulliparity\",\"Smoking\",\"Persistent high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV) infection\",\"History of sexually transmitted infections associated with high-risk sexual exposure\",\"Multiple sexual partners\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Nulliparity is a classic risk factor for endometrial carcinoma, not a recognized independent risk factor for cervical cancer.\",\"Smoking increases cervical squamous carcinoma risk and acts synergistically with persistent high-risk HPV infection.\",\"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV, especially types 16 and 18, is the central causal factor in most cervical cancers.\",\"A history of sexually transmitted infections/high-risk sexual exposure often marks behaviors that increase HPV acquisition and persistence.\",\"Having multiple sexual partners increases the probability of exposure to oncogenic HPV.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent infection with oncogenic HPV is necessary for the great majority of cervical cancers, with smoking and immunosuppression promoting persistence and progression.\",\"Nulliparity does not fit the cervical-risk profile; it is more closely linked to endometrial cancer through longer lifetime unopposed-estrogen exposure.\",\"HPV E6 and E7 oncoproteins disable p53 and Rb tumor suppressor pathways, allowing dysplastic cervical cells to escape normal cell-cycle control.\"],\"tk\":\"Persistent high-risk HPV infection is the dominant causal factor for cervical cancer; nulliparity is not a typical cervical cancer risk factor.\",\"pr\":\"HPV vaccination prevents most cancers caused by vaccine-covered oncogenic types but does not replace cervical screening in eligible populations.\",\"tp\":\"Record vaccination status, prior abnormal screening, smoking, and immunosuppression; manage screening according to the current national cervical-screening program rather than symptoms alone.\",\"cn\":\"Multiparity, not nulliparity, is associated with cervical cancer; human papillomavirus (HPV), smoking and sexual behaviour are risk factors.\",\"sl\":\"Multiparous, low socioeconomic class, poor hygiene. Risk factors: human papillomavirus (HPV) as the primary factor, sexual behaviour, smoking, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), chlamydia, multiple pregnancies.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[8,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F196\"},{\"id\":\"F197\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Shoulder dystocia after failed McRoberts\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman delivering a baby that had shoulder dystocia, tried (the McRoberts maneuver) & failed, what is the next step?\",\"o\":[\"Fundal pressure\",\"Suprapubic pressure\",\"Routine downward traction with increasing force\",\"Immediate symphysiotomy\",\"Vacuum application to the delivered fetal head\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Fundal pressure can further impact the anterior shoulder against the symphysis and is not recommended for shoulder dystocia.\",\"Suprapubic pressure is applied with McRoberts to adduct/rotate the anterior fetal shoulder into an oblique diameter and improve release.\",\"Increasing traction on the fetal head raises the risk of brachial plexus injury and is not an appropriate response to an impacted shoulder.\",\"Symphysiotomy is a rarely used rescue procedure in resource-limited settings, not the next maneuver after McRoberts.\",\"The fetal head is already delivered in shoulder dystocia; vacuum extraction has no role in releasing the impacted shoulders.\"],\"e\":[\"Shoulder dystocia is an obstetric emergency in which the shoulders fail to deliver with routine axial traction.\",\"After McRoberts, suprapubic pressure is an appropriate immediate maneuver and can be used concurrently to reduce the fetal bisacromial diameter and rotate the anterior shoulder.\",\"If simple measures fail, clinicians should move promptly to internal rotational maneuvers or delivery of the posterior arm rather than applying fundal pressure or excessive traction.\"],\"tk\":\"After unsuccessful McRoberts maneuver, add suprapubic pressure and escalate promptly to internal maneuvers if needed.\",\"pr\":\"Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) emphasizes early recourse to posterior-arm delivery or internal rotation when McRoberts plus suprapubic pressure does not resolve the dystocia.\",\"tp\":\"Call for help, note the time of head delivery, stop maternal pushing briefly while maneuvers are organized, and avoid fundal pressure.\",\"cn\":\"If McRoberts manoeuvre fails in shoulder dystocia, apply suprapubic pressure next; never fundal pressure.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F197\"},{\"id\":\"F198\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Normal menstrual cycle duration\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"About the normal menstrual cycle in reproductive-age women, all of the following are correct EXCEPT:\",\"o\":[\"Cycle regularity generally improves after the early post-menarcheal years\",\"Bleeding duration may shorten modestly with reproductive aging\",\"Cycle length can vary with age because the follicular phase is less constant than the luteal phase\",\"Normal menstrual bleeding lasts only 2–5 days\",\"Irregular cycles are common during the first years after menarche\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"As hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian maturation progresses after menarche, cycles generally become more regular and ovulatory.\",\"The duration and amount of bleeding can change with age, and modest shortening of menses may occur across reproductive life.\",\"Most cycle-length variability reflects the follicular phase, while the luteal phase is relatively more constant.\",\"Normal menstrual bleeding is not restricted to 2–5 days; contemporary reference ranges generally include bleeding lasting up to about 7–8 days.\",\"Early after menarche, anovulatory and irregular cycles are common while the reproductive axis matures.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal menstrual characteristics vary across reproductive life, especially during adolescence and the menopausal transition.\",\"A strict statement that normal bleeding lasts only 2–5 days is too narrow; modern definitions allow a broader upper limit, while cycle regularity and length also vary with age.\",\"Variation in cycle length is driven mainly by the follicular phase, whereas corpus-luteum lifespan makes the luteal phase comparatively stable.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal menstrual bleeding has a broader duration range than 2–5 days, and cycle variability is mainly follicular-phase variability.\",\"pr\":\"Cycle irregularity is physiologic for a time after menarche, but very prolonged intervals or persistent abnormalities still warrant evaluation.\",\"tp\":\"Ask patients to quantify cycle interval, bleeding duration, and impact on function using a menstrual history or tracking app rather than relying on labels such as “irregular.”\",\"cn\":\"Normal menstrual bleeding lasts up to 8 days, so a 2-5 day limit is wrong; duration shortens with age.\",\"sl\":\"Normal duration is 8 days or less (2-8 days, average 4-6 days). Menstrual cycle is initially irregular and tends to shorten with age, and as age increases the duration of menstruation decreases.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[3]],[\"L33_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F198\"},{\"id\":\"F199\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Chronic hypertension preconception medication counseling\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"False about chronic HTN in pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"An ACE inhibitor must be stopped exactly 1 month before conception in every patient\",\"Women planning pregnancy should discuss switching from ACE inhibitors or ARBs to a pregnancy-compatible agent\",\"If pregnancy occurs while taking an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB), it should be stopped promptly and an alternative offered\",\"Labetalol is commonly considered for chronic hypertension during pregnancy\",\"Women with chronic hypertension should receive individualized antenatal surveillance\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Current National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance does not mandate an exact 1-month washout for every ACE inhibitor; women planning pregnancy should avoid these drugs when possible, and they should be stopped promptly if pregnancy occurs.\",\"ACE inhibitors and ARBs are associated with fetal toxicity and should be reviewed before conception with transition to a safer antihypertensive when appropriate.\",\"NICE recommends stopping an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB) preferably within 2 working days after notification of pregnancy and offering an alternative.\",\"Labetalol is commonly considered first-line for chronic hypertension in pregnancy, with nifedipine or methyldopa used when appropriate.\",\"Chronic hypertension increases risks such as preeclampsia and fetal growth problems, so antenatal follow-up is individualized beyond routine low-risk care.\"],\"e\":[\"ACE inhibitors and ARBs should generally be avoided in pregnancy because of fetal renal and other toxicity.\",\"A rule requiring discontinuation exactly 1 month before conception is not a current universal standard; planned pregnancy should trigger medication review, and an ACE inhibitor/ARB found in pregnancy should be stopped promptly.\",\"Pregnancy-compatible agents such as labetalol, nifedipine, or methyldopa control maternal pressure without the fetotoxic renin-angiotensin blockade of ACE inhibitors and ARBs.\"],\"tk\":\"Women with chronic hypertension should review ACE inhibitors/ARBs before conception; if pregnancy occurs, these drugs should be stopped promptly rather than relying on an arbitrary 1-month rule.\",\"pr\":\"NICE recommends a treatment target around 135/85 mmHg once antihypertensive therapy is used for chronic hypertension in pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"At preconception review, reconcile every antihypertensive and document a specific replacement plan so treatment is not simply stopped without blood-pressure control.\",\"cn\":\"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) are switched before conception or stopped promptly once pregnant; no fixed 1-month rule.\",\"sl\":\"Pre-pregnancy counselling: optimise blood pressure, screen for end-organ damage, and stop teratogenic agents (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors [ACEIs], angiotensin receptor blockers [ARBs], statins, thiazides) and switch to labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F199\"},{\"id\":\"F200\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Risk\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Post-cesarean VTE risk assessment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement best reflects current venous thromboembolism risk assessment after cesarean birth?\",\"o\":[\"Every woman should receive therapeutic-dose low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) for 6 weeks after cesarean birth\",\"The Caprini score is the universally mandated and validated obstetric postpartum risk tool\",\"Postpartum risk should be reassessed using an obstetric-specific risk-factor framework, incorporating cesarean birth and additional maternal factors\",\"Mechanical prophylaxis is contraindicated after cesarean birth\",\"A previous venous thromboembolism (VTE) has no effect on postpartum prophylaxis decisions\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Universal therapeutic-dose low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) would expose many postpartum patients to unnecessary bleeding risk; prophylaxis requires an individual thrombosis-versus-bleeding assessment.\",\"The Caprini score is widely used in general surgical populations, whereas current Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) obstetric practice uses an obstetric-specific risk-factor framework for pregnancy and the puerperium.\",\"RCOG recommends repeat postpartum VTE risk assessment using obstetric risk factors such as cesarean birth, previous VTE, obesity, age, immobility, hemorrhage, and infection.\",\"Mechanical prophylaxis can be used around cesarean delivery, particularly when pharmacologic prophylaxis is delayed or contraindicated.\",\"A previous VTE is among the strongest predictors of recurrence and materially changes antepartum and postpartum thromboprophylaxis planning.\"],\"e\":[\"Cesarean birth increases postpartum venous thromboembolism risk, but prophylaxis is not determined by a single universal general-surgery score.\",\"Current obstetric guidance uses an obstetric-specific reassessment of cumulative risk factors after delivery, including the mode of birth and new complications such as hemorrhage or infection.\",\"Pregnancy and the puerperium increase coagulation and venous stasis; cesarean surgery adds vascular injury and immobility, strengthening all elements of Virchow’s triad.\"],\"tk\":\"Post-cesarean VTE prevention should be based on repeat obstetric risk assessment rather than assuming the Caprini score is universally required.\",\"pr\":\"Postpartum VTE risk is highest in the first weeks after birth, so events during labour or surgery can change a patient’s prophylaxis category.\",\"tp\":\"Complete and document a postpartum VTE risk assessment before discharge, including new factors that were absent at booking.\",\"cn\":\"Venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk after caesarean section is reassessed with an obstetric-specific risk-factor framework including caesarean and other maternal factors.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors for venous thromboembolism (VTE) in pregnancy are grouped as pre-existing, pregnancy-related, and labour and delivery (caesarean section, preterm birth before 36 weeks, postpartum haemorrhage [PPH] or transfusion, postpartum infection, stillbirth, prolonged labour).\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F200\"},{\"id\":\"F201\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Uterine atony risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All cause uterine atony except:\",\"o\":[\"Multiple gestation\",\"Polyhydramnios\",\"Breech presentation by itself\",\"Prolonged labour\",\"Chorioamnionitis\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Multiple gestation causes uterine overdistension, which impairs effective postpartum myometrial contraction.\",\"Polyhydramnios produces uterine overdistension, a classic mechanical pathway to postpartum atony.\",\"A breech presentation alone does not directly cause uterine overdistension or myometrial exhaustion and is not a classic independent atony risk factor.\",\"Prolonged labour can produce myometrial fatigue, reducing the uterus's ability to contract effectively after placental separation.\",\"Chorioamnionitis promotes inflammatory myometrial dysfunction and increases the risk of atonic postpartum hemorrhage.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine atony causes most primary postpartum hemorrhage and occurs when the myometrium fails to contract effectively after placental separation.\",\"Overdistension from multiple gestation or polyhydramnios, prolonged labour, and infection all reduce effective postpartum contraction; breech presentation alone does not.\",\"Myometrial contraction normally compresses uterine vessels as physiologic ligatures, so failure of contraction permits continued high-flow bleeding from the placental bed.\"],\"tk\":\"Uterine overdistension, prolonged labour, and chorioamnionitis predispose to atony; breech presentation alone is not a classic cause.\",\"pr\":\"Remember the PPH causes as the 4 Ts: Tone, Trauma, Tissue, and Thrombin, with tone/atony the commonest category.\",\"tp\":\"When heavy bleeding begins after birth, palpate uterine tone immediately while simultaneously initiating resuscitation and quantified blood-loss assessment.\",\"cn\":\"Uterine atony is caused by overdistension (multiples, polyhydramnios), prolonged labour and chorioamnionitis, not breech presentation alone.\",\"sl\":\"Multifetal gestation: uterine overdistension leads to preterm labour, atony and postpartum haemorrhage (PPH).\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F201\"},{\"id\":\"F202\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"External cephalic version outcomes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"When external cephalic version (ECV) is attempted for a term breech presentation, which outcome is most common?\",\"o\":[\"Emergency cesarean delivery for persistent fetal compromise\",\"Placental abruption\",\"Failure to achieve a cephalic presentation\",\"Clinically significant fetomaternal hemorrhage\",\"Uterine rupture\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Transient fetal heart-rate abnormalities can occur after ECV, but persistent compromise requiring emergency cesarean is uncommon.\",\"Placental abruption is a recognized but uncommon complication of ECV and warrants urgent assessment if pain or bleeding develops.\",\"ECV succeeds in only about half of attempts, so failed version is a common outcome even when the procedure is performed appropriately.\",\"Small-volume fetomaternal hemorrhage may occur, which is why RhD-negative unsensitized patients may need anti-D prophylaxis.\",\"Uterine rupture is exceedingly rare during ECV and is not the expected outcome of an uncomplicated attempt.\"],\"e\":[\"External cephalic version is offered near term to reduce persistent breech presentation and cesarean birth; overall success is roughly 50%.\",\"Among attempted versions, failure to turn the fetus is substantially more frequent than serious complications.\",\"Version depends on fetal mobility, parity, placental location, liquor volume, and engagement; after the attempt, fetal heart-rate monitoring is used to detect the uncommon complication of sustained compromise.\"],\"tk\":\"Failed ECV is common because overall success is only about one half, whereas serious maternal or fetal complications are uncommon.\",\"pr\":\"ECV is generally more successful in multiparous patients and when the breech is not deeply engaged.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm fetal wellbeing before and after ECV, and ensure immediate access to cesarean delivery if sustained fetal compromise occurs.\",\"cn\":\"Failed version is the commonest adverse outcome of external cephalic version (ECV); serious complications are uncommon.\",\"sl\":\"The most common risk of external cephalic version (ECV) is failed version. Overall success is 60% in multiparous and 40% in nulliparous women.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[22,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F181\"},{\"id\":\"F203\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Cephalopelvic disproportion\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding argues most strongly against clinically significant cephalopelvic disproportion during labor?\",\"o\":[\"Engaged, well-applied head with progressive descent\",\"Persistent high, poorly applied fetal head despite adequate contractions\",\"Increasing caput and molding with arrest of descent\",\"No descent despite adequate uterine activity\",\"Arrest of cervical dilation with an adequately contracted uterus\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An engaged, well-applied head that continues to descend demonstrates effective passage through the pelvis and argues against important CPD.\",\"A persistently high, poorly applied head despite adequate contractions raises concern for malposition or fetopelvic disproportion.\",\"Progressive caput and molding with arrested descent suggests mechanical difficulty as the fetal head is forced against a limiting pelvis.\",\"Failure of descent despite adequate contractions is a classic clue to obstruction after malposition and inadequate power are considered.\",\"True active-phase arrest with adequate uterine activity can reflect fetopelvic disproportion and requires reassessment of position and pelvis.\"],\"e\":[\"Cephalopelvic disproportion is a mismatch between the fetal presenting part and maternal pelvis that produces failure of progress despite adequate uterine power.\",\"Progressive descent with an engaged, well-flexed and well-applied head is evidence that the presenting part can traverse the pelvis.\",\"Diagnosis is clinical and dynamic rather than based on a single pelvic measurement; persistent arrest requires assessment of power, passenger, and passage before operative delivery.\"],\"tk\":\"Progressive descent of an engaged, well-applied head argues against clinically significant CPD.\",\"pr\":\"Marked molding, caput, and a high unengaged head are particularly concerning when contractions are already adequate.\",\"tp\":\"Before labeling labor as obstructed, document contraction adequacy and fetal position, station, and descent.\",\"cn\":\"Progressive descent of an engaged, well-applied head argues against cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD).\",\"sl\":\"Cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD) is anatomical disproportion between fetal head and maternal pelvis and cannot be diagnosed definitively except after failed trial of labour; moulding and caput reflect a tight birth canal.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[9,10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F203\"},{\"id\":\"F204\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Magnesium sulfate toxicity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following statements about magnesium sulfate in obstetrics are true EXCEPT:\",\"o\":[\"Loss of deep-tendon reflexes is an early sign of toxicity\",\"Calcium gluconate is used as an antidote for clinically significant toxicity\",\"Oliguria increases the risk of magnesium accumulation\",\"Magnesium toxicity typically causes an increased respiratory rate\",\"Magnesium sulfate is used for seizure prevention and treatment in severe preeclampsia/eclampsia\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Loss of deep-tendon reflexes is an early clinical sign of rising magnesium levels and should prompt reassessment of the infusion.\",\"Intravenous calcium gluconate antagonizes magnesium at the neuromuscular junction and is the standard antidote for serious toxicity.\",\"Magnesium is cleared renally, so oliguria predisposes to accumulation and requires closer monitoring or dose adjustment.\",\"Toxicity produces respiratory depression, not tachypnea; severe accumulation can progress to respiratory arrest and cardiac conduction abnormalities.\",\"Magnesium sulfate is first-line for eclamptic seizures and is used for seizure prophylaxis when severe preeclampsia warrants it.\"],\"e\":[\"Magnesium sulfate is the preferred anticonvulsant for eclampsia and for seizure prophylaxis in selected patients with severe preeclampsia.\",\"The false statement is the claim of an increased respiratory rate; toxicity instead suppresses neuromuscular transmission and ventilation.\",\"Because elimination is renal, bedside monitoring of respiratory rate, reflexes, and urine output helps detect toxicity before life-threatening respiratory or cardiac effects.\"],\"tk\":\"Magnesium toxicity causes hyporeflexia and respiratory depression, with risk increased by impaired renal clearance.\",\"pr\":\"Loss of patellar reflexes generally precedes severe respiratory compromise and is a useful bedside warning sign.\",\"tp\":\"Stop the infusion for suspected toxicity, assess airway and ventilation, and give IV calcium gluconate when clinically significant toxicity is present.\",\"cn\":\"Magnesium toxicity causes loss of reflexes and respiratory depression (not increased respiratory rate); calcium gluconate is the antidote.\",\"sl\":\"Magnesium toxicity: loss of patellar reflexes (8-10 mEq/L), respiratory depression or arrest (12 mEq/L), mental status changes then electrocardiogram (ECG) changes. If toxicity develops, stop magnesium sulphate and give 1 g calcium gluconate intravenously (IV) over 3 minutes.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F204\"},{\"id\":\"F205\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Insulin requirements in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about insulin requirements during the second half of pregnancy is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Placental hormones progressively increase insulin resistance\",\"Good glycemic control reduces several maternal and fetal complications\",\"Poor glycemic control increases the risk of fetal macrosomia\",\"Insulin needs often rise as pregnancy advances\",\"Insulin requirements usually decrease progressively through late pregnancy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Placental hormones such as human placental lactogen contribute to increasing insulin resistance in the second half of pregnancy.\",\"Tighter glycemic control lowers risks including macrosomia and neonatal hypoglycemia while avoiding maternal hypoglycemia.\",\"Maternal hyperglycemia drives fetal hyperinsulinemia and increases macrosomia and shoulder-dystocia risk.\",\"Many patients with pre-existing diabetes need higher insulin doses in later gestation because insulin resistance intensifies.\",\"Insulin requirements generally rise rather than fall as placental insulin resistance increases; a sudden late fall can merit clinical review.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy becomes increasingly insulin resistant after mid-gestation as placental hormones antagonize insulin action.\",\"A progressive late-pregnancy decrease in insulin requirement is therefore not the expected physiologic pattern.\",\"After placental delivery, the diabetogenic hormonal drive falls abruptly, so postpartum insulin needs decrease rapidly and doses must be reassessed.\"],\"tk\":\"Insulin requirements usually rise in the second half of pregnancy and fall sharply after placental delivery.\",\"pr\":\"An unexpected major fall in insulin needs before delivery can accompany placental dysfunction and should be interpreted in clinical context.\",\"tp\":\"Review glucose logs frequently in late gestation and proactively reduce insulin after birth to prevent postpartum hypoglycemia.\",\"cn\":\"Insulin requirements rise as pregnancy advances because placental hormones progressively increase insulin resistance.\",\"sl\":\"Pre-existing diabetes in pregnancy causes increased insulin requirement. Placental hormones rise through the second and third trimesters and confer increasing insulin resistance; by the third trimester 24-hour mean insulin levels are 50% higher than non-pregnant.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[4,20,21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F064\"},{\"id\":\"F206\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia during pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Immune thrombocytopenia\",\"Haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP) syndrome\",\"Gestational thrombocytopenia\",\"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura\",\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Immune thrombocytopenia can occur in pregnancy and may produce more marked thrombocytopenia, including before the third trimester.\",\"HELLP syndrome combines hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets in the setting of hypertensive disease and systemic illness.\",\"Gestational thrombocytopenia is the commonest cause; it is typically mild, asymptomatic, late in pregnancy, and resolves postpartum.\",\"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) causes thrombocytopenia with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and organ ischemia and requires urgent plasma-exchange therapy.\",\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is secondary to major obstetric pathology such as abruption, sepsis, or massive hemorrhage and causes consumptive coagulopathy.\"],\"e\":[\"Gestational thrombocytopenia accounts for most maternal thrombocytopenia and is usually mild and asymptomatic.\",\"Among causes encountered in pregnancy, gestational thrombocytopenia is far more frequent than immune or thrombotic microangiopathic disorders.\",\"A very low platelet count, early onset, bleeding, hemolysis, hypertension, renal dysfunction, or liver abnormalities should trigger evaluation for pathologic causes rather than assuming a benign gestational process.\"],\"tk\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia is the commonest cause of low platelets in pregnancy and usually resolves after delivery.\",\"pr\":\"Platelet counts below about 70–80 ×10^9/L are atypical for uncomplicated gestational thrombocytopenia and should prompt further assessment.\",\"tp\":\"Trend the platelet count and review blood pressure, hemolysis markers, and liver tests when the pattern is not clearly benign.\",\"cn\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia is the commonest cause of thrombocytopenia in pregnancy; it is mild, late, asymptomatic and resolves after birth.\",\"sl\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia occurs in about 5-10% of pregnancies: mild (usually above 70-80 x10^9/L), late, asymptomatic, resolves after birth. Below 100 with raised blood pressure or liver enzymes, think haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets (HELLP).\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F018\"},{\"id\":\"F207\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Renin-angiotensin blockade in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication is contraindicated during pregnancy because of fetal renal toxicity?\",\"o\":[\"Azathioprine\",\"Labetalol\",\"Nifedipine\",\"Hydroxychloroquine\",\"Valsartan\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Azathioprine can be used when clinically necessary in pregnancy because fetal exposure to active metabolites is limited relative to teratogenic immunosuppressants.\",\"Labetalol is widely used for hypertension in pregnancy when no patient-specific contraindication is present.\",\"Nifedipine is an accepted antihypertensive in pregnancy and is also used in selected settings as a tocolytic.\",\"Hydroxychloroquine is generally continued for indicated autoimmune disease because maintaining maternal disease control improves outcomes.\",\"Valsartan is an angiotensin-receptor blocker; fetal renin-angiotensin blockade can cause renal failure, oligohydramnios, skull hypoplasia, and neonatal complications.\"],\"e\":[\"ACE inhibitors and angiotensin-receptor blockers are avoided in pregnancy because fetal renin-angiotensin blockade can injure the developing kidneys.\",\"Valsartan is an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB), making it inappropriate once pregnancy is recognized when safer antihypertensive alternatives are available.\",\"Reduced fetal renal perfusion can lead to oligohydramnios and neonatal renal failure, particularly with exposure later in gestation.\"],\"tk\":\"ACE inhibitors and ARBs such as valsartan are contraindicated in pregnancy because of fetotoxic renal effects.\",\"pr\":\"The major fetal toxicity is not a classically isolated malformation but a fetopathy involving renal dysfunction, oligohydramnios, and impaired skull ossification.\",\"tp\":\"Review antihypertensive therapy preconception and switch renin-angiotensin blockers to a pregnancy-compatible agent before or promptly after conception.\",\"cn\":\"Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) such as valsartan and angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy due to fetal renal toxicity.\",\"sl\":\"Stop teratogenic antihypertensives (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors [ACEIs], angiotensin receptor blockers [ARBs], thiazide diuretics). ACE inhibitors are contraindicated: oligohydramnios, intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR), renal failure, abnormal bone ossification.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[10,11]],[\"L11_0\",[37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F207\"},{\"id\":\"F208\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Gravidity and parity notation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has had seven pregnancies reaching viability, including one twin gestation, and one miscarriage. How should her obstetric history be recorded in G/P+ notation?\",\"o\":[\"G7 P7+1\",\"G8 P8+0\",\"G9 P8+1\",\"G8 P7+1\",\"G8 P6+2\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A history with one miscarriage includes one pregnancy that did not reach viability, so gravidity must exceed the number of viable pregnancies.\",\"Counting all eight pregnancies as viable ignores the pregnancy loss represented after the plus sign.\",\"A twin gestation does not count as two separate gravidities, so G9 overcounts the number of pregnancies.\",\"Seven pregnancies reached viability and one ended in miscarriage, giving G8 P7+1; twins count as one pregnancy event for parity.\",\"The history specifies seven viable pregnancies, so P6 would undercount parity even though one pregnancy produced twins.\"],\"e\":[\"Gravidity counts the total number of pregnancies, whereas parity counts pregnancies reaching the locally defined threshold of viability rather than the number of infants.\",\"With seven viable pregnancies plus one miscarriage, the total is eight pregnancies and the parity component is P7+1.\",\"A twin pregnancy counts as one pregnancy for gravidity and one viable pregnancy for parity, even though two infants are born.\"],\"tk\":\"G/P notation counts pregnancies rather than babies, so a twin gestation contributes one pregnancy event.\",\"pr\":\"When local notation uses P+losses, the figure after the plus sign records pregnancies ending before the parity threshold.\",\"tp\":\"Clarify whether a historical shorthand counts pregnancies or living children before assigning G/P, because twins are a common source of charting errors.\",\"cn\":\"A twin pregnancy counts once for both gravidity and parity; miscarriages are recorded after the plus sign.\",\"sl\":\"Gravidity is the total number of pregnancies irrespective of outcome; parity is the total number of previous pregnancies carried to viability (24 weeks in Jordan); miscarriage is pregnancy loss before viability (+), e.g. G3P1+1.\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F208\"},{\"id\":\"F209\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Lower urinary tract symptom classification\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is a voiding symptom rather than a storage symptom of lower urinary tract dysfunction?\",\"o\":[\"Urgency\",\"Hesitancy\",\"Nocturia\",\"Urgency urinary incontinence\",\"Increased daytime frequency\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Urgency is a storage symptom characterized by a sudden compelling desire to void that is difficult to defer.\",\"Hesitancy is a voiding symptom reflecting delayed initiation of micturition after the patient is ready to urinate.\",\"Nocturia is a storage-phase complaint in which the patient wakes from sleep to void.\",\"Urgency incontinence is leakage associated with urgency and belongs to the storage-symptom group.\",\"Increased daytime frequency is a storage symptom and is commonly associated with overactive bladder syndromes.\"],\"e\":[\"Lower urinary tract symptoms are divided into storage, voiding, and post-micturition symptoms.\",\"Hesitancy occurs during initiation of urination and therefore belongs to the voiding-symptom category rather than the storage group.\",\"This classification helps target evaluation: urgency/frequency suggest altered bladder storage, whereas hesitancy and poor stream raise concern for outlet or emptying dysfunction.\"],\"tk\":\"Hesitancy is a voiding symptom, whereas urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urgency incontinence are storage symptoms.\",\"pr\":\"Intermittency, straining, and a slow stream are additional voiding symptoms that often travel with hesitancy.\",\"tp\":\"Ask patients to separate problems of bladder filling from problems starting or maintaining flow; this often narrows the differential before urodynamics.\",\"cn\":\"Hesitancy is a voiding symptom; urgency, nocturia, frequency and urge incontinence are storage symptoms.\",\"sl\":\"Voiding symptoms: slow stream, intermittency, hesitancy (difficulty initiating micturition with delay in onset of voiding), straining to void. Storage symptoms: incontinence, urgency, increased daytime frequency, nocturia, nocturnal enuresis.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[4,7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F003\"},{\"id\":\"F210\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Mid-luteal progesterone timing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a regular 26-day menstrual cycle, on which day is serum progesterone expected to be highest?\",\"o\":[\"Day 7\",\"Day 3\",\"Day 26\",\"Day 19\",\"Day 12\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Day 7 lies in the follicular phase of a 26-day cycle, before expected ovulation and before substantial luteal progesterone production.\",\"Day 3 is in the early follicular phase, when progesterone is physiologically low.\",\"Day 26 is near menstruation, when corpus-luteum regression has already caused progesterone concentrations to fall.\",\"Ovulation is expected around day 12, and progesterone peaks about 7 days later, making day 19 the best estimate.\",\"Day 12 is near ovulation in a 26-day cycle; progesterone rises afterward and reaches its maximum later in the luteal phase.\"],\"e\":[\"The luteal phase is relatively constant at about 14 days, so ovulation in a 26-day cycle is expected near day 12.\",\"Serum progesterone reaches its mid-luteal maximum roughly 7 days after ovulation, which is around day 19 in this cycle.\",\"Progesterone reflects corpus-luteum function; measuring it about 7 days before the anticipated next period is more accurate than using a fixed 'day 21' rule for every cycle length.\"],\"tk\":\"Mid-luteal progesterone should be timed about 7 days after ovulation, not automatically on day 21.\",\"pr\":\"A progesterone sample is best scheduled relative to the expected next menses, especially when cycle length differs from 28 days.\",\"tp\":\"When assessing ovulation, document cycle length first and time progesterone approximately 7 days before the expected period.\",\"cn\":\"In a 26-day cycle ovulation is near day 12, so progesterone peaks about 7 days later, around day 19.\",\"sl\":\"Peak levels of progesterone are attained 7 days after ovulation; the luteal (progesterone) phase is fixed at 14 days while the follicular phase is variable.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[10,11]],[\"L33_1\",[2,6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F026\"},{\"id\":\"F211\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Neuraxial anesthesia and LMWH\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postpartum patient receiving low-molecular-weight heparin has just had an epidural catheter removed. Which statement is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"A prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) dose generally requires at least a 12-hour interval before epidural catheter removal\",\"Therapeutic-dose LMWH requires longer neuraxial timing precautions than prophylactic-dose LMWH\",\"LMWH can be restarted immediately after epidural catheter removal\",\"LMWH should not be given for at least 4 hours after epidural catheter removal\",\"Therapeutic-dose LMWH generally requires at least 24 hours before neuraxial techniques\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"With prophylactic LMWH, Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) guidance uses a 12-hour safety interval before neuraxial procedures or catheter removal.\",\"Therapeutic LMWH dosing requires a longer interval because residual anticoagulant effect and spinal-hematoma risk are greater.\",\"Immediate LMWH administration after catheter removal is unsafe because the fresh neuraxial tract carries a risk of spinal or epidural hematoma.\",\"After catheter removal, a minimum 4-hour delay before the next LMWH dose is recommended in the RCOG schedule.\",\"For therapeutic-dose LMWH, neuraxial techniques are generally avoided until at least 24 hours after the previous dose.\"],\"e\":[\"Neuraxial anesthesia in anticoagulated obstetric patients requires timed interruption of LMWH to minimize spinal hematoma.\",\"The incorrect statement is immediate restart; after epidural catheter removal, RCOG advises waiting at least 4 hours before LMWH is administered.\",\"Dose intensity matters because therapeutic anticoagulation has a longer clinically important anti-Xa effect, leading to a longer pre-neuraxial interval than prophylaxis.\"],\"tk\":\"LMWH must not be restarted immediately after epidural-catheter removal; neuraxial timing depends on dose intensity.\",\"pr\":\"RCOG uses 12 hours for prophylactic dosing and 24 hours for therapeutic dosing before neuraxial techniques.\",\"tp\":\"Write the last LMWH dose and catheter-removal time clearly in the chart before prescribing the next anticoagulant dose.\",\"cn\":\"After epidural catheter removal, wait at least 4 hours before giving low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH).\",\"sl\":\"Wait at least 12 hours after a prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) dose and at least 24 hours after a therapeutic dose before siting or removing an epidural catheter. After removing an epidural catheter, wait 4 hours before giving LMWH.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[21,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F211\"},{\"id\":\"F212\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Epidural analgesia contraindications\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a contraindication to epidural analgesia in labor?\",\"o\":[\"Patient refusal\",\"Maternal hypertension with stable hemodynamics\",\"Uncorrected severe coagulopathy\",\"Infection at the planned needle-insertion site\",\"Severe uncorrected hypovolemia\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Patient refusal is an absolute contraindication because neuraxial procedures require informed consent.\",\"Stable maternal hypertension is not itself a contraindication; neuraxial analgesia is commonly used in hypertensive pregnancies when coagulation and hemodynamics permit.\",\"Severe coagulopathy increases the risk of spinal or epidural hematoma and generally precludes neuraxial placement until corrected.\",\"A local infection at the insertion site risks inoculating deeper tissues and is a contraindication to needle placement through infected skin.\",\"Severe hypovolemia increases the danger of profound hypotension after sympathetic blockade and should be corrected before neuraxial anesthesia.\"],\"e\":[\"Epidural analgesia provides effective labor pain relief through neuraxial blockade and is widely used in obstetric care.\",\"Hypertension alone does not preclude an epidural; the important exclusions are conditions such as coagulopathy, refusal, local infection, or severe hypovolemia.\",\"Sympathetic blockade can lower systemic vascular resistance, so careful blood-pressure and volume assessment is required even when the technique is otherwise appropriate.\"],\"tk\":\"Maternal hypertension is not by itself a contraindication to epidural analgesia when coagulation and hemodynamics are acceptable.\",\"pr\":\"In severe preeclampsia, the platelet trend matters because thrombocytopenia may change neuraxial eligibility even when the blood pressure is controlled.\",\"tp\":\"Before neuraxial placement, document consent, platelet/coagulation status when indicated, infection status, and current volume/hemodynamic state.\",\"cn\":\"Hypertension alone is not a contraindication to epidural; refusal, coagulopathy, local infection and hypovolaemia are.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F212\"},{\"id\":\"F213\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Screening\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Routine fetal assessment timing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about routine antenatal fetal assessment is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Crown-rump length is the preferred first-trimester measurement for pregnancy dating\",\"A modified biophysical profile combines fetal heart-rate testing with amniotic-fluid assessment\",\"The detailed fetal anomaly scan is usually performed in the mid-trimester\",\"The routine structural anomaly scan is performed at about 8 weeks\",\"Amniotic-fluid assessment contributes information about chronic placental function\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Crown-rump length is the most accurate standard ultrasound measurement for first-trimester gestational dating.\",\"A modified biophysical profile uses a non-stress test (NST)/cardiotocography (CTG) component plus amniotic-fluid assessment to screen fetal wellbeing.\",\"Detailed anatomy is assessed around 18–22 weeks in many protocols, when fetal structures are sufficiently developed and visible.\",\"The routine fetal anomaly scan is not performed at 8 weeks; detailed structural assessment is undertaken in the mid-trimester.\",\"Reduced amniotic-fluid volume can reflect chronic placental insufficiency because fetal urine production depends on renal perfusion.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy dating and structural screening occur at different gestational windows: first-trimester crown–rump length (CRL) is used for dating, while anatomy is assessed later.\",\"The source contained a stray non-obstetric statement, so the item was repaired to test the intended point that the anomaly scan is a mid-trimester study, not an 8-week examination.\",\"Timing matters because organ development and fetal size determine what ultrasound can reliably show; a detailed structural survey requires later gestation than basic viability and dating assessment.\"],\"tk\":\"First-trimester dating and mid-trimester anomaly screening are distinct ultrasound tasks with different optimal timing.\",\"pr\":\"An early normal scan cannot replace the later systematic fetal-anatomy examination.\",\"tp\":\"When counseling after a first-trimester scan, explicitly schedule the mid-trimester anatomy study rather than implying that early viability imaging completes structural screening.\",\"cn\":\"The routine structural fetal anomaly scan is a mid-trimester scan (about 18 to 21 weeks), not an 8-week scan.\",\"sl\":\"In diabetes, an ultrasound scan at 18 to 21 weeks to detect fetal structural anomalies, including the fetal heart, should be offered; in twins the detailed anomaly scan is at about 20 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[14]],[\"L23_0\",[18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F081\"},{\"id\":\"F214\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":31,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hirsutism pharmacotherapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about treatment of hirsutism is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"5-alpha-reductase itself is used as an antiandrogen medication\",\"Antiandrogens require reliable contraception because of fetal risk\",\"Idiopathic hirsutism may occur with regular cycles and normal circulating androgen levels\",\"Finasteride reduces androgen effect by inhibiting 5-alpha-reductase\",\"Combined oral contraceptives are commonly used when pregnancy is not desired and no contraindication exists\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"5-alpha-reductase is an enzyme, not a therapeutic antiandrogen drug; medications such as finasteride work by inhibiting it.\",\"Agents such as spironolactone or finasteride require effective contraception because antiandrogen exposure can affect development of a male fetus.\",\"Idiopathic hirsutism can occur despite regular menses and normal serum androgen concentrations because follicular androgen sensitivity varies.\",\"Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase and thereby reduces conversion of testosterone to the more potent dihydrotestosterone.\",\"Combined oral contraceptives reduce ovarian androgen production and increase sex hormone-binding globulin, making them a common pharmacologic first step.\"],\"e\":[\"Hirsutism treatment combines cosmetic methods with suppression of androgen production or action when symptoms are bothersome.\",\"The false statement confuses a drug target with a medication: 5-alpha-reductase is an enzyme, whereas finasteride is an inhibitor.\",\"Because hair follicles respond slowly, pharmacologic improvement takes months and treatment must also account for pregnancy prevention when antiandrogens are used.\"],\"tk\":\"Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase; the enzyme itself is not an antiandrogen drug.\",\"pr\":\"New rapid-onset virilization or markedly elevated androgens should prompt evaluation for an androgen-secreting tumor rather than routine cosmetic management alone.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about pregnancy plans before prescribing antiandrogens and pair them with reliable contraception when they are used.\",\"cn\":\"Finasteride is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor used for hirsutism; the enzyme itself is not a drug.\",\"sl\":\"Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is formed from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase. Androgen antagonists (cyproterone acetate, spironolactone, flutamide, finasteride) are second-line and must be combined with effective contraception to avoid feminization of a male fetus.\",\"s\":[[\"L31_0\",[10,21,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F214\"},{\"id\":\"F215\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Lactational suppression of ovulation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which postpartum factor decreases the probability of ovulation and conception rather than increasing it?\",\"o\":[\"Frequent exclusive breastfeeding\",\"Return of regular menses\",\"Long intervals between feeds\",\"Regular supplemental formula feeds\",\"Infant age beyond 6 months\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Frequent exclusive breastfeeding increases prolactin-mediated suppression of GnRH pulsatility, delaying ovulation and supporting lactational amenorrhea.\",\"Return of regular menses indicates recovery of ovarian cycling and therefore increasing potential for conception.\",\"Long feed intervals reduce suckling stimulation and weaken the neuroendocrine suppression that maintains lactational amenorrhea.\",\"Regular supplemental feeds reduce breastfeeding frequency and therefore reduce the contraceptive reliability of the lactational amenorrhea method.\",\"After 6 months postpartum, lactational amenorrhea becomes less reliable even when breastfeeding continues because ovulation may resume.\"],\"e\":[\"Frequent suckling increases prolactin and suppresses hypothalamic GnRH pulsatility, which delays ovulation during lactation.\",\"The source asks for a factor that does not increase conception risk; frequent exclusive breastfeeding instead lowers the probability of ovulation in the early postpartum period.\",\"The lactational amenorrhea method is reliable only when all criteria are met: amenorrhea, exclusive or near-exclusive breastfeeding, and less than 6 months postpartum.\"],\"tk\":\"Frequent exclusive breastfeeding suppresses ovulation and lowers conception probability in the early postpartum period.\",\"pr\":\"Ovulation can precede the first postpartum period, so absence of menses outside validated LAM criteria should not be assumed to guarantee contraception.\",\"tp\":\"At postpartum visits, confirm all three LAM criteria and discuss a backup method before feeding frequency falls or the infant reaches 6 months.\",\"cn\":\"Frequent exclusive breastfeeding suppresses ovulation through suckling-induced prolactin inhibiting gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) pulsatility.\",\"sl\":\"Lactation delays ovulation: suckling-driven prolactin suppresses gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) pulsatility. The lactational amenorrhoea method (LAM) is over 98% effective only if amenorrhoeic, fully breastfeeding and less than 6 months postpartum.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[14,26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F215\"},{\"id\":\"F216\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Hydroxychloroquine in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication is considered compatible with pregnancy when clinically indicated?\",\"o\":[\"Methotrexate\",\"Hydroxychloroquine\",\"Valsartan\",\"Mycophenolate mofetil\",\"Cyclophosphamide during organogenesis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Methotrexate is a folate antagonist with embryotoxic and teratogenic effects and is contraindicated in pregnancy.\",\"Hydroxychloroquine is generally compatible with pregnancy and is commonly continued for indicated autoimmune disease.\",\"Valsartan blocks the fetal renin-angiotensin system and can cause oligohydramnios and fetal renal injury.\",\"Mycophenolate is strongly associated with pregnancy loss and a characteristic pattern of congenital malformations.\",\"Cyclophosphamide is avoided during organogenesis because alkylating agents carry substantial fetal toxicity and teratogenic risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Medication decisions in pregnancy balance fetal drug exposure against the harms of uncontrolled maternal disease.\",\"Among these agents, hydroxychloroquine has extensive pregnancy experience and is commonly continued for conditions such as lupus when indicated.\",\"By contrast, methotrexate and mycophenolate are teratogenic, while renin-angiotensin blockade can cause fetal renal toxicity later in gestation.\"],\"tk\":\"Hydroxychloroquine is compatible with pregnancy for appropriate maternal indications and often should not be stopped solely because of conception.\",\"pr\":\"Stopping effective lupus therapy can precipitate a maternal flare, which itself increases obstetric risk.\",\"tp\":\"Review teratogenic medications before conception and coordinate substitutions with the treating specialist rather than abruptly stopping disease-control therapy.\",\"cn\":\"Hydroxychloroquine is compatible with pregnancy and should be continued; methotrexate, mycophenolate, valsartan and cyclophosphamide are contraindicated.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F216\"},{\"id\":\"F217\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Cause\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Primary versus secondary postpartum hemorrhage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about postpartum hemorrhage is false?\",\"o\":[\"Subinvolution of the placental site is mainly a cause of primary postpartum hemorrhage\",\"Uterine atony is the most common cause of primary postpartum hemorrhage\",\"Retained placental tissue can contribute to secondary postpartum hemorrhage\",\"Secondary postpartum hemorrhage occurs after the first 24 hours postpartum\",\"Genital tract trauma can cause major bleeding despite a well-contracted uterus\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Placental-site subinvolution is classically associated with secondary postpartum hemorrhage, not bleeding confined to the first 24 hours.\",\"Uterine atony is the leading cause of primary postpartum hemorrhage because failure of myometrial contraction leaves placental vessels open.\",\"Retained products can prevent normal involution and cause secondary postpartum bleeding, often with infection or persistent lochia.\",\"Secondary PPH begins after 24 hours and can occur for several weeks postpartum depending on the definition used.\",\"Cervical or vaginal trauma can bleed briskly even when the uterus is firm and well contracted.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum hemorrhage is approached by cause using the 4 Ts: tone, trauma, tissue, and thrombin.\",\"Placental-site subinvolution is a cause of secondary PPH, so describing it as mainly a primary-PPH lesion is false.\",\"Primary atony reflects failure of myometrial contraction to compress the spiral vessels, whereas delayed bleeding often involves retained tissue, infection, or subinvolution.\"],\"tk\":\"Placental-site subinvolution is associated with secondary, not primary, postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"pr\":\"A firm uterus with ongoing heavy bleeding should shift attention toward laceration or coagulopathy rather than repeated uterotonic dosing alone.\",\"tp\":\"In delayed postpartum bleeding, assess hemodynamic status first, then evaluate for retained tissue and infection while resuscitation proceeds as needed.\",\"cn\":\"Subinvolution of the placental site causes secondary, not primary, postpartum haemorrhage (PPH).\",\"sl\":\"Secondary postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) is abnormal or excessive bleeding between 24 hours and 12 weeks postnatally; causes: endometritis, retained placental tissue, sub-involution of placental site, pseudo-aneurysms and arteriovenous malformations.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[2,24]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F217\"},{\"id\":\"F218\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is NOT typical of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Pruritus that may involve the palms and soles\",\"Elevated serum bile acids\",\"Increased risk of adverse fetal outcomes at higher bile-acid concentrations\",\"Symptoms that typically resolve after delivery\",\"A primary inflammatory skin rash\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pruritus, often prominent on the palms and soles and worse at night, is a characteristic symptom of the disorder.\",\"Elevated serum bile acids are a diagnostic marker and help stratify fetal risk in affected pregnancies.\",\"Higher bile-acid levels correlate with greater fetal risk, including stillbirth risk at severe concentrations.\",\"The biochemical and pruritic syndrome usually resolves postpartum, and persistent abnormalities should prompt evaluation for another liver disorder.\",\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) causes pruritus without a primary rash; excoriations may appear secondarily from scratching.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is characterized by pruritus and elevated bile acids in the absence of another explanation.\",\"A primary inflammatory skin rash is not typical; visible lesions are usually scratch marks rather than the cause of itching.\",\"Accumulated bile acids reflect altered hepatobiliary transport and are clinically important because severe elevations are associated with fetal complications.\"],\"tk\":\"ICP causes pruritus without a primary rash, with serum bile acids used to support diagnosis and risk stratification.\",\"pr\":\"New rash morphology, fever, or mucosal involvement should prompt evaluation for a dermatologic or systemic diagnosis rather than attributing all itching to ICP.\",\"tp\":\"Order liver tests and bile acids when ICP is suspected, and repeat testing if pruritus persists despite an initially normal bile-acid result.\",\"cn\":\"Obstetric cholestasis causes pruritus, especially of palms and soles, without a primary skin rash.\",\"sl\":\"Obstetric cholestasis presents with pruritus without skin rash other than excoriations (especially palms and soles), worse at night, with abnormal liver function tests (LFT) and raised bile acids; intrauterine fetal death has a little correlation with bile acid levels.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[16,17,19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F218\"},{\"id\":\"F219\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Placental abruption recurrence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which historical factor confers the greatest risk of placental abruption in a subsequent pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Previous placental abruption\",\"Controlled chronic hypertension alone\",\"Maternal age 35 years\",\"Prior uncomplicated cesarean birth\",\"Multiparity without prior abruption\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A history of placental abruption is one of the strongest risk factors for abruption in a subsequent pregnancy.\",\"Chronic hypertension increases abruption risk, particularly when complicated by superimposed preeclampsia.\",\"Advanced maternal age is associated with placental complications but does not carry the recurrence risk of a prior event.\",\"A prior cesarean birth is more strongly linked to placenta previa/accreta than to recurrent placental abruption.\",\"Multiparity is associated with several obstetric risks but is a weaker predictor than a documented prior abruption.\"],\"e\":[\"Placental abruption is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta and can cause concealed or revealed maternal hemorrhage and fetal compromise.\",\"Among the listed historical factors, a previous abruption provides the strongest specific recurrence signal.\",\"Decidual vascular injury and placental separation can recur in patients with persistent vascular risk factors, so subsequent pregnancies require attention to blood pressure and placental symptoms.\"],\"tk\":\"Previous placental abruption is a major risk factor for recurrence in a later pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"The amount of visible vaginal bleeding can underestimate total blood loss because a substantial component may remain concealed behind the placenta.\",\"tp\":\"Treat pain, bleeding, uterine hypertonus, or fetal compromise in a high-risk patient as an obstetric emergency while maternal resuscitation and fetal assessment proceed.\",\"cn\":\"Previous placental abruption is the most predictive risk factor for abruption in a subsequent pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"The most predictive risk factor is abruption in a previous pregnancy: 4.4% recurrence, and 19-25% after two previous abruptions.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F184\"},{\"id\":\"F220\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Uroflowmetry versus cystometry\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which parameter is NOT measured by simple uroflowmetry?\",\"o\":[\"Maximum urinary flow rate\",\"Voiding time\",\"Voided volume\",\"Urinary flow pattern\",\"First sensation of bladder filling\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maximum flow rate (Qmax) is a core uroflowmetry output and helps characterize voiding performance.\",\"Voiding time is derived directly from the flow trace and is part of the uroflowmetry record.\",\"Voided volume is recorded because a very small void can make the flow-rate interpretation unreliable.\",\"The shape of the flow curve can suggest obstruction, straining, or intermittent voiding.\",\"The first sensation of bladder filling is assessed during filling cystometry, not by free uroflowmetry.\"],\"e\":[\"Uroflowmetry is a noninvasive test that records the rate and pattern of urinary flow during spontaneous voiding.\",\"Bladder sensations require controlled filling and pressure measurement, so first sensation belongs to cystometry rather than uroflowmetry.\",\"Interpretation depends on adequate voided volume because Qmax and curve shape are volume dependent.\"],\"tk\":\"Uroflowmetry measures urine flow during voiding; bladder sensation is assessed during filling cystometry.\",\"pr\":\"A low Qmax is not diagnostic by itself because obstruction, weak detrusor contraction, and an inadequately filled bladder can produce similar traces.\",\"tp\":\"Record the voided volume together with Qmax and curve morphology before deciding whether further pressure-flow testing is needed.\",\"cn\":\"Uroflowmetry records flow rate, voided volume and flow pattern; first sensation of bladder filling needs filling cystometry.\",\"sl\":\"Uroflowmetry: simple, non-invasive, voided volume and flow rate; normal peak flow >15 mL/sec, voided volume >150 mL. Filling cystometry involves retrograde filling with pressures measured via transducers.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[33,34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F157\"},{\"id\":\"F221\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pelvic inflammatory disease diagnosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about pelvic inflammatory disease is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"A low threshold for empiric treatment is appropriate when pelvic tenderness is present and no better diagnosis explains the symptoms\",\"Symptoms must be present for at least 3 months before pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) can be diagnosed\",\"Wet-mount examination can identify concomitant bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis\",\"Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is the preferred diagnostic method for genital Chlamydia trachomatis infection\",\"Delay in treatment increases the risk of tubal damage and infertility\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"PID is primarily a clinical diagnosis, and treatment should not be delayed when compatible pelvic tenderness is present without a better explanation.\",\"PID is an acute clinical syndrome and has no 3-month minimum duration requirement; waiting would risk preventable upper-tract damage.\",\"A vaginal wet mount can show clue cells, motile trichomonads, and inflammatory cells that support associated lower-tract infection.\",\"NAAT is the most sensitive recommended method for detecting genital Chlamydia trachomatis infection.\",\"Delayed therapy increases the chance of tubal scarring, chronic pelvic pain, ectopic pregnancy, and infertility.\"],\"e\":[\"PID is an ascending infection of the upper genital tract that is diagnosed mainly from clinical findings, not by waiting for chronic symptoms.\",\"A mandatory 3-month symptom duration is incorrect and would dangerously delay treatment.\",\"Inflammation can scar the fallopian tubes, so the treatment threshold is intentionally low when cervical-motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness occurs in an appropriate setting.\"],\"tk\":\"PID should be treated promptly on clinical suspicion; there is no 3-month diagnostic duration requirement.\",\"pr\":\"Normal imaging does not exclude uncomplicated PID, whereas ultrasound becomes especially useful when tubo-ovarian abscess is suspected.\",\"tp\":\"Perform pregnancy testing and sexually transmitted infection (STI) nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs), but start empiric PID treatment when minimum clinical criteria are met rather than waiting for all laboratory results.\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is diagnosed clinically and treated promptly; no minimum symptom duration is required.\",\"sl\":\"Slides do not discuss pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) diagnosis; for chlamydia, nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is the current standard of care, with vulvovaginal swab the specimen of choice.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F221\"},{\"id\":\"F222\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":5,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postpartum diabetes management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about diabetes management immediately after delivery is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Glucose-lowering therapy used only for gestational diabetes is usually stopped immediately after birth\",\"Insulin needs usually fall rapidly after birth in patients with pre-existing diabetes\",\"Breastfeeding can increase maternal risk of hypoglycemia in insulin-treated diabetes\",\"Insulin requirements do not change after placental delivery\",\"Women with previous gestational diabetes require postpartum diabetes screening\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"For gestational diabetes, pharmacologic glucose-lowering treatment is typically stopped after delivery unless hyperglycemia persists.\",\"Patients with pre-existing diabetes generally need prompt insulin-dose reduction after birth to avoid hypoglycemia.\",\"Breastfeeding increases glucose use and can heighten hypoglycemia risk, especially around feeds in insulin-treated patients.\",\"Placental delivery removes major diabetogenic hormones, so insulin requirements change abruptly and usually fall rather than remaining unchanged.\",\"A history of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) predicts future type 2 diabetes, so structured postpartum testing and long-term surveillance are required.\"],\"e\":[\"Placental hormones create much of the pregnancy-associated insulin resistance, so their removal at birth rapidly changes insulin needs.\",\"The false statement is that insulin requirements remain unchanged; they usually fall immediately postpartum.\",\"GDM often resolves after delivery, but the metabolic predisposition persists, which is why postpartum diabetes screening remains necessary.\"],\"tk\":\"Insulin requirements typically fall sharply after delivery, and women with prior GDM still need postpartum diabetes testing.\",\"pr\":\"Breastfeeding mothers using insulin should have rapid access to carbohydrate because feeding can precipitate hypoglycemia.\",\"tp\":\"Reduce insulin promptly after birth, monitor capillary glucose closely, and arrange documented postpartum testing before discharge.\",\"cn\":\"Insulin requirements fall immediately after placental delivery; women with gestational diabetes stop glucose-lowering therapy after birth.\",\"sl\":\"Women with insulin-treated pre-existing diabetes should reduce their insulin immediately after birth and monitor blood glucose; women with gestational diabetes should stop blood glucose-lowering therapy immediately after birth.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[40]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F222\"},{\"id\":\"F223\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal complex ovarian cyst\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman has a unilateral multilocular ovarian cyst, no solid components or ascites, cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) of 40 IU/mL, and risk of malignancy index (RMI) I of 120. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Discharge with no follow-up because the RMI is below 200\",\"Repeat ultrasound only because CA-125 is below 50 IU/mL\",\"Surgical salpingo-oophorectomy, usually bilateral in a postmenopausal patient\",\"Immediate cytotoxic treatment without tissue diagnosis\",\"Needle aspiration as definitive treatment\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An RMI below 200 lowers malignancy risk, but conservative follow-up is reserved for cysts meeting simple, unilateral, unilocular criteria.\",\"A multilocular cyst is a non-simple feature and changes management even when CA-125 is only mildly elevated.\",\"Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) recommends considering salpingo-oophorectomy, usually bilateral, for a postmenopausal cyst with non-simple features and RMI below 200.\",\"Empiric cytotoxic therapy is inappropriate for an undiagnosed adnexal mass without oncologic staging or tissue diagnosis.\",\"Cyst aspiration has poor diagnostic and therapeutic value in this setting and risks spillage if malignancy is present.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmenopausal adnexal masses are stratified by ultrasound morphology, CA-125, and RMI I.\",\"Although the RMI is below 200, multilocularity means the cyst does not meet conservative simple-cyst criteria, so surgery is considered.\",\"In postmenopausal patients, removal is generally by salpingo-oophorectomy rather than cystectomy, reducing recurrence and inadvertent rupture risk.\"],\"tk\":\"A non-simple postmenopausal ovarian cyst with RMI I <200 generally warrants surgical assessment rather than simple surveillance.\",\"pr\":\"Unilateral, unilocular simple cysts ≤3 cm now require no routine follow-up under the 2025 RCOG/RCR consensus update.\",\"tp\":\"Calculate the RMI from the documented ultrasound score, menopausal status, and CA-125, then route RMI ≥200 cases for oncology assessment and CT.\",\"cn\":\"A postmenopausal ovarian cyst that is not simple, even with risk of malignancy index (RMI) below 200, warrants surgery, usually bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.\",\"sl\":\"A cyst in a postmenopausal woman is a tumour until proven otherwise; work-up is history, examination, ultrasound, cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) and computed tomography (CT) or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). Risk of malignancy index (RMI) = U x M x CA-125; refer to a gynaecological oncologist if CA-125 is above 200, ascites or metastasis.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[14,15,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F223\"},{\"id\":\"F224\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Low-transverse cesarean incision\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about a low-transverse lower-segment cesarean incision is correct compared with a classical uterine incision?\",\"o\":[\"It has a higher risk of rupture in a subsequent pregnancy\",\"It is routinely preferred when the lower uterine segment is inaccessible\",\"It has a lower risk of uterine rupture in a subsequent pregnancy\",\"It is more difficult to repair\",\"It usually causes more intraoperative bleeding\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A prior classical vertical incision carries the higher subsequent rupture risk because it traverses the contractile upper uterine segment.\",\"When the lower uterine segment is inaccessible, a classical incision may be required rather than the routine low-transverse approach.\",\"A prior low-transverse scar has substantially lower future uterine-rupture risk than a classical scar.\",\"Low-transverse incisions are generally easier to repair and heal with a stronger scar than classical uterine incisions.\",\"The low-transverse incision usually causes less bleeding than a classical incision because it is made in the less vascular lower segment.\"],\"e\":[\"The low-transverse lower-segment uterine incision is preferred for most cesareans because it has less blood loss and better scar integrity.\",\"The source's adhesions claim was not a reliable single-best discriminator, so the item was repaired to test the established advantage of lower future rupture risk.\",\"A classical incision crosses the contractile upper uterine corpus, creating greater mechanical stress in later pregnancy and a higher risk of scar dehiscence or rupture.\"],\"tk\":\"Low-transverse cesarean scars have a much lower subsequent uterine-rupture risk than classical scars.\",\"pr\":\"A prior classical incision is generally a contraindication to planned labor after cesarean because rupture can occur even before active labor.\",\"tp\":\"Review the prior operative note rather than assuming the uterine incision type from the skin incision.\",\"cn\":\"A low-transverse cesarean scar has much lower future uterine rupture risk (about 0.5%) than a classical scar (about 5%).\",\"sl\":\"Uterine rupture incidence: classical cesarean section 5%, lower uterine segment cesarean section (LUSCS) 0.5%.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[55]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F188\"},{\"id\":\"F225\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Primary dysmenorrhea mechanism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Pathophysiology of primary dysmenorrhea includes all of the following EXCEPT:\",\"o\":[\"Increased endometrial prostaglandin production\",\"Endometrioma as an underlying pelvic lesion\",\"Increased uterine contractility\",\"Elevated uterine resting tone\",\"Transient myometrial ischemia during menstruation\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Primary dysmenorrhea is driven by increased prostaglandin production in the secretory endometrium around menstruation.\",\"An endometrioma is a manifestation of endometriosis and therefore indicates secondary dysmenorrhea due to pelvic pathology.\",\"Prostaglandins increase uterine contractility, producing cramping pain and reduced uterine perfusion.\",\"Higher resting uterine tone contributes to painful contractions and impaired blood flow during menstruation.\",\"Intense contractions can cause transient myometrial ischemia, which contributes to the characteristic cyclic pain.\"],\"e\":[\"Primary dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation without structural pelvic disease and is mediated largely by excess endometrial prostaglandins.\",\"An endometrioma represents endometriosis, so it belongs to the differential for secondary rather than primary dysmenorrhea.\",\"Prostaglandin-driven contractions increase uterine tone and reduce perfusion, producing ischemic cramping pain that responds well to prostaglandin-synthesis inhibition.\"],\"tk\":\"Endometrioma is a cause of secondary dysmenorrhea, not a mechanism of primary dysmenorrhea.\",\"pr\":\"Pain that begins years after menarche, progressively worsens, or occurs outside menses should raise suspicion for secondary causes.\",\"tp\":\"Use scheduled non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) started just before or at onset of menses when primary dysmenorrhea is predictable and there are no contraindications.\",\"cn\":\"Primary dysmenorrhea is caused by uterine contraction and vasoconstriction without pelvic pathology; endometrioma causes secondary dysmenorrhea.\",\"sl\":\"Primary dysmenorrhoea is idiopathic; theories include uterine contraction and vasoconstriction. Secondary dysmenorrhoea is due to organic causes such as endometriosis, adenomyosis, pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) and fibroids.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[23,24,26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F225\"},{\"id\":\"F226\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Cervical cancer risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a recognized risk factor for cervical cancer?\",\"o\":[\"Persistent high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV) infection\",\"Cigarette smoking\",\"Immunosuppression\",\"High parity\",\"Low parity\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Persistent high-risk HPV infection is the central causal factor for cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and invasive cervical cancer.\",\"Smoking increases cervical cancer risk through local carcinogen exposure and impaired immune clearance of HPV.\",\"Immunosuppression, including human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, promotes HPV persistence and progression to high-grade lesions.\",\"High parity is associated with increased cervical cancer risk among HPV-exposed populations.\",\"Low parity is not a recognized risk factor; epidemiologic risk is associated with high parity rather than having few births.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent oncogenic HPV infection is necessary for nearly all cervical cancers, with host and behavioral cofactors modifying progression risk.\",\"Low parity is the exception; the established reproductive association is high parity, not few pregnancies.\",\"Smoking and immunosuppression reduce effective HPV clearance, increasing the probability that persistent infection progresses through cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) to invasive carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Low parity is not a cervical-cancer risk factor; persistent high-risk HPV is the dominant causal exposure.\",\"pr\":\"HPV vaccination prevents new vaccine-type infections but does not treat established invasive cervical cancer.\",\"tp\":\"Maintain screening even in vaccinated patients according to the national program because vaccination does not cover every oncogenic HPV type.\",\"cn\":\"Multiparity, not nulliparity, is associated with cervical cancer; human papillomavirus (HPV), smoking and sexual behaviour are risk factors.\",\"sl\":\"Multiparous, low socioeconomic class, poor hygiene. Risk factors: human papillomavirus (HPV) as the primary factor, smoking, HIV infection, multiple pregnancies.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[8,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F196\"},{\"id\":\"F227\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":7,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Secondary dysmenorrhea causes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is NOT a typical cause of secondary dysmenorrhea?\",\"o\":[\"Pelvic inflammatory disease\",\"Endometriosis\",\"Adenomyosis\",\"Uterine fibroids\",\"Polycystic ovary syndrome\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Chronic pelvic inflammation after PID can cause secondary pelvic pain and dysmenorrhea.\",\"Endometriosis is a classic cause of secondary dysmenorrhea, often with deep dyspareunia, dyschezia, or infertility.\",\"Adenomyosis can cause progressively painful, heavy menses with a tender globular uterus.\",\"Submucosal or large fibroids can contribute to secondary dysmenorrhea and heavy menstrual bleeding.\",\"PCOS commonly causes oligo-ovulation and irregular bleeding but is not a typical structural or inflammatory cause of secondary dysmenorrhea.\"],\"e\":[\"Secondary dysmenorrhea results from an identifiable pelvic disorder rather than isolated prostaglandin-mediated menstrual physiology.\",\"PCOS is the exception because its hallmark problems are ovulatory dysfunction and hyperandrogenism, not painful menses from pelvic lesions.\",\"Endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, and inflammatory pelvic disease can alter pelvic anatomy or inflammatory signaling and thereby produce secondary menstrual pain.\"],\"tk\":\"PCOS is not a typical cause of secondary dysmenorrhea, unlike endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, and pelvic inflammatory disease.\",\"pr\":\"Progressive dysmenorrhea with dyspareunia or dyschezia strongly raises suspicion for endometriosis.\",\"tp\":\"When dysmenorrhea changes from a longstanding stable pattern to progressive pain, look for secondary pathology rather than simply escalating analgesics.\",\"cn\":\"Secondary dysmenorrhoea is caused by pelvic pathology such as endometriosis, adenomyosis, pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) and fibroids, not polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).\",\"sl\":\"Secondary dysmenorrhoea is due to organic or psychosexual causes: endometriosis, adenomyosis, pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), intrauterine contraceptive device (IUCD), pelvic adhesions, fibroids (not always causal), cervical stenosis and obstructive congenital anomalies.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F227\"},{\"id\":\"F228\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Obstetric VTE parity threshold\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Under current obstetric venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk-assessment principles, which of the following is NOT by itself a standard listed antenatal risk factor?\",\"o\":[\"Previous venous thromboembolism\",\"Known thrombophilia\",\"Body mass index (BMI) of 30 kg/m² or more\",\"Parity of 2 or more\",\"Parity of 3 or more\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A previous VTE is one of the strongest predictors of pregnancy-associated recurrence and carries substantial weight in risk scoring.\",\"A thrombophilia can increase VTE risk, with the magnitude depending on type and personal/family history.\",\"BMI ≥30 kg/m² is a recognized maternal VTE risk factor and contributes to formal assessment.\",\"Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) lists parity ≥3, not parity ≥2 alone, as the parity-based VTE risk factor in its established scoring framework.\",\"Having had three or more births is the parity threshold used in the RCOG VTE risk-factor list.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy VTE assessment combines persistent maternal factors with current obstetric and transient risks such as immobility, infection, and cesarean birth.\",\"The parity threshold of parity ≥2 is not the standard RCOG criterion; the listed parity risk factor is parity ≥3.\",\"Risk factors are cumulative, so prophylaxis decisions depend on the total risk profile and may change at booking, admission, delivery, and postpartum review.\"],\"tk\":\"RCOG uses parity ≥3 as the parity-based VTE risk factor, not parity ≥2 alone.\",\"pr\":\"RCOG issued a September 2026 position statement while Green-top 37a is being updated; clinicians should follow the current guideline together with the interim amendments.\",\"tp\":\"Repeat formal VTE risk assessment after delivery because cesarean birth, hemorrhage, infection, or immobility can change prophylaxis needs.\",\"cn\":\"The parity threshold for venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk in pregnancy is parity 3 or more, not 2.\",\"sl\":\"Pre-existing venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk factors: previous VTE, age over 35, body mass index (BMI) 30 kg/m2 or more, parity 3 or more, smoking, medical conditions, thrombophilia.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F194\"},{\"id\":\"F229\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":1,\"L\":2,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Post-molar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about gestational trophoblastic neoplasia after a molar pregnancy is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) can be diagnosed from characteristic human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) trends without histopathologic proof\",\"A single normal hCG level confirms metastatic GTN\",\"GTN can be diagnosed only when uterine bleeding is present\",\"Pregnancy should be encouraged immediately during hCG surveillance\",\"Histopathologic proof is required before GTN can be diagnosed in every case\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A sustained hCG plateau or rise can establish post-molar GTN clinically without histopathologic confirmation.\",\"A normal hCG argues against active trophoblastic disease and does not establish metastatic GTN.\",\"Abnormal hCG kinetics can identify GTN even when vaginal bleeding is absent.\",\"A new pregnancy produces hCG and disrupts post-molar surveillance, so conception is deferred during the recommended monitoring period.\",\"Post-molar GTN is commonly diagnosed by serial hCG criteria, so tissue confirmation is not mandatory in every patient.\"],\"e\":[\"Post-molar surveillance relies on quantitative hCG trends because persistent trophoblastic tissue continues to secrete the hormone.\",\"Histopathology is not required when the serial pattern meets accepted GTN diagnostic criteria.\",\"The mechanism is persistent proliferating trophoblast after evacuation, so failure of hCG to fall appropriately provides a sensitive marker before symptoms or a mass necessarily appear.\"],\"tk\":\"Post-molar GTN can be diagnosed clinically from abnormal serial hCG trends without mandatory histopathology.\",\"pr\":\"A new pregnancy can obscure surveillance because normal gestation also raises hCG, which is why reliable contraception is advised during follow-up.\",\"tp\":\"Plot every post-evacuation hCG result chronologically; recognize a plateau or rise early and refer promptly to a trophoblastic-disease center.\",\"cn\":\"Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) can be diagnosed from human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) trends alone without histological confirmation.\",\"sl\":\"The diagnosis of gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) does not require histological confirmation; complete mole, partial mole, atypical placental site nodule and placental site trophoblastic tumour (PSTT)/epithelioid trophoblastic tumour (ETT) do.\",\"s\":[[\"L02_0\",[3,28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F229\"},{\"id\":\"F230\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Treatment duration for pregnancy-associated VTE\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about treatment of confirmed acute venous thromboembolism during pregnancy is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Warfarin is the preferred first-line anticoagulant throughout pregnancy\",\"Treatment can stop once leg pain and swelling resolve\",\"Therapeutic anticoagulation should continue through pregnancy and for at least 6 weeks postpartum, with at least 3 months total treatment\",\"A negative D-dimer is required before anticoagulation is discontinued\",\"Prophylactic-dose low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) is sufficient for a confirmed proximal deep vein thrombosis (DVT)\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Warfarin crosses the placenta and is generally avoided for routine treatment of acute VTE during pregnancy.\",\"Clinical symptom improvement does not mean the thrombus has resolved, so stopping therapy early exposes the patient to recurrence or embolization.\",\"Pregnancy-associated VTE requires therapeutic anticoagulation through pregnancy and for at least 6 weeks postpartum, with a minimum total duration of 3 months.\",\"Treatment duration is based on the provoked VTE context and minimum duration, not on obtaining a negative D-dimer.\",\"A confirmed proximal deep vein thrombosis (DVT) requires therapeutic, not prophylactic, anticoagulant dosing unless a specialist identifies a specific exception.\"],\"e\":[\"Low-molecular-weight heparin is the standard anticoagulant for most acute pregnancy-associated DVT or pulmonary embolism (PE) because it does not cross the placenta.\",\"Treatment extends into the puerperium because thrombosis risk remains high for 6 weeks postpartum, and the total course should be at least 3 months.\",\"Therapeutic anticoagulation prevents clot extension and embolization while endogenous fibrinolysis and vessel recanalization occur over time.\"],\"tk\":\"Confirmed acute VTE in pregnancy requires therapeutic anticoagulation through pregnancy and at least 6 weeks postpartum, with a minimum 3-month total course.\",\"pr\":\"A VTE diagnosed late in pregnancy often requires treatment beyond 6 weeks postpartum so the total duration still reaches at least 3 months.\",\"tp\":\"Before planned delivery, coordinate low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) interruption and neuraxial timing with obstetrics, hematology, and anesthesia rather than simply omitting doses ad hoc.\",\"cn\":\"Anticoagulation for venous thromboembolism (VTE) in pregnancy continues throughout pregnancy and at least 6 weeks postpartum, minimum 3 months total.\",\"sl\":\"Anticoagulant treatment should continue throughout pregnancy and until at least 6 weeks postpartum, and a minimum of at least 3 months should be given in total.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F230\"},{\"id\":\"F231\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Carpenter-Coustan gestational diabetes threshold\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Using the Carpenter-Coustan thresholds for the 100-g 3-hour oral glucose tolerance test, which 1-hour plasma glucose value meets the abnormal threshold?\",\"o\":[\"180 mg/dL\",\"140 mg/dL\",\"155 mg/dL\",\"130 mg/dL\",\"200 mg/dL\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The Carpenter-Coustan 100-g OGTT uses 180 mg/dL at 1 hour as an abnormal threshold.\",\"140 mg/dL is another common screening cutoff for a 50-g challenge and is lower than the 1-hour 100-g OGTT diagnostic threshold.\",\"155 mg/dL is below the Carpenter-Coustan 1-hour cutoff and is closer to values used at later sampling times.\",\"130 mg/dL is sometimes used as a screening threshold for the 50-g glucose challenge, not the Carpenter-Coustan 1-hour diagnostic value.\",\"200 mg/dL exceeds the diagnostic threshold but is not the defined 1-hour cutoff in the Carpenter-Coustan scheme.\"],\"e\":[\"Gestational-diabetes thresholds depend on the testing strategy; the US two-step approach can use a 100-g 3-hour OGTT after an abnormal screening challenge.\",\"Under Carpenter-Coustan criteria, the 1-hour threshold is 180 mg/dL, which is the defined value in this diagnostic scheme.\",\"Diagnostic interpretation requires the full set of fasting and timed values because the testing protocol reflects maternal glucose handling after a standardized load.\"],\"tk\":\"For the 100-g 3-hour Carpenter-Coustan OGTT, the 1-hour abnormal threshold is 180 mg/dL.\",\"pr\":\"Do not mix thresholds from the 50-g screening challenge, 75-g 2-hour OGTT, and 100-g 3-hour OGTT; they are different protocols.\",\"tp\":\"When reviewing an OGTT result, first identify the glucose load and protocol before deciding whether any time point is abnormal.\",\"cn\":\"The 1-hour abnormal glucose threshold is 180 mg/dL on the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) for gestational diabetes.\",\"sl\":\"International Association of Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups (IADPSG) thresholds for the 75 g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT): fasting 92 mg/dL, 1-hour 180 mg/dL, 2-hour 153 mg/dL; one or more values met or exceeded diagnoses gestational diabetes. Carpenter-Coustan criteria are not on the slides.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[30]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F231\"},{\"id\":\"F232\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Labor augmentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A laboring patient has slow progress with intact membranes and inadequate uterine contractions. Fetal status is reassuring and there is no evidence of cephalopelvic disproportion. What is the appropriate augmentation strategy?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate fundal pressure\",\"Amniotomy followed by oxytocin as needed\",\"Vacuum extraction before full cervical dilation\",\"Cesarean delivery without a trial of augmentation\",\"Observation despite ongoing inadequate contractions and failure to progress\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Fundal pressure is not a treatment for dystocia and may cause maternal or fetal harm.\",\"When labor progress is slow from inadequate uterine activity and obstruction is excluded, amniotomy and oxytocin are standard augmentation tools.\",\"Vacuum delivery requires full cervical dilation, an engaged head, and appropriate fetal position; it is not a first-stage augmentation method.\",\"Cesarean may be required if augmentation fails or obstruction/fetal compromise develops, but it is not automatically the first step when hypocontractility is correctable.\",\"Persistent ineffective contractions with stalled labor warrant reassessment and active augmentation rather than indefinite observation.\"],\"e\":[\"Labor dystocia can result from problems with power, passenger, or passage; augmentation is appropriate only after obstruction is excluded.\",\"In the setting of intact membranes and inadequate contractions, amniotomy with oxytocin can improve uterine activity and labor progress.\",\"Oxytocin increases coordinated myometrial contractions, while amniotomy can enhance endogenous prostaglandin release; both require fetal and contraction monitoring.\"],\"tk\":\"Amniotomy and oxytocin are appropriate for selected labor delay caused by inadequate uterine activity after CPD is excluded.\",\"pr\":\"Oxytocin should not be used to force labor through suspected mechanical obstruction because stronger contractions increase maternal and fetal risk.\",\"tp\":\"Before augmentation, document fetal position, station, cervical findings, contraction pattern, and fetal heart-rate status.\",\"cn\":\"Delay from inadequate contractions with intact membranes is managed by amniotomy, then oxytocin if progress remains slow.\",\"sl\":\"If delay is suspected, offer amniotomy and repeat vaginal examination in 2 hours; if progress is less than 1 cm, consider oxytocin, increasing no more often than every 30 minutes to 4-5 contractions in 10 minutes.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[6,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F232\"},{\"id\":\"F233\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fetal growth restriction consequences\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about fetal growth restriction is false?\",\"o\":[\"Placental insufficiency is a common cause\",\"Oligohydramnios may accompany placental insufficiency\",\"Fetal and neonatal hyperglycemia is characteristic\",\"Abdominal circumference and estimated fetal weight are key ultrasound measurements\",\"Affected infants are at increased risk of neonatal hypoglycemia\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Placental vascular insufficiency is a common mechanism of pathologic FGR, especially when maternal hypertensive disease is present.\",\"Reduced fetal renal perfusion can lower urine production and produce oligohydramnios in placental-insufficiency FGR.\",\"Growth-restricted fetuses are prone to hypoglycemia, not characteristic hyperglycemia, because glycogen and fat stores are limited.\",\"Serial abdominal circumference and estimated fetal weight are central ultrasound measures used to identify impaired growth.\",\"Low energy stores and limited gluconeogenic reserve make neonatal hypoglycemia a recognized complication of FGR.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal growth restriction reflects failure to reach biologic growth potential, commonly because of placental insufficiency.\",\"The false statement is hyperglycemia; growth-restricted fetuses and neonates are instead vulnerable to hypoglycemia and temperature instability.\",\"Chronic nutrient and oxygen restriction reduces hepatic glycogen and adipose reserves while redistributing blood flow, explaining both growth abnormalities and metabolic vulnerability after birth.\"],\"tk\":\"FGR is associated with neonatal hypoglycemia rather than hyperglycemia.\",\"pr\":\"Umbilical-artery Doppler abnormalities help distinguish placental insufficiency and guide surveillance and delivery timing in early or severe FGR.\",\"tp\":\"At birth, anticipate glucose and temperature instability in a growth-restricted neonate and ensure early feeding and monitoring.\",\"cn\":\"Fetal growth restriction (FGR) is associated with neonatal hypoglycaemia, not hyperglycaemia, because of depleted glycogen and fat stores.\",\"sl\":\"Reduced glucose transfer causes fetal hypoglycaemia; depleted liver glycogen and fat stores give a small abdominal circumference (AC) and neonatal hypoglycaemia and hypothermia.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[24,55]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F151\"},{\"id\":\"F234\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Cause\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Central versus peripheral precocious puberty\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about precocious puberty is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Central precocious puberty results from premature activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis\",\"Central precocious puberty is GnRH-dependent in only 75% of cases\",\"Evaluation includes growth velocity and Tanner staging\",\"Bone age is often advanced when sex-steroid exposure is sustained\",\"Peripheral precocious puberty is gonadotropin-independent\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Central precocious puberty reflects premature GnRH pulsatility and activation of pituitary gonadotropin secretion.\",\"Central precocious puberty is GnRH-dependent by definition, not in only 75% of affected patients.\",\"Assessment of growth velocity and Tanner stage helps establish whether pubertal development is progressive and premature.\",\"Sustained sex-steroid exposure accelerates epiphyseal maturation, so bone age is commonly advanced.\",\"Peripheral precocious puberty arises from sex steroids independent of central gonadotropin drive.\"],\"e\":[\"Precocious puberty is divided mechanistically into central GnRH-dependent and peripheral GnRH-independent forms.\",\"The statement limiting GnRH dependence to 75% is incorrect because central precocious puberty is defined by central axis activation.\",\"Premature sex-steroid exposure accelerates linear growth initially but also advances skeletal maturation, risking reduced adult height if untreated.\"],\"tk\":\"Central precocious puberty is GnRH-dependent by definition, whereas peripheral puberty is gonadotropin-independent.\",\"pr\":\"A pubertal luteinising hormone (LH) response after GnRH stimulation supports central activation when basal gonadotropin results are equivocal.\",\"tp\":\"Plot height velocity and obtain bone age in progressive cases; rapid progression or neurologic features should prompt specialist evaluation.\",\"cn\":\"Central precocious puberty is by definition gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH)-dependent; peripheral precocious puberty is gonadotropin-independent.\",\"sl\":\"Central (gonadotrophin-dependent, true) precocious puberty accounts for 80% of cases and the majority (75%) are idiopathic; peripheral precocious puberty (pseudopuberty) is 20% and always pathological. Gonadotrophins are elevated in central and suppressed in peripheral.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[6,7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F234\"},{\"id\":\"F235\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":9,\"L\":43,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Vulvar cancer risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor is associated with an increased risk of vulvar squamous cell carcinoma?\",\"o\":[\"Combined oral contraceptive use\",\"Prior appendectomy\",\"Early menopause\",\"Low body mass index\",\"Cigarette smoking\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Combined oral contraceptives are not a defining risk factor for vulvar squamous carcinoma.\",\"Prior appendectomy is unrelated to the HPV- or dermatosis-associated pathways of vulvar cancer.\",\"Early menopause is not a recognized major vulvar-cancer risk factor.\",\"A low body mass index has no established direct causal role in vulvar squamous carcinogenesis.\",\"Smoking is associated with HPV-related vulvar neoplasia and increases risk of vulvar squamous cell carcinoma.\"],\"e\":[\"Vulvar squamous carcinomas arise through an HPV-associated pathway or an HPV-independent pathway linked to chronic vulvar dermatoses.\",\"Smoking is an established cofactor for HPV persistence and vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia.\",\"Chronic inflammatory disorders such as lichen sclerosus promote epithelial injury and atypia through a separate differentiated vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia (VIN) pathway.\"],\"tk\":\"Smoking increases the risk of vulvar squamous cell carcinoma, particularly in HPV-associated disease.\",\"pr\":\"Lichen sclerosus carries a small but important lifetime risk of vulvar squamous carcinoma, so new ulceration or a persistent focal lesion requires biopsy.\",\"tp\":\"Biopsy any persistent, changing, pigmented, ulcerated, or indurated vulvar lesion rather than treating chronic pruritus empirically for months.\",\"cn\":\"Smoking is an important cofactor for human papillomavirus (HPV)-related vulvar squamous carcinoma.\",\"sl\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV), usually type 16, 80-90% under the age of 50; smoking is an important cofactor.\",\"s\":[[\"L43_0\",[3]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F035\"},{\"id\":\"F236\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":7,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Timing intercourse for conception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which advice about timing intercourse for natural conception is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Regular intercourse every 2–3 days gives good coverage of the fertile window\",\"Female age is an important determinant of natural fertility\",\"Ovulation-prediction kits can help identify the fertile interval for some couples\",\"Intercourse should be restricted to the exact day of ovulation\",\"Sperm survival for several days means intercourse before ovulation can result in conception\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Intercourse every 2–3 days is recommended by National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) because it reliably covers the fertile window without requiring exact ovulation prediction.\",\"Natural fecundity declines with increasing female age, particularly from the mid-30s onward.\",\"Urinary LH-based ovulation-prediction kits can help some couples identify the peri-ovulatory interval but are not mandatory for conception.\",\"Restricting intercourse to the exact ovulation day is unnecessarily narrow because conception can follow intercourse in the preceding days.\",\"Sperm can remain viable for several days in favorable cervical mucus, so intercourse before ovulation can lead to fertilization.\"],\"e\":[\"Natural conception depends on exposure during the fertile window, which extends across several days rather than a single moment.\",\"NICE advises vaginal intercourse every 2–3 days, so limiting attempts to the exact ovulation day is incorrect.\",\"Sperm survival before ovulation and the short post-ovulatory lifespan of the oocyte make repeated intercourse across the window more reliable than single-day timing.\"],\"tk\":\"Intercourse should not be limited to the exact day of ovulation; every 2–3 days optimizes natural conception chances.\",\"pr\":\"Ovulation-prediction kits may reduce uncertainty but can increase stress and are not required when intercourse is already regular.\",\"tp\":\"For couples trying naturally, recommend regular intercourse every 2–3 days and reserve more intensive timing strategies for specific clinical circumstances.\",\"cn\":\"Intercourse every 2 to 3 days optimises the chance of natural conception; restricting it to ovulation day is incorrect.\",\"sl\":\"Frequency of intercourse every 2 to 3 days optimises the chance of pregnancy (once weekly 17% versus 3 times weekly 50% within 6 months). 85% of couples conceive within 1 year if the woman is under 35.\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[5,6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F236\"},{\"id\":\"F237\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Routine antenatal booking investigations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"At the booking visit for an otherwise uncomplicated pregnancy, which investigation is not routinely offered to every patient?\",\"o\":[\"Full blood count\",\"Blood group and rhesus D status\",\"Screening for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), syphilis, and hepatitis B according to the antenatal programme\",\"Routine serum kidney function tests in every low-risk pregnancy\",\"Screening for sickle cell disease and thalassaemia according to the antenatal programme\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A full blood count is routinely offered early in pregnancy to identify anemia and establish a hematologic baseline.\",\"Maternal ABO group and rhesus D status are established at booking because they affect transfusion planning and anti-D prophylaxis.\",\"Antenatal infectious-disease programmes routinely include human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), syphilis, and hepatitis B screening to reduce vertical transmission through timely treatment and prophylaxis.\",\"Serum creatinine and renal-function testing are obtained when renal disease, hypertension, preeclampsia risk, or another indication is present; they are not universal booking tests for low-risk pregnancies.\",\"Screening for sickle cell disease and thalassaemia identifies maternal or fetal carrier status according to the antenatal screening pathway.\"],\"e\":[\"Routine antenatal booking establishes maternal baseline health with full blood count, blood group/rhesus D (RhD) status, and nationally recommended infection and haemoglobinopathy screening.\",\"In a healthy patient without renal disease or hypertension, serum kidney-function tests are not part of the universal low-risk booking panel.\",\"Renal investigations become appropriate when history, blood pressure, urinalysis, or comorbidity suggests renal impairment or hypertensive disease, because pregnancy alters renal physiology and abnormal creatinine has clinical significance.\"],\"tk\":\"Routine booking investigations should be distinguished from tests ordered for a specific maternal risk factor or abnormal finding.\",\"pr\":\"A normal serum creatinine in pregnancy is typically lower than outside pregnancy because glomerular filtration increases.\",\"tp\":\"At booking, document blood pressure and urine findings; order renal studies promptly when hypertension, proteinuria, or known kidney disease is present.\",\"cn\":\"Serum kidney function tests are not part of routine booking investigations in a low-risk pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Booking investigations: complete blood count (CBC), blood group (BG), urine analysis and culture, hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), rubella IgG and screening for sexually transmitted diseases (STDs).\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F237\"},{\"id\":\"F238\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Risk\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"TOLAC and uterine rupture risk\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman with a previous cesarean delivery has an ultrasound-estimated macrosomic fetus and is being counselled about labor. Which statement is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"A prior classical uterine incision carries a higher rupture risk than a prior low-transverse incision\",\"Suspected fetal macrosomia makes uterine rupture inevitable and is an absolute contraindication to trial of labour after caesarean (TOLAC)\",\"Induction of labor can increase the risk of scar rupture during TOLAC\",\"Continuous fetal heart-rate monitoring is recommended during planned vaginal birth after caesarean (VBAC) labor\",\"A facility offering TOLAC should be able to perform emergency cesarean delivery if scar rupture is suspected\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A classical uterine scar lies in the contractile upper segment and has a substantially greater rupture risk than a low-transverse scar.\",\"Suspected macrosomia increases labor and shoulder-dystocia risk, but it does not make scar rupture inevitable and is not by itself an absolute prohibition of TOLAC.\",\"Labor induction or augmentation after cesarean can raise scar-rupture risk compared with spontaneous labor, so method and indication matter.\",\"Continuous fetal heart-rate monitoring is used during VBAC because abrupt fetal heart-rate abnormalities may be an early sign of scar dehiscence or rupture.\",\"Planned VBAC requires access to urgent operative delivery because suspected rupture demands rapid maternal and fetal assessment and usually emergency cesarean.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine-rupture risk after cesarean is driven mainly by uterine scar type, prior rupture, and intrapartum factors such as induction rather than by fetal size alone.\",\"In this patient, suspected macrosomia warrants individualized counselling about labor dystocia and shoulder dystocia, but an absolute statement that rupture is inevitable is incorrect.\",\"A classical scar involves the active upper uterine segment, while a low-transverse scar is less stressed in labor; induction can increase scar stress, which is why intrapartum monitoring and emergency surgical capability are central to TOLAC safety.\"],\"tk\":\"Suspected macrosomia alone should not be equated with inevitable uterine rupture or an automatic absolute contraindication to TOLAC.\",\"pr\":\"A prior classical cesarean incision is a major reason VBAC is generally not advised because rupture risk is substantially higher.\",\"tp\":\"Review the operative note for the uterine incision type before counselling about VBAC; the skin scar does not reliably identify the uterine scar.\",\"cn\":\"Suspected macrosomia does not make uterine rupture inevitable; scar type and induction drive trial of labour after cesarean (TOLAC) risk.\",\"sl\":\"Uterine rupture risk with vaginal birth after cesarean (VBAC) depends on the previous scar: classical 5%, lower segment 0.5%; risk factors include induction or augmentation and macrosomia. Previous cesarean (VBAC) needs continuous cardiotocography (CTG).\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[55,56]],[\"L15_0\",[62]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F238\"},{\"id\":\"F239\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"First-line long-term treatment of heavy menstrual bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 40-year-old multiparous woman has heavy menstrual bleeding, no major cavity-distorting pathology, and no current desire for pregnancy. Which long-term treatment is preferred first?\",\"o\":[\"Tranexamic acid taken during menses\",\"Endometrial ablation as initial therapy\",\"Hysterectomy as initial therapy\",\"Combined hormonal contraception\",\"Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Tranexamic acid reduces menstrual blood loss by inhibiting fibrinolysis and is useful when an LNG-IUS is declined or unsuitable.\",\"Endometrial ablation produces endometrial destruction and is generally reserved for appropriate patients after less invasive therapy is unsuccessful or unsuitable.\",\"Hysterectomy is definitive major surgery with greater morbidity and recovery burden than first-line medical treatment.\",\"Combined hormonal contraception uses an estrogen-progestin regimen that can reduce bleeding but is an alternative when an LNG-IUS is declined or unsuitable.\",\"The levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system causes endometrial suppression and is recommended first when no major cavity-distorting pathology is present.\"],\"e\":[\"Heavy menstrual bleeding management depends on symptoms, uterine pathology, fertility goals, and patient preference; for suitable patients, the LNG-IUS is first-line long-term therapy.\",\"This woman has no major cavity-distorting lesion and does not seek pregnancy, so the levonorgestrel intrauterine system offers both substantial bleeding reduction and contraception.\",\"Local progestin exposure induces endometrial suppression, reducing menstrual blood loss while avoiding the invasiveness of ablation or hysterectomy.\"],\"tk\":\"Use the LNG-IUS as first-line treatment for suitable HMB patients without major cavity-distorting pathology.\",\"pr\":\"Tranexamic acid is a useful nonhormonal alternative and is taken only during bleeding days.\",\"tp\":\"Before inserting an LNG-IUS, exclude pregnancy and assess for symptoms or risk factors that warrant investigation for endometrial or structural disease.\",\"cn\":\"The levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) is the preferred first-line medical treatment for heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) without major cavity distortion.\",\"sl\":\"First-line medical options for heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) are the levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS), tranexamic acid, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and combined hormonal contraception; surgery is chosen by cause and fertility wishes.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[17,27]],[\"L33_0\",[62]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F085\"},{\"id\":\"F240\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Nuchal translucency and aneuploidy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"An increased first-trimester nuchal translucency measurement is classically associated with which fetal condition?\",\"o\":[\"Trisomy 21\",\"Open neural tube defect\",\"Placenta previa\",\"Isolated fetal growth restriction\",\"Ectopic pregnancy\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Increased nuchal translucency is associated with fetal aneuploidy, particularly trisomy 21, and also with some structural anomalies.\",\"Open neural tube defects are associated with elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) and characteristic second-trimester ultrasound findings rather than increased NT as the primary marker.\",\"Placenta previa is defined by placental relationship to the internal cervical os and is assessed by ultrasound later in pregnancy.\",\"Fetal growth restriction is evaluated with serial biometry and Doppler studies, not nuchal translucency as its defining marker.\",\"An ectopic pregnancy is diagnosed by pregnancy location using transvaginal ultrasound and serial human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) assessment, not fetal neck measurement.\"],\"e\":[\"First-trimester combined screening incorporates nuchal translucency with maternal age and biochemical markers to estimate aneuploidy risk.\",\"An increased NT raises concern for trisomy 21 and other chromosomal abnormalities, although it is a screening finding rather than a diagnosis.\",\"NT reflects fluid accumulation in the fetal neck; abnormal measurements can also accompany cardiac and structural anomalies, so abnormal screening may prompt diagnostic testing and targeted ultrasound.\"],\"tk\":\"Increased nuchal translucency is a first-trimester marker of fetal aneuploidy, especially trisomy 21.\",\"pr\":\"A normal NT does not exclude aneuploidy; screening performance improves when NT is combined with biochemical or cell-free DNA-based screening strategies.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm gestational age and crown-rump length before interpreting NT because valid measurement depends on the appropriate first-trimester window.\",\"cn\":\"Increased first-trimester nuchal translucency (NT) is classically associated with fetal aneuploidy, especially trisomy 21.\",\"sl\":\"The combined test of nuchal translucency (NT), beta human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG) and pregnancy-associated plasma protein A (PAPP-A) at 11 to 14 weeks screens for chromosomal anomalies; cell-free DNA performs well for trisomy 21.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F109\"},{\"id\":\"F241\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":3,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antiseizure medication in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about antiseizure medication treatment during pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Valproate carries a comparatively high risk of major congenital malformations and adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes\",\"Abruptly stopping effective antiseizure medication can endanger both the pregnant patient and fetus\",\"Single-drug treatment generally has a higher teratogenic risk than multidrug treatment when seizure control can be maintained\",\"Medication choice should be optimized before conception whenever possible\",\"Folic acid supplementation is recommended for patients with epilepsy who may become pregnant\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Valproate has one of the highest established fetal risk profiles, including dose-related neural-tube and neurodevelopmental harm.\",\"Abrupt drug withdrawal may precipitate breakthrough seizures or status epilepticus, creating maternal trauma, hypoxia, and fetal risk.\",\"When seizure control permits, single-drug therapy at the lowest effective dose is generally preferred because avoiding unnecessary multiple-drug fetal exposure can reduce treatment-related risk.\",\"Preconception review allows clinicians to choose a safer effective regimen before organogenesis while avoiding destabilizing medication changes after conception.\",\"Guidelines recommend folic acid supplementation before conception and during pregnancy as part of preconception risk reduction for people taking antiseizure medication.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy epilepsy care balances prevention of maternal seizures against fetal medication exposure; uncontrolled generalized seizures can be dangerous to both patient and fetus.\",\"The false statement reverses a core prescribing principle: when clinically suitable, effective monotherapy at the lowest effective dose is favored over unnecessary polytherapy.\",\"Teratogenic risk varies substantially by drug and dose; valproate carries particularly high fetal risk, so medication selection should be optimized before conception without abrupt discontinuation.\"],\"tk\":\"Use the safest effective antiseizure regimen while maintaining seizure control; unnecessary polytherapy should be avoided.\",\"pr\":\"Lamotrigine and levetiracetam often require serum-level or clinical monitoring during pregnancy because pharmacokinetics can change substantially.\",\"tp\":\"Do not advise a pregnant patient to stop antiseizure medication abruptly; coordinate changes with neurology and maternal-fetal medicine.\",\"cn\":\"Antiepileptic polytherapy carries greater teratogenic risk than monotherapy; use a single drug at the lowest effective dose.\",\"sl\":\"Polytherapy with more than one drug carries greater risk than monotherapy. Valproate carries the greatest risk and should be avoided when planning pregnancy. Pre-pregnancy: simplify to a single antiepileptic drug (AED) at lowest effective dose and give folic acid 5 mg daily.\",\"s\":[[\"L03_0\",[3,10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F241\"},{\"id\":\"F242\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Mechanism of vaginal breech birth\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"During a vaginal breech birth, which statement about the normal mechanism of delivery is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Fundal pressure is routinely used to force the aftercoming head through the pelvis\",\"Vacuum extraction is applied to the breech to deliver the trunk\",\"The sacrum normally rotates posteriorly and remains posterior throughout delivery\",\"Traction on the fetus should be used routinely before spontaneous delivery to the umbilicus\",\"The anterior buttock rotates toward the symphysis as the bitrochanteric diameter turns into the anteroposterior diameter\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Routine fundal pressure is not a standard maneuver for normal vaginal breech birth; excessive external uterine force can increase maternal or fetal injury.\",\"A vacuum cup is designed for the fetal scalp in selected cephalic operative births and is not applied to the breech trunk.\",\"Normal internal rotation brings the fetal sacrum anteriorly, facilitating sequential delivery of the hips and trunk.\",\"During an assisted breech birth, the fetus is generally allowed to deliver spontaneously to the umbilicus before specific maneuvers are used if needed.\",\"As the breech descends, the anterior buttock rotates anteriorly and the bitrochanteric diameter rotates into the anteroposterior pelvic diameter.\"],\"e\":[\"Vaginal breech birth follows a sequence of descent and internal rotation of the buttocks, followed by delivery of the trunk, shoulders, and aftercoming head.\",\"The normal pelvic mechanism brings the anterior buttock beneath the symphysis as the bitrochanteric diameter rotates into the anteroposterior diameter.\",\"Controlled delivery avoids premature traction because extension of the arms or head can complicate birth; specific maneuvers are reserved for failure of spontaneous progression at each stage.\"],\"tk\":\"Breech delivery depends on orderly rotation of the bitrochanteric diameter and controlled delivery without routine traction.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent sacroposterior position makes delivery of the aftercoming head more difficult and may require skilled maneuvers.\",\"tp\":\"Call an experienced obstetrician and neonatal team early for planned or unexpected vaginal breech birth; success depends heavily on operator skill.\",\"cn\":\"In vaginal breech birth the bitrochanteric diameter rotates into the anteroposterior diameter, with the sacrum then brought anterior.\",\"sl\":\"The breech usually emerges sacrum transverse or oblique; as the bitrochanteric diameter passes under the symphysis an episiotomy can be considered; the fetus is then rotated to the sacrum anterior position.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[30]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F242\"},{\"id\":\"F243\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":2,\"L\":44,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Fibroids during pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about uterine fibroids during pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Red degeneration can cause acute localized pain\",\"Fibroids invariably enlarge throughout pregnancy\",\"Large or lower-segment fibroids may contribute to malpresentation\",\"Many fibroids remain asymptomatic\",\"Fibroids can increase the risk of postpartum hemorrhage in selected patients\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Red degeneration is a painful ischemic degeneration of a leiomyoma that is classically associated with pregnancy.\",\"Fibroid size can increase, decrease, or remain stable during pregnancy; continuous enlargement is not inevitable.\",\"Large or strategically located fibroids may distort the uterine cavity and contribute to malpresentation or labor obstruction.\",\"Many leiomyomas remain asymptomatic and are found as an incidental ultrasound finding during pregnancy.\",\"Fibroids can contribute to uterine atony, which increases postpartum hemorrhage risk in selected pregnancies.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine leiomyomas are hormone-responsive benign smooth-muscle tumors, but their behavior in pregnancy is variable rather than uniformly progressive.\",\"The false statement is that fibroids always enlarge; some remain unchanged or become smaller, while symptomatic lesions may undergo red degeneration.\",\"Clinical impact depends more on size and location than simple presence, with possible malpresentation, labor obstruction, cesarean delivery, and postpartum uterine atony.\"],\"tk\":\"Fibroid growth during pregnancy is variable, so universal enlargement should not be assumed.\",\"pr\":\"Pain from red degeneration is usually managed conservatively after excluding obstetric emergencies such as abruption or preterm labor.\",\"tp\":\"Document the number, size, and location of clinically important fibroids because lower-segment lesions may alter delivery planning.\",\"cn\":\"Fibroid growth in pregnancy is variable (most do not grow significantly); red degeneration causes acute pain.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F243\"},{\"id\":\"F244\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Maternal physiologic changes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Normal pregnancy produces all of the following physiologic changes except which one?\",\"o\":[\"Increased maternal plasma volume\",\"Decreased functional residual capacity\",\"Increased glomerular filtration rate\",\"Increased serum albumin concentration\",\"Mild chronic respiratory alkalosis\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal plasma volume increases substantially, exceeding the rise in red-cell mass and contributing to physiologic hemodilution.\",\"The enlarging uterus causes diaphragm elevation, reducing functional residual capacity despite increased minute ventilation.\",\"Renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rate increase, which lowers normal serum creatinine during pregnancy.\",\"Serum albumin concentration decreases because of hemodilution and altered protein distribution rather than increasing.\",\"Progesterone-driven hyperventilation produces chronic respiratory alkalosis with renal bicarbonate compensation.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal pregnancy causes coordinated cardiovascular, respiratory, renal, and protein adaptations that support maternal-fetal physiology.\",\"Plasma expansion produces hemodilution and a lower serum albumin concentration, so an increase in albumin is not expected.\",\"Progesterone-driven hyperventilation causes mild respiratory alkalosis, while increased renal blood flow raises GFR and lowers serum creatinine compared with nonpregnant values.\"],\"tk\":\"Normal pregnancy lowers serum albumin concentration while increasing plasma volume and glomerular filtration.\",\"pr\":\"Because GFR rises, a serum creatinine that appears merely “normal” for a nonpregnant adult may represent renal impairment in pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret laboratory values using pregnancy-specific expectations rather than nonpregnant reference ranges whenever possible.\",\"cn\":\"Serum albumin falls in pregnancy due to haemodilution, while plasma volume and glomerular filtration rate (GFR) rise.\",\"sl\":\"Albumin/total protein fall due to haemodilution. Plasma volume rises 40-50%; functional residual capacity (FRC) falls about 20%; glomerular filtration rate (GFR) rises about 50%; blood gases show compensated respiratory alkalosis.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[22,31,33,37,45]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F244\"},{\"id\":\"F245\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Assessment of antepartum hemorrhage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient presents with antepartum hemorrhage. Which statement is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"The volume of blood seen vaginally reliably equals the total maternal blood loss\",\"Concealed placental abruption can cause major blood loss with limited visible bleeding\",\"Digital vaginal examination should be avoided until placenta previa has been excluded\",\"Placental location is assessed with ultrasound when placenta previa is suspected\",\"Maternal hemodynamic status may be more informative than the visible amount of bleeding\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Visible vaginal bleeding can underestimate total hemorrhage, particularly when blood is concealed behind an abrupted placenta.\",\"Placental abruption may produce concealed retroplacental bleeding, so maternal compromise can exceed the apparent external loss.\",\"A digital examination can trigger cervical manipulation across a placenta previa, so it is deferred until placental location is known.\",\"Ultrasound defines the placental edge relative to the internal os when a low-lying placenta or previa is suspected.\",\"Pulse, blood pressure, mental status, perfusion, and laboratory trends help assess maternal blood-loss severity when visible bleeding is misleading.\"],\"e\":[\"Antepartum hemorrhage requires simultaneous maternal stabilization and evaluation of placental and fetal causes; the amount seen externally may poorly reflect true blood loss.\",\"Concealed abruption can produce substantial internal bleeding, so a reassuring-looking pad or small vaginal loss must not outweigh maternal vital signs and uterine findings.\",\"Because placenta previa can bleed catastrophically after cervical manipulation, digital vaginal examination is avoided until placental location is established, usually by ultrasound.\"],\"tk\":\"Do not estimate APH severity from visible blood loss alone because concealed bleeding can be substantial.\",\"pr\":\"Abruption is primarily a clinical diagnosis; ultrasound can be normal even when clinically important abruption is present.\",\"tp\":\"Establish intravenous (IV) access and assess maternal hemodynamics promptly in significant APH while fetal monitoring and placental localization proceed in parallel.\",\"cn\":\"Visible vaginal loss underestimates blood loss in antepartum haemorrhage (APH), especially concealed abruption; assess for shock.\",\"sl\":\"The amount of blood from the introitus may not represent the total blood lost, for example in concealed placental abruption, so assess for signs of clinical shock.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[5,15,41]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F245\"},{\"id\":\"F246\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Placental-insufficiency fetal growth restriction\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding fetal growth restriction caused by placental insufficiency, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Reduced fetal glycogen and fat stores increase neonatal hypoglycemia risk\",\"Abdominal circumference and estimated fetal weight are central ultrasound measurements\",\"Polyhydramnios is the typical amniotic-fluid abnormality\",\"Oligohydramnios may accompany chronic placental insufficiency\",\"Umbilical artery Doppler can help assess placental vascular resistance\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Growth-restricted neonates have limited glycogen and adipose reserves, increasing the risk of hypoglycemia after birth.\",\"Serial abdominal circumference and estimated fetal weight help detect abnormal growth velocity and classify fetal size.\",\"Placental insufficiency more often reduces fetal urine output and is associated with oligohydramnios, not polyhydramnios as the typical fluid pattern.\",\"Chronic fetal hypoxemia can redistribute blood flow away from the kidneys, contributing to reduced amniotic fluid volume.\",\"Umbilical artery Doppler evaluates placental resistance through end-diastolic flow patterns, helping stratify surveillance and delivery timing.\"],\"e\":[\"Placental-insufficiency FGR reflects inadequate placental transfer of oxygen and nutrients and is assessed with fetal biometry and Doppler surveillance.\",\"The characteristic fluid abnormality is often oligohydramnios, because chronic redistribution can reduce renal perfusion and fetal urine production.\",\"Reduced nutrient stores also explain neonatal complications such as hypoglycemia and hypothermia, while abnormal umbilical artery flow identifies increasing placental resistance.\"],\"tk\":\"Placental-insufficiency FGR is commonly associated with oligohydramnios rather than polyhydramnios.\",\"pr\":\"Absent or reversed umbilical artery end-diastolic flow indicates severe placental vascular disease and materially changes surveillance and delivery planning.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret fetal size together with growth velocity, Dopplers, fluid volume, and maternal disease rather than using a single estimated-weight percentile in isolation.\",\"cn\":\"Placental-insufficiency fetal growth restriction (FGR) typically causes oligohydramnios through reduced renal perfusion, not polyhydramnios.\",\"sl\":\"Redistribution reduces renal perfusion and fetal urine output, causing oligohydramnios (deepest vertical pocket below 2 cm). Polyhydramnios with small for gestational age (SGA) suggests aneuploidy or a syndrome.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[23,28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F246\"},{\"id\":\"F247\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Ultrasound diagnosis of placenta previa\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient is suspected to have placenta previa. Which investigation best defines placental location relative to the internal cervical os?\",\"o\":[\"Digital vaginal examination\",\"Transvaginal ultrasonography\",\"Magnetic resonance imaging as the routine first test\",\"Abdominal palpation alone\",\"Visual estimation of vaginal blood loss\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Digital vaginal examination is avoided until placenta previa is excluded because cervical manipulation may provoke severe hemorrhage.\",\"Transvaginal ultrasonography accurately measures the placental edge relative to the internal cervical os and is safe when performed appropriately.\",\"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is reserved for selected placenta accreta spectrum questions when additional invasion mapping may aid multidisciplinary planning.\",\"Abdominal examination can identify fetal lie and uterine tenderness but cannot determine the placenta-to-os distance.\",\"The amount of visible bleeding helps assess the presentation but cannot establish the anatomic location of the placenta.\"],\"e\":[\"Placenta previa is defined anatomically by placental relationship to the internal cervical os, so accurate localization requires ultrasound imaging.\",\"Transvaginal ultrasound gives high-resolution assessment of the lower uterine segment and is safe for suspected low-lying placenta or previa.\",\"Precise measurement guides follow-up and delivery planning, while digital examination is deferred because disruption of placental tissue over the cervix can cause major hemorrhage.\"],\"tk\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound is the preferred method for accurately defining placental location in suspected placenta previa.\",\"pr\":\"Most placentas labeled low lying at the mid-trimester scan are no longer low lying later because the lower uterine segment develops and the placental edge becomes farther from the os.\",\"tp\":\"Do not perform a digital vaginal examination for unexplained late-pregnancy bleeding until placenta previa has been excluded.\",\"cn\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound is the best and safe test to define placental location relative to the internal os.\",\"sl\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound scan is superior to transabdominal and transperineal approaches and is safe for diagnosing placenta praevia.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[15,41]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F247\"},{\"id\":\"F248\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Frequency of spontaneous miscarriage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about clinically recognized early pregnancy is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Vaginal bleeding complicates fewer than 5% of clinically detected pregnancies\",\"Most first-trimester miscarriages are caused by uterine anomalies\",\"The yolk sac normally appears only after fetal cardiac activity is visible\",\"Fetal cardiac activity is usually first seen on transvaginal ultrasound at 10 to 12 weeks\",\"Approximately 10–20% of clinically recognized pregnancies end in spontaneous miscarriage\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Bleeding complicates about 21% (one in five) of clinically detected pregnancies (Dr. Firas, slide 8), so it is common, not rare.\",\"Most first-trimester losses are due to sporadic fetal chromosomal abnormalities; uterine anomalies and cervical weakness matter mainly in second-trimester loss.\",\"On transvaginal ultrasound the gestational sac appears first, then the yolk sac, then the fetal pole and fetal heart at about 6 weeks.\",\"Embryonic cardiac activity is usually first seen on transvaginal ultrasound at about 5 to 6 weeks.\",\"Spontaneous miscarriage occurs in roughly 10–20% of clinically recognized pregnancies, with chromosomal abnormalities accounting for many early losses.\"],\"e\":[\"Early pregnancy loss is common, and population estimates place spontaneous miscarriage at roughly 10–20% of clinically recognized pregnancies.\",\"The source figure of about 15% lies within this accepted range (Dr. Firas, slide 8).\",\"Most very early losses arise from sporadic chromosomal abnormalities, while maternal age substantially increases the probability of aneuploid conception and miscarriage.\"],\"tk\":\"Spontaneous miscarriage affects roughly one in six clinically recognized pregnancies, with estimates commonly around 10–20%.\",\"pr\":\"The true loss rate from conception is higher because many pregnancies fail before clinical recognition.\",\"tp\":\"When counselling after a single early miscarriage, emphasize that most patients do not require an extensive recurrent-loss investigation.\",\"cn\":\"About 15% of clinically recognized pregnancies end in spontaneous miscarriage.\",\"sl\":\"15% of clinically recognized pregnancies spontaneously miscarry; bleeding complicates 21% of clinically detected pregnancies.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F248\"},{\"id\":\"F249\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ultrasound confirmation of early pregnancy loss\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A transvaginal scan shows an intrauterine fetal pole with a crown-rump length of 5 mm and no visible heartbeat. The patient is stable. What is the most appropriate next step before diagnosing miscarriage?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate methotrexate\",\"Cervical cerclage\",\"Immediate uterine evacuation without repeat imaging\",\"Repeat transvaginal ultrasound after at least 7 days\",\"Serial fetal fibronectin testing\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Methotrexate targets proliferating trophoblastic tissue in selected ectopic pregnancies and is inappropriate when an intrauterine pregnancy of uncertain viability is present.\",\"Cervical cerclage treats selected cervical insufficiency and has no role in establishing viability at 5 mm CRL.\",\"Immediate evacuation risks ending a potentially viable pregnancy because a single early scan can be nondiagnostic at this size.\",\"With a CRL below 7 mm and no heartbeat, National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends a second transvaginal scan a minimum of 7 days later before diagnosing miscarriage.\",\"Fetal fibronectin is used in assessment of preterm-birth risk later in pregnancy, not for first-trimester viability.\"],\"e\":[\"Very early ultrasound viability criteria must be applied strictly because dating and measurement uncertainty can lead to a false diagnosis of pregnancy loss.\",\"A 5-mm CRL without cardiac activity is below the definitive threshold, so the safe next step is repeat transvaginal imaging after at least 7 days.\",\"Waiting for the guideline interval protects against inadvertent termination of a potentially viable pregnancy when embryonic development is simply earlier than expected.\"],\"tk\":\"When early ultrasound viability criteria are not definitive, repeat imaging after the guideline-specified interval before diagnosing miscarriage.\",\"pr\":\"For transabdominal scans, the minimum repeat interval is generally longer than for transvaginal scans because resolution is lower.\",\"tp\":\"Record the exact CRL or mean sac diameter and imaging route; vague phrases such as “small embryo” are insufficient for viability decisions.\",\"cn\":\"Crown-rump length (CRL) below 7 mm without fetal heart is pregnancy of uncertain viability; rescan after 1 week before diagnosing miscarriage.\",\"sl\":\"Gestational sac 25 mm or less, or crown-rump length (CRL) 7 mm or less, with no fetal heart (FH) is intrauterine pregnancy (IUP) of uncertain viability: rescan in 1 week.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[4,5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F249\"},{\"id\":\"F250\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":6,\"L\":31,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antiandrogen therapy for hirsutism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A reproductive-age woman with hirsutism is considering antiandrogen therapy. Which statement is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Reliable contraception is required because antiandrogens can feminize a male fetus\",\"Antiandrogens are preferred during pregnancy because they reduce fetal virilization\",\"Finasteride is used without contraception because it has no fetal sex-development effects\",\"Spironolactone is first-line treatment for hirsutism in a patient actively trying to conceive\",\"Antiandrogens restore ovulation and are therefore used as fertility drugs in anovulatory polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Antiandrogens can interfere with male fetal genital development, so reliable contraception is required during therapy in patients who could become pregnant.\",\"Antiandrogens are contraindicated in pregnancy because fetal androgen blockade can impair male sexual differentiation.\",\"Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase and may disrupt development of male external genitalia after fetal exposure.\",\"Spironolactone is avoided while attempting conception because fetal antiandrogen exposure may impair male genital development.\",\"Antiandrogens treat androgen-mediated hair growth but do not function as ovulation-induction drugs for infertility.\"],\"e\":[\"Antiandrogens such as spironolactone and finasteride can improve hirsutism by reducing androgen action, but they pose fetal sex-development risk.\",\"A patient with pregnancy potential therefore needs reliable contraception while receiving these medications.\",\"The biologic concern is blockade of androgen signaling or conversion to dihydrotestosterone during male genital differentiation, which can cause feminization or undervirilization of a male fetus.\"],\"tk\":\"Antiandrogen therapy for hirsutism requires effective contraception because fetal exposure can impair male genital development.\",\"pr\":\"Clinical improvement in terminal hair growth is slow because treatment affects new hair cycles, so several months may be needed before benefit is obvious.\",\"tp\":\"Discuss pregnancy intentions before starting spironolactone or finasteride and stop antiandrogen therapy before attempting conception.\",\"cn\":\"Antiandrogens for hirsutism need reliable contraception because they can feminize a male fetus.\",\"sl\":\"Androgen antagonists are second-line monotherapy or combined with the oral contraceptive pill (OCP) in severe cases and should be combined with effective contraception to avoid feminization of a male fetus.\",\"s\":[[\"L31_0\",[22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F250\"},{\"id\":\"F251\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Indications for classical cesarean incision\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is not, by itself, a typical indication for a classical vertical uterine incision at cesarean delivery?\",\"o\":[\"An obstructing lower-segment fibroid that prevents safe transverse uterine entry\",\"A transverse lie with the fetal back inferior when extraction through the lower segment is not feasible\",\"Posterior placenta previa without obstruction of the lower uterine segment\",\"An extremely preterm pregnancy with an undeveloped lower uterine segment in selected circumstances\",\"Dense lower-segment adhesions or anatomy that makes a low-transverse uterine incision unsafe\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An obstructing lower-segment fibroid can prevent safe transverse entry and may require an upper-segment vertical incision for delivery.\",\"A difficult transverse lie with the fetal back down can make lower-segment extraction impractical and require a vertical approach.\",\"A posterior placenta previa lies away from the usual anterior uterine entry site, so it does not by itself require a classical incision.\",\"At very early gestations the lower uterine segment may be poorly developed, so a classical incision may be selected when safe extraction through a transverse incision is difficult.\",\"Severe lower-segment adhesive disease or distorted anatomy can make standard low-transverse entry hazardous and require an alternative uterine incision.\"],\"e\":[\"A classical cesarean incision traverses the contractile upper uterine segment and is reserved for situations in which safe lower-segment access or fetal extraction is not feasible.\",\"Posterior placenta previa alone does not obstruct the usual anterior lower-segment entry, so it is not a standard standalone indication for a classical vertical incision.\",\"Because a classical scar has a materially higher risk of future uterine rupture, the technique is avoided when a safe low-transverse incision can accomplish delivery.\"],\"tk\":\"Reserve classical cesarean incision for situations where lower-segment entry or fetal extraction is unsafe or impractical.\",\"pr\":\"After a prior classical uterine incision, labor in a future pregnancy is generally avoided because rupture can occur before or during labor.\",\"tp\":\"Document the uterine incision type clearly in the operative note and discharge summary because it directly determines future birth counselling.\",\"cn\":\"Classical cesarean incision is reserved for inaccessible or unsafe lower segment; posterior placenta previa alone is not an indication.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F251\"},{\"id\":\"F252\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"hope\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Anti-D prophylaxis after early pregnancy loss\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Under current National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance, which statement about anti-D prophylaxis for an unsensitized RhD-negative patient with ectopic pregnancy or miscarriage is correct?\",\"o\":[\"Anti-D is routinely given for every threatened miscarriage from the moment pregnancy is diagnosed\",\"Anti-D is never required before 20 weeks, regardless of gestation or management\",\"Anti-D is routinely required after every spontaneous miscarriage at 8–10 weeks\",\"Anti-D is given only after surgical management and never after medical management at 12 weeks\",\"No anti-D is offered through 11+6 weeks; at 12+0 to 12+6 weeks it is offered when ectopic pregnancy or miscarriage is managed medically or surgically\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Current National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance does not recommend routine anti-D prophylaxis for threatened miscarriage through 11+6 weeks.\",\"The need for anti-D depends on gestational age and management context; a blanket rule of no prophylaxis before 20 weeks is incorrect.\",\"For ectopic pregnancy or miscarriage up to and including 11+6 weeks, NICE advises against anti-D prophylaxis, including after early loss.\",\"At 12+0 to 12+6 weeks, NICE recommends anti-D for both medical management and surgical procedures, not surgery alone.\",\"The 2026 NICE update recommends no anti-D through 11+6 weeks and at least 250 IU from 12+0 to 12+6 weeks when medical or surgical management is used.\"],\"e\":[\"Current guidance for anti-D after early pregnancy loss has changed, so older rules based simply on procedure type can be outdated.\",\"NICE now states no anti-D prophylaxis through 11+6 weeks for ectopic pregnancy, miscarriage, or threatened miscarriage; at 12+0 to 12+6 weeks, prophylaxis is offered when medical or surgical management is used.\",\"The purpose is to prevent maternal rhesus D (RhD) alloimmunization after clinically meaningful fetomaternal red-cell exposure while avoiding unnecessary prophylaxis at very early gestations.\"],\"tk\":\"Apply gestation-specific current guidance for anti-D after early pregnancy loss rather than older blanket rules.\",\"pr\":\"NICE specifies a dose of at least 250 IU (50 micrograms) at 12+0 to 12+6 weeks when prophylaxis is indicated.\",\"tp\":\"Record RhD status, gestational age by ultrasound when dates disagree, and management method before deciding whether anti-D is indicated.\",\"cn\":\"No anti-D for ectopic or miscarriage before 12 weeks; at 12+0 to 12+6 weeks offer it after medical or surgical management.\",\"sl\":\"Anti-D immunoglobulin for all threatened or spontaneous miscarriages at 12 weeks or more and all miscarriages where the uterus is evacuated surgically.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F252\"},{\"id\":\"F253\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antenatal corticosteroids with symptomatic low-lying placenta\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 37-year-old primiparous woman at 32+3 weeks has mild antepartum bleeding and uterine tightenings. A 32-week ultrasound showed a low-lying placenta, and the cardiotocography (CTG) is reassuring. Which intervention should be offered now because preterm birth is a realistic possibility?\",\"o\":[\"Start tocolytics\",\"Vaginal examination\",\"Start antibiotics\",\"Offer corticosteroids\",\"Ultrasound examination\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Tocolysis can sometimes be considered briefly to facilitate steroid administration in selected stable preterm patients, but ongoing antepartum bleeding requires individualized senior assessment before uterine relaxation.\",\"A digital vaginal examination can provoke severe hemorrhage when placenta previa or a low-lying placenta is present and should be avoided until placental location is safely established.\",\"Antibiotics are indicated for infection or specific membrane-rupture protocols; this patient has bleeding and contractions without evidence of infection or preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM).\",\"At 32+3 weeks with contractions and a low-lying placenta, antenatal corticosteroids reduce neonatal respiratory morbidity if delivery occurs within the following days.\",\"Repeat ultrasound assessment can refine placental location and fetal status, but it does not replace antenatal corticosteroids when preterm delivery is threatened.\"],\"e\":[\"Antenatal corticosteroids accelerate fetal lung maturation and reduce respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular hemorrhage, and neonatal death when preterm birth is likely.\",\"This patient is 32+3 weeks, has bleeding and tightenings, and therefore has a clinically meaningful risk of delivery within 7 days despite a reassuring cardiotocography (CTG).\",\"Betamethasone promotes surfactant production and structural lung maturation, so benefit is greatest when the course is completed before preterm birth.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize that antenatal corticosteroids are indicated when preterm birth within 7 days is likely, including in symptomatic placental bleeding when maternal and fetal status permit expectant management.\",\"pr\":\"For placenta previa or low-lying placenta, digital vaginal examination is avoided because cervical manipulation can precipitate major hemorrhage.\",\"tp\":\"In a stable preterm patient with antepartum bleeding, document maternal observations, fetal status, bleeding severity, and delivery contingency planning while steroids are administered.\",\"cn\":\"Antenatal corticosteroids are offered between 24+0 and 34+6 weeks in antepartum haemorrhage (APH) when preterm birth is a risk.\",\"sl\":\"Offer a single course of antenatal corticosteroids to women between 24+0 and 34+6 weeks at risk of preterm birth; they reduce neonatal death, respiratory distress syndrome and intraventricular haemorrhage.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F253\"},{\"id\":\"F254\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Timing of monozygotic embryo division and chorionicity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about twin gestation is accurate?\",\"o\":[\"The most common types of monozygotic twins are dichorionic and diamniotic\",\"The earlier the embryo splits, the more separate the membranes and the placentas will be\",\"Perinatal mortality is reduced\",\"Monozygotic twins incidence is strongly influenced by family history\",\"Less than one-third of spontaneously conceived twins are fraternal\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Most monozygotic twins are monochorionic diamniotic, arising when division occurs after chorion formation but before amnion formation.\",\"An earlier embryonic split produces more independent placental and amniotic structures; very early division can yield dichorionic diamniotic twins.\",\"Twin pregnancy carries higher perinatal morbidity and mortality than singleton pregnancy because prematurity, growth restriction, and monochorionic complications are more frequent.\",\"The rate of monozygotic twinning is relatively constant across populations, whereas dizygotic twinning varies with heredity, maternal age, parity, and fertility treatment.\",\"Most spontaneously conceived twins are dizygotic, so describing fewer than one-third as fraternal reverses the usual distribution.\"],\"e\":[\"Monozygotic chorionicity depends on the timing of embryonic division after fertilization.\",\"The earlier the embryo separates, the more likely the twins are to have separate chorions and amnions; later division produces shared placental or amniotic structures.\",\"Shared chorionic circulation creates vascular anastomoses, which explains complications such as twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome in monochorionic twins.\"],\"tk\":\"Link timing of monozygotic embryo division to chorionicity and amnionicity in twin pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Chorionicity is most accurately established in the first trimester, when the lambda and T signs are easiest to interpret.\",\"tp\":\"At the first twin ultrasound, record chorionicity and amnionicity explicitly because surveillance frequency and complication risk depend on them.\",\"cn\":\"The earlier a monozygotic embryo splits, the more separate its chorions and amnions (day 0 to 3 dichorionic diamniotic).\",\"sl\":\"Dichorionic diamniotic (DCDA): monozygotic (MZ) split day 0 to 3; monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA): day 4 to 8; monochorionic monoamniotic (MCMA): day 8 to 12; conjoined: day 13 or later. DCDA includes all dizygotic twins and about one third of MZ twins.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F254\"},{\"id\":\"F255\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound in placenta previa\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about the diagnosis and management of placenta previa is true?\",\"o\":[\"50% of women diagnosed to have a low-lying placenta in the second trimester continue to have placenta praevia at delivery.\",\"A full bladder is a pre-requisite for transvaginal scanning\",\"90% of patients with placenta praevia present with vaginal bleeding before 32 weeks of gestation\",\"magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is superior to transvaginal scanning in the diagnosis of placenta praevia\",\"Transvaginal scanning is of use in obese women as there are fewer problems with soundwave attenuation\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Most second-trimester low-lying placentas migrate away from the internal os as the lower uterine segment develops, so persistence to delivery is far below 50%.\",\"A full bladder is not required for transvaginal sonography and can distort lower-segment anatomy during transabdominal assessment.\",\"Placenta previa often first bleeds in the third trimester, and the claim that 90% present before 32 weeks greatly overstates early presentation.\",\"Transvaginal ultrasound is the diagnostic standard for placental edge-to-os distance; magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is reserved mainly for selected placenta accreta spectrum assessment.\",\"Because the transvaginal probe is close to the cervix, maternal obesity causes less sound attenuation than with transabdominal placental localization.\"],\"e\":[\"Transvaginal sonography is safe and provides the most accurate assessment of placental relationship to the internal cervical os.\",\"It is particularly useful when transabdominal views are limited by maternal habitus, posterior placentation, or bladder-related distortion.\",\"The short probe-to-target distance improves image resolution because fewer tissues attenuate the ultrasound beam.\"],\"tk\":\"Use transvaginal ultrasound as the preferred test for suspected low-lying placenta or placenta previa.\",\"pr\":\"A low-lying placenta diagnosed at the mid-trimester scan often resolves as the uterus enlarges, so follow-up localization is required before assigning a delivery diagnosis.\",\"tp\":\"When previa is suspected, obtain placental localization before any digital cervical examination and document the placental edge-to-os distance.\",\"cn\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound is the preferred, safe test for placenta previa and is useful when abdominal views are limited, as in obesity.\",\"sl\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound scan is superior to transabdominal and transperineal approaches and is safe.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[41]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F255\"},{\"id\":\"F256\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Clinical manifestations of endometriosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding endometriosis, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Dyspareunia is generally associated with deep-thrust penetration during intercourse\",\"The characteristic triad of symptoms associated with endometriosis is dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and heavy vaginal discharge.\",\"There is no clear relationship between the stage of endometriosis and the frequency and severity of pain symptoms\",\"Dyschezia is experienced with uterosacral, cul-de-sac, and rectosigmoid colon involvement\",\"Premenstrual and postmenstrual spotting is a characteristic symptom of endometriosis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometriosis commonly causes deep dyspareunia, especially when lesions involve the cul-de-sac, uterosacral ligaments, or rectovaginal septum.\",\"The classic symptom cluster is dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic pain or deep dyspareunia, and subfertility; heavy vaginal discharge is not a characteristic component.\",\"The anatomic stage of endometriosis correlates poorly with pain severity, so minimal disease can cause severe symptoms and extensive disease can be relatively silent.\",\"Posterior compartment disease involving the uterosacral ligaments, cul-de-sac, or rectosigmoid can produce dyschezia, often worse during menstruation.\",\"Endometriosis may produce cyclic premenstrual or postmenstrual spotting, particularly with uterine or cervical involvement and associated adenomyosis.\"],\"e\":[\"Endometriosis is ectopic endometrial-like tissue that drives cyclic inflammation, fibrosis, and pain.\",\"Typical complaints include dysmenorrhea, deep dyspareunia, dyschezia, chronic pelvic pain, and subfertility, rather than heavy vaginal discharge.\",\"Repeated bleeding within ectopic implants recruits inflammatory mediators and promotes adhesions and sensitization, explaining pain that may be disproportionate to visible disease burden.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the typical symptom pattern of endometriosis and avoid using vaginal discharge as a defining feature.\",\"pr\":\"Pain severity in endometriosis has poor correlation with surgical stage, so symptom burden should guide clinical attention even with limited imaging findings.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically about cyclic bowel pain, deep dyspareunia, and fertility goals because these details influence both disease mapping and treatment choice.\",\"cn\":\"The classic symptoms of endometriosis are dysmenorrhoea, deep dyspareunia and chronic pelvic pain, not heavy vaginal discharge.\",\"sl\":\"Endometriosis presents with dysmenorrhoea, dyspareunia, chronic pelvic pain, dyschezia and subfertility. Deep dyspareunia occurs particularly with deposits in the vagina or pouch of Douglas.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[14,15,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F256\"},{\"id\":\"F257\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":9,\"L\":43,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Epidemiology and pathology of vulvar carcinoma\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about vulvar carcinoma is false?\",\"o\":[\"50% of vulval malignancies are squamous cell carcinomas\",\"There are up to 1000 new cases each year in the UK\",\"Although high-grade vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia (VIN) is a premalignant stage of squamous carcinoma, carcinoma often arises de novo\",\"Carcinoma of the vulva accounts for 5% of genital tract cancers\",\"Vulval carcinoma is most common after the age of 60\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Approximately 90% of vulvar cancers are squamous cell carcinomas, so describing only half of vulvar malignancies as squamous is inaccurate.\",\"Vulvar cancer is uncommon in the United Kingdom, with annual case numbers on the order of one to two thousand rather than tens of thousands.\",\"High-grade vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia is a precursor lesion, although some vulvar squamous carcinomas arise through HPV-independent pathways without recognized prior vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia (VIN).\",\"Vulvar carcinoma constitutes roughly 5% of gynecologic malignancies, making it far less common than endometrial, ovarian, or cervical cancer.\",\"Vulvar carcinoma occurs predominantly in older women, although HPV-associated disease can present at younger ages.\"],\"e\":[\"The dominant histology of vulvar malignancy is squamous cell carcinoma, accounting for the great majority of cases.\",\"Two major pathways are recognized: HPV-associated disease and HPV-independent disease linked to chronic vulvar dermatoses such as lichen sclerosus.\",\"Persistent epithelial injury and oncogenic signaling permit progression from intraepithelial neoplasia to invasive carcinoma, although invasive disease can also appear without a documented precursor lesion.\"],\"tk\":\"Know that squamous cell carcinoma is the predominant vulvar malignancy and that both HPV-related and HPV-independent pathways occur.\",\"pr\":\"Any persistent vulvar ulcer, plaque, pigment change, or focal thickening that is clinically suspicious requires biopsy, even when symptoms are mild.\",\"tp\":\"During vulvar examination, describe site, size, surface, pigmentation, and proximity to urethra or anus before biopsy or referral.\",\"cn\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma makes up about 90% of vulvar malignancies, not 50%; melanoma is the second commonest.\",\"sl\":\"Vulvar cancer is rare, a disease of the older age group; majority are of squamous origin; melanomas are the second most common malignancy.\",\"s\":[[\"L43_0\",[2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F257\"},{\"id\":\"F258\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Conservative management of stress urinary incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which option is not a conservative treatment for female stress urinary incontinence?\",\"o\":[\"Vaginal cones\",\"Pelvic Floor Muscle Training (PFMT)\",\"Electrical stimulation\",\"Trans Vaginal Tape\",\"Biofeedback\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Vaginal cones can provide resistance-based pelvic-floor training and may be used as an adjunct in selected women.\",\"Supervised pelvic floor muscle training is first-line conservative therapy for stress urinary incontinence.\",\"Electrical stimulation may assist women who cannot generate a voluntary pelvic-floor contraction, although routine use is selective.\",\"A tension-free vaginal tape is a mid-urethral sling operation and therefore represents surgical rather than conservative management.\",\"Biofeedback can help patients recognize an effective pelvic-floor contraction and improve rehabilitation technique.\"],\"e\":[\"Initial treatment of stress urinary incontinence emphasizes pelvic-floor rehabilitation and modifiable risk factors.\",\"Pelvic floor muscle training, with selected use of cones, biofeedback, or stimulation, aims to improve urethral support during rises in intra-abdominal pressure.\",\"A mid-urethral sling such as TVT mechanically supports the urethra and is an operative intervention, generally considered when conservative measures are insufficient and surgery is appropriate.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate conservative pelvic-floor therapies from surgical mid-urethral sling treatment for stress urinary incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"Effective PFMT requires repeated correct pelvic-floor contractions over at least several months, not simply unsupervised advice to perform Kegel exercises.\",\"tp\":\"Before escalating treatment, confirm the leakage pattern with history, examination, and where useful a bladder diary or cough stress test.\",\"cn\":\"Tension-free vaginal tape (TVT) is a surgical mid-urethral sling, not a conservative treatment for stress urinary incontinence (SUI).\",\"sl\":\"SUI conservative measures: pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT), biofeedback, electrical stimulation, vaginal cones, urethral devices. Mid-urethral mesh sling techniques: tension-free vaginal tape (TVT, retropubic) and transobturator tape (TOT).\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[59,65]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F258\"},{\"id\":\"F259\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Physiologic insulin resistance of pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor is least likely to contribute to the physiologic increase in maternal insulin resistance during later pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Adiponectin\",\"Cortisol\",\"Estrogen\",\"Progesterone\",\"Human placental lactogen\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Adiponectin is generally insulin-sensitizing; lower rather than higher concentrations are associated with greater gestational insulin resistance.\",\"Rising cortisol contributes to reduced peripheral insulin sensitivity and greater hepatic glucose availability during pregnancy.\",\"Increasing estrogen participates in endocrine remodeling of maternal glucose metabolism and is among the hormones associated with gestational insulin resistance.\",\"Progesterone can reduce insulin signaling, including effects on insulin-receptor substrate pathways in adipose and skeletal muscle.\",\"Human placental lactogen antagonizes insulin action and promotes lipolysis, helping reserve maternal glucose for fetal use.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal pregnancy becomes progressively insulin resistant, especially in the second and third trimesters, to preserve circulating glucose for the fetus.\",\"Placental and maternal mediators include hPL, placental growth hormone, progesterone, cortisol, estrogen, and inflammatory cytokines such as TNF-α.\",\"Adiponectin has predominantly insulin-sensitizing effects; falling concentrations are associated with impaired glucose disposal and greater risk of gestational dysglycemia.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize that pregnancy-related insulin resistance is driven by placental hormones and cytokines, whereas adiponectin is insulin-sensitizing.\",\"pr\":\"Maternal insulin requirements can fall rapidly after placental delivery because placental diabetogenic hormones decline within days postpartum.\",\"tp\":\"When adjusting insulin late in pregnancy, anticipate rising requirements through the third trimester and reassess promptly after delivery to avoid postpartum hypoglycemia.\",\"cn\":\"Insulin resistance in pregnancy is driven by human placental lactogen (hPL), growth hormone variant, cortisol and progesterone; adiponectin is insulin-sensitising.\",\"sl\":\"Second half of pregnancy: insulin resistance (human placental lactogen [hPL], growth hormone [GH] variant, cortisol, progesterone); insulin sensitivity falls 50-60% by the 3rd trimester.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[9,48]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F259\"},{\"id\":\"F260\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":6,\"L\":31,\"ty\":[\"Cause\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Evaluation and treatment of hirsutism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding hirsutism, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Orlistat is peripherally acting lipase inhibitor\",\"Vaniqa cream(eflornithine) is used for 12 weeks to be effective\",\"Electrolysis, plucking, waxing, shaving and laser will be the first line in the treatment\",\"21- Hydroxylase deficiency is an irreversible cause of hirsutism\",\"Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha- Reductase activity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Orlistat is a peripherally acting gastrointestinal lipase inhibitor used for weight loss, which may indirectly improve hyperandrogenism in patients with obesity and polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).\",\"Topical eflornithine slows facial-hair growth and requires sustained use over several weeks before a visible response develops.\",\"Direct hair-removal methods such as shaving, waxing, electrolysis, and laser/photoepilation can be used early for cosmetic control while endocrine treatment addresses new growth.\",\"Nonclassic 21-hydroxylase deficiency is treatable; glucocorticoid or antiandrogen therapy can reduce androgen excess, so labeling it an irreversible cause is inaccurate.\",\"Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase, thereby reducing dihydrotestosterone formation in androgen-sensitive tissues.\"],\"e\":[\"Hirsutism results from androgen excess, increased follicular androgen sensitivity, or both; common causes include PCOS, idiopathic/familial hirsutism, and nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia.\",\"A woman with suspected 21-hydroxylase deficiency is screened with early-morning 17-hydroxyprogesterone, while rapid virilization warrants evaluation for an androgen-secreting tumor.\",\"Pharmacologic therapy reduces new terminal-hair growth but existing terminal hairs usually require cosmetic removal, so meaningful improvement takes months.\"],\"tk\":\"Evaluate hirsutism by tempo, menstrual pattern, virilization, and targeted androgen testing, including screening for nonclassic 21-hydroxylase deficiency when indicated.\",\"pr\":\"Rapid onset over months, deepening voice, clitoromegaly, or markedly elevated testosterone should trigger urgent assessment for an androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumor.\",\"tp\":\"Before prescribing an antiandrogen such as finasteride or spironolactone, ensure reliable contraception because antiandrogens can impair male fetal genital development.\",\"cn\":\"Non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) from 21-hydroxylase deficiency is a treatable cause of hirsutism, not an irreversible one.\",\"sl\":\"Adult-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) from 21-hydroxylase deficiency causes virilism and is screened with baseline 17-hydroxyprogesterone. Treatment: manage symptoms (electrolysis, plucking, waxing, shaving, laser) and treat the cause; eflornithine is stopped if no improvement after 12 weeks; orlistat is a peripherally acting lipase inhibitor.\",\"s\":[[\"L31_0\",[6,13,19,20,22,24]],[\"L30_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F260\"},{\"id\":\"F261\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":1,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ectopic pregnancy after tubal sterilization\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 37-year-old para 6+2 woman requests permanent female contraception. Which statement about tubal sterilization is true?\",\"o\":[\"Thermal tubal cutting has the highest ectopic pregnancy rate from all female sterilization\",\"Should be performed in the luteal phase to be effective\",\"Filshe clips have the highest failure rate\",\"The failure rate is 1/2000\",\"It is more effective when done at the time of the cesarean section\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pregnancy after bipolar tubal coagulation is uncommon but has a comparatively high proportion and cumulative risk of ectopic implantation among traditional sterilization methods.\",\"Interval sterilization is usually scheduled when pregnancy can be reasonably excluded; performing it specifically in the luteal phase is not required for contraceptive efficacy.\",\"Mechanical clips have a recognized long-term failure risk, but the highest ectopic risk in classic CREST data occurred after bipolar coagulation in younger women.\",\"Female sterilization failure is higher than 1 in 2000 over long-term follow-up and varies by method and age at sterilization.\",\"Sterilization performed during cesarean delivery is convenient, but effectiveness depends mainly on the tubal technique rather than the concurrent cesarean itself.\"],\"e\":[\"Tubal sterilization is highly effective but pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy can still occur years later.\",\"Classic long-term data found particularly high ectopic risk after bipolar coagulation in younger women, compared with postpartum partial salpingectomy.\",\"Recanalization or tuboperitoneal fistula formation can allow sperm passage while impairing embryo transit, producing ectopic implantation after sterilization failure.\"],\"tk\":\"Remember that prior tubal sterilization does not exclude ectopic pregnancy, especially when conception follows a coagulation procedure.\",\"pr\":\"A reproductive-age patient with pain or bleeding after sterilization still needs a pregnancy test because ectopic pregnancy remains possible even many years after the procedure.\",\"tp\":\"Before permanent contraception, counsel about irreversibility, small long-term failure risk, ectopic pregnancy if failure occurs, and highly effective reversible alternatives.\",\"cn\":\"Ectopic pregnancy after tubal sterilization failure is most frequent after thermal (bipolar) coagulation methods.\",\"sl\":\"Sterilization is permanent and usually irreversible; counselling includes failure rates: female tubal occlusion failure rate 1/200, male vasectomy 1/2000, with early and late failure.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[58]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F261\"},{\"id\":\"F262\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Future uterine rupture risk after cesarean incision\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding cesarean delivery, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Maternal morbidity with cesarean delivery is increased compared with vaginal delivery\",\"Perinatal and neonatal mortality and significant neonatal morbidity have been shown to improve from 5.0% for those breeches delivered vaginally to 1.6% for those delivered by cesarean.\",\"Low transverse cesarean delivery (LTCD) incision is associated with a 5% risk for symptomatic uterine rupture in the subsequent pregnancy.\",\"An absolute indication for a cesarean delivery is a previous full-thickness, nontransverse incision through the myometrium.\",\"Cesarean deliveries are classified by the uterine incision, not the skin incision\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cesarean birth generally carries greater maternal surgical morbidity than uncomplicated vaginal birth, including hemorrhage, infection, thromboembolism, and visceral injury.\",\"For term breech presentation, planned cesarean delivery reduces serious perinatal and neonatal morbidity compared with planned vaginal breech birth in many settings.\",\"A prior low-transverse uterine incision has a future symptomatic rupture risk well below 5%, typically under 1% during an appropriately selected trial of labor.\",\"A previous classical or other full-thickness nontransverse uterine incision carries enough rupture risk that labor is generally contraindicated in a subsequent pregnancy.\",\"Cesarean delivery is classified by the uterine incision because that incision determines operative features and future uterine-rupture risk.\"],\"e\":[\"The future obstetric significance of a cesarean depends strongly on the type of uterine scar.\",\"A low-transverse scar has the lowest rupture risk and can permit trial of labor in appropriately selected patients, whereas a classical vertical scar usually precludes labor.\",\"Upper-segment myometrium contracts more forcefully during labor, so scars extending through this region experience greater mechanical stress and dehiscence risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate low-transverse from classical cesarean scars by future uterine rupture risk and suitability for labor.\",\"pr\":\"The skin incision does not reliably reveal the uterine incision, so prior operative notes are valuable when planning a future birth.\",\"tp\":\"When counseling about vaginal birth after caesarean (VBAC), verify the previous uterine incision, number of prior cesareans, indication, and availability of immediate emergency cesarean capability.\",\"cn\":\"Symptomatic uterine rupture after a low-transverse cesarean is about 0.5-1%, not 5%; 5% applies to classical scars.\",\"sl\":\"Uterine rupture incidence: classical cesarean section 5%, lower uterine segment cesarean section (LUSCS) 0.5%.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[55]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F262\"},{\"id\":\"F263\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":6,\"L\":31,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Familial hirsutism with regular menses\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"An 18-year-old woman has regular 26-day menstrual cycles and 6 months of increased facial hair. She has no virilization, medication exposure, or other symptoms. What is the most likely cause?\",\"o\":[\"Exogenous androgen treatment\",\"Ovarian hyperthecosis\",\"Familial hirsutism\",\"Polycystic ovarian syndrome\",\"Late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Exogenous androgen exposure usually requires a medication, supplement, or household-contact history and may cause more abrupt androgenic changes.\",\"Ovarian hyperthecosis usually causes substantial hyperandrogenism and often virilization in older women, rather than mild stable adolescent hirsutism.\",\"Familial or idiopathic hirsutism commonly presents with normal regular cycles, no virilization, and increased terminal hair related to follicular androgen sensitivity.\",\"PCOS often includes ovulatory dysfunction and biochemical or clinical hyperandrogenism; completely regular cycles make it less likely in this presentation.\",\"Nonclassic 21-hydroxylase deficiency can cause hirsutism but often produces menstrual disturbance or biochemical evidence of adrenal androgen excess.\"],\"e\":[\"Hirsutism with regular ovulatory cycles and no virilization can reflect familial or idiopathic increased hair-follicle sensitivity to androgens.\",\"This patient has short-duration facial hair growth but regular 26-day cycles and no virilizing features, making severe ovarian or adrenal androgen excess less likely.\",\"Local 5-alpha-reductase activity and androgen-receptor sensitivity determine conversion of vellus hairs into terminal pigmented hairs even when circulating androgens are normal.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify familial or idiopathic hirsutism when menstrual cycles are regular and virilization is absent.\",\"pr\":\"In eumenorrheic women with only localized unwanted hair and no abnormal hirsutism score, extensive androgen testing may have low diagnostic yield.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about the speed of hair growth, menstrual regularity, voice change, scalp hair loss, clitoromegaly, drugs, and family pattern before ordering broad endocrine testing.\",\"cn\":\"Hirsutism with regular cycles, no virilization and no drug exposure is most likely familial (idiopathic) hirsutism.\",\"sl\":\"Causes of hyperandrogenism: polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) 75%, idiopathic hirsutism 15%, adrenal hyperplasia 3%, ovarian tumour 1%, medications 1%.\",\"s\":[[\"L31_0\",[14,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F103\"},{\"id\":\"F264\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Lactational amenorrhea method criteria\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 30-year-old woman planning an elective cesarean at 39 weeks asks about postpartum contraception. Which counseling statement is accurate?\",\"o\":[\"Combined oral contraceptives can be started immediately after delivery while breastfeeding\",\"Exclusive or nearly exclusive breastfeeding can provide temporary contraception for up to 6 months only if she remains amenorrheic\",\"An intrauterine device cannot be inserted until 6 months postpartum\",\"Vasectomy has an expected failure rate of about 10%\",\"Sterilization performed at cesarean has no possibility of long-term failure\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Immediate postpartum combined hormonal contraception is restricted because of venous-thromboembolism risk and, in breastfeeding patients, early postpartum eligibility limitations.\",\"The lactational amenorrhea method is effective only when three criteria coexist: less than 6 months postpartum, amenorrhea, and fully or nearly fully breastfeeding.\",\"A postplacental intrauterine device (IUD) can be inserted immediately after vaginal or cesarean birth when appropriate, although expulsion risk differs from interval insertion.\",\"Vasectomy is highly effective after semen clearance is confirmed; its expected failure rate is far below 10%.\",\"Tubal sterilization at cesarean is highly effective but long-term failures can occur, so no permanent method should be described as absolutely failure-proof.\"],\"e\":[\"Breastfeeding suppresses hypothalamic gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) pulsatility and can delay ovulation, forming the physiologic basis of lactational amenorrhea contraception.\",\"Protection is reliable only while the patient is amenorrheic, fully or nearly fully breastfeeding, and less than 6 months postpartum.\",\"Frequent suckling raises prolactin and suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis; as feeding intervals lengthen or menstruation returns, ovulation can precede the next visible period.\"],\"tk\":\"Apply the three LAM criteria: amenorrhea, full or near-full breastfeeding, and less than 6 months postpartum.\",\"pr\":\"Return of ovulation may precede the first postpartum menstrual period, so breastfeeding alone is not adequate contraception once any LAM criterion is lost.\",\"tp\":\"Discuss postpartum contraception antenatally and offer methods that can be started immediately postpartum, including implants and appropriately placed IUDs when suitable.\",\"cn\":\"The lactational amenorrhoea method (LAM) is over 98% effective only if fully breastfeeding, amenorrhoeic and under 6 months postpartum.\",\"sl\":\"Lactational amenorrhoea method (LAM) is more than 98% effective if fully breastfeeding, less than 6 months postpartum and amenorrhoeic. Vasectomy failure rate is 1 in 2000 and female sterilisation 1 in 200, with early and late failure.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[10,58]],[\"L35_1\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F264\"},{\"id\":\"F265\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":7,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Professional approach to the gynecologic patient\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about the initial approach to a gynecologic patient is inappropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Knock before entering the patient’s room\",\"Ensure cleanliness, good grooming, and good manners in all patient encounters\",\"Meet the patient initially when she is in examination gown and ready for exam\",\"Address the patient courteously and respectfully\",\"Respect the patient’s privacy and modesty during the interview and examination\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Knocking before entry respects privacy and signals that the clinician will not enter without warning.\",\"Cleanliness, grooming, and professional manners contribute to trust and safe patient-centered care.\",\"Initial introductions and history should occur with the patient appropriately dressed whenever feasible, rather than first meeting her already undressed in an examination gown.\",\"Courteous, respectful forms of address support rapport and preserve patient dignity.\",\"Protection of privacy and modesty is especially important during an intimate gynecologic examination and sensitive history-taking.\"],\"e\":[\"A gynecologic encounter should establish privacy, dignity, consent, and rapport before any intimate examination.\",\"Meeting the patient while she is appropriately dressed allows introductions and history-taking without the vulnerability of being unnecessarily exposed in an examination gown.\",\"Respectful preparation improves communication and gives the patient control over consent, chaperone preference, and the sequence of examination.\"],\"tk\":\"Begin intimate clinical encounters by protecting patient dignity, privacy, informed consent, and choice of chaperone.\",\"pr\":\"Consent for a pelvic examination is an ongoing process; the patient can ask to pause or stop at any time.\",\"tp\":\"Explain each step before touching the patient, offer a chaperone, expose only the area being examined, and re-cover the patient promptly.\",\"cn\":\"A gynaecological encounter starts with introductions while the patient is dressed, protecting privacy, dignity and courtesy before any examination.\",\"sl\":\"Kindness and courtesy must be maintained at all times. The patient's privacy must be respected at all costs, and a male doctor should have a female chaperone.\",\"s\":[[\"L07_0\",[2,3]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F265\"},{\"id\":\"F266\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antenatal corticosteroid timing and benefits\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding antenatal glucocorticoid therapy for threatened preterm birth, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Can be given between 20 and 32 weeks gestational age\",\"Reduces the incidence of intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) in preterm infants\",\"Reduces perinatal mortality\",\"Treatment consists of 2 doses of 12 mg of betamethasone, given intramuscularly 24 hours apart\",\"Reduces the incidence of Respiratory distress syndrome\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Routine corticosteroid treatment is not defined as 20 to 32 weeks; major guidelines recommend a course from about 24 to 33+6 weeks when birth within 7 days is likely, with selected use outside that range.\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids reduce neonatal intraventricular hemorrhage and other complications of prematurity.\",\"A completed corticosteroid course reduces perinatal and neonatal mortality when given before appropriately timed preterm birth.\",\"A standard betamethasone regimen is 12 mg intramuscularly twice, 24 hours apart.\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids reduce the incidence and severity of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.\"],\"e\":[\"Antenatal corticosteroids are among the most effective interventions for improving outcomes after anticipated preterm birth.\",\"The classic regimen is betamethasone 12 mg IM, repeated after 24 hours, when delivery within 7 days is considered likely in the recommended gestational window.\",\"Glucocorticoids accelerate type II pneumocyte maturation and surfactant synthesis, while also reducing cerebral and intestinal complications of prematurity.\"],\"tk\":\"Use antenatal corticosteroids for patients at substantial risk of preterm birth within 7 days in guideline-supported gestational windows.\",\"pr\":\"Antenatal steroids are beneficial in multiple gestation and preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) when otherwise indicated; membrane rupture alone is not a contraindication to a first course.\",\"tp\":\"Before giving a course, document the estimated gestational age, likelihood of birth within 7 days, prior steroid exposure, and reason delivery is anticipated.\",\"cn\":\"Antenatal corticosteroids are given at 24+0 to 34+6 weeks as betamethasone 12 mg intramuscularly (IM) twice, 24 hours apart.\",\"sl\":\"Corticosteroids decrease respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), neonatal mortality and intraventricular haemorrhage; offer at 24+0 to 34+6 weeks in suspected or diagnosed preterm labour. Betamethasone: two doses of 12 mg intramuscularly 24 hours apart.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[31,32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F266\"},{\"id\":\"F267\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Oxytocin and milk ejection\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about the physiology of lactation is true?\",\"o\":[\"Colostrum contains less secretory IgA than mature breast milk\",\"Mastitis is a contraindication to breastfeeding\",\"Oxytocin release is enhanced by close skin-to-skin contact between the infant and breastfeeding woman\",\"The milk ejection reflex is initiated via prolactin\",\"Breast milk volume on day three postpartum is determined by maternal body mass index (BMI)\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Colostrum is rich in secretory IgA and other immune factors, providing concentrated mucosal immune protection compared with later mature milk.\",\"Mastitis usually does not require breastfeeding cessation; continued breast emptying is generally encouraged unless a specific clinical circumstance prevents it.\",\"Close skin-to-skin contact and suckling stimulate oxytocin release, which strengthens the milk ejection reflex.\",\"The milk ejection reflex is mediated by oxytocin, whereas prolactin primarily stimulates milk synthesis.\",\"Early milk volume is governed mainly by secretory activation after placental delivery and effective milk removal, not maternal body mass index (BMI) alone.\"],\"e\":[\"Lactation depends on coordinated prolactin-mediated milk synthesis and oxytocin-mediated milk ejection.\",\"Infant suckling and skin contact activate maternal neuroendocrine pathways that release oxytocin from the posterior pituitary, causing myoepithelial contraction around the alveoli.\",\"Removal of the placenta lowers progesterone, allowing prolactin to drive secretory activation, while repeated breast emptying sustains supply through local and hormonal feedback.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate prolactin-driven milk production from oxytocin-driven milk ejection during breastfeeding.\",\"pr\":\"Colostrum contains concentrated secretory IgA, lactoferrin, leukocytes, and other immune factors despite its relatively small volume.\",\"tp\":\"When milk transfer is poor, observe a full feed and assess position, latch, swallowing, maternal pain, breast emptying, and infant output before assuming low supply.\",\"cn\":\"Oxytocin, released by suckling and skin-to-skin contact, causes milk ejection (let-down); prolactin drives milk synthesis.\",\"sl\":\"Prolactin from the anterior pituitary drives milk synthesis; oxytocin from the posterior pituitary causes myoepithelial contraction (let-down) and is inhibited by stress and pain. Colostrum is immunoglobulin A (IgA)-rich; skin-to-skin and first feed within 1 hour are encouraged.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[15,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F267\"},{\"id\":\"F268\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Iron-deficiency anemia in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the most common anemia encountered during pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Folate-deficiency (megaloblastic) anaemia\",\"Vitamin B12-deficiency anaemia\",\"Beta-thalassaemia trait\",\"Iron deficiency\",\"Sickle cell anaemia\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Folate deficiency is the next most common nutritional anaemia in pregnancy (higher demand, multiple pregnancy, antiepileptic drugs, haemolysis) but is much less common than iron deficiency.\",\"Vitamin B12 deficiency is uncommon in pregnancy (mainly strict vegan diet or malabsorption) and is not the commonest anaemia.\",\"Thalassaemia trait gives a mild microcytic anaemia with normal or raised ferritin and must be distinguished from iron deficiency, but it is much less common than iron deficiency (the slide lists haemoglobinopathies as a cause to consider).\",\"Iron-deficiency anemia is the most common pathologic anemia in pregnancy because iron requirements rise with maternal red-cell expansion and fetal-placental needs.\",\"Sickle cell disease causes a chronic haemolytic anaemia that can worsen in pregnancy, but it affects only a small minority of pregnant women.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy increases iron requirement for maternal erythropoiesis, fetal growth, and the placenta, making iron deficiency the commonest pathologic anemia.\",\"Iron deficiency typically produces low ferritin and microcytic, hypochromic indices, although early deficiency can still be normocytic.\",\"Inadequate iron limits hemoglobin synthesis, reducing oxygen-carrying capacity and eventually depleting tissue iron stores before severe anemia develops.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize iron deficiency as the most common pathologic cause of anemia in pregnancy and confirm it with appropriate iron studies when needed.\",\"pr\":\"A low serum ferritin is the most useful marker of depleted iron stores, although inflammation can raise ferritin and obscure deficiency.\",\"tp\":\"For anemia in pregnancy, review mean corpuscular volume (MCV), ferritin, diet, bleeding history, hemoglobinopathy risk, and response to oral iron before escalating therapy.\",\"cn\":\"Iron deficiency is the commonest cause of anaemia in pregnancy, confirmed by serum ferritin below 30 microg/L.\",\"sl\":\"Commonest cause of anaemia in pregnancy: iron deficiency; serum ferritin below 30 microg/L confirms it. Also consider folate deficiency and haemoglobinopathies (for example beta-thalassaemia trait). Management: trial of oral iron, recheck haemoglobin (Hb) at 2-4 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F268\"},{\"id\":\"F269\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Non-contraceptive benefits of combined hormonal contraception\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which is not a recognized non-contraceptive benefit of combined oral hormonal contraception?\",\"o\":[\"Prevention of formation of physiological ovarian cyst\",\"Prevention of menstrual-related pain symptoms\",\"Delay the occurrence of the menopause\",\"Reduce in the incidence of uterine carcinoma\",\"Containing anti-androgenic progestogens were shown to be effective medications for the treatment of androgenization symptoms (seborrhea, acne, hirsutism, alopecia).\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Combined hormonal contraception suppresses ovulation and can reduce formation of functional ovarian cysts during active use.\",\"By suppressing ovulation and reducing endometrial proliferation, combined pills often improve dysmenorrhea and cycle-related pain.\",\"Combined oral contraceptives do not postpone the biologic age of menopause or preserve the finite ovarian follicle pool.\",\"Long-term combined oral contraceptive use reduces the incidence of endometrial carcinoma, with protection persisting after discontinuation.\",\"Preparations with antiandrogenic progestogenic activity can improve acne and hirsutism by suppressing ovarian androgen production and increasing sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG).\"],\"e\":[\"Combined hormonal contraceptives provide several benefits beyond pregnancy prevention through ovulation suppression and endometrial stabilization.\",\"They can reduce dysmenorrhea, improve bleeding control, lower endometrial and ovarian cancer risk, and help some androgen-related symptoms.\",\"Menopause occurs when the ovarian follicle reserve reaches a critical threshold; temporary ovulation suppression does not prevent ongoing follicular atresia.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish genuine non-contraceptive benefits of combined hormonal contraception from effects it does not have, such as delaying menopause.\",\"pr\":\"The protective association against endometrial and ovarian cancer can persist for years after combined oral contraceptives are stopped.\",\"tp\":\"When choosing a combined method, balance non-contraceptive benefits against venous thromboembolism (VTE) risk, migraine with aura, smoking, hypertension, and other estrogen contraindications.\",\"cn\":\"Combined oral contraceptives (COC) reduce dysmenorrhoea, functional cysts and endometrial and ovarian cancer but do not delay menopause.\",\"sl\":\"Non-contraceptive benefits: decrease menstrual disorders (menorrhagia 50%, dysmenorrhoea 40%, premenstrual syndrome [PMS]), functional ovarian cysts, benign ovarian tumours, benign breast disease, pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), endometrial and ovarian cancer by 50% and colorectal cancer by 20%.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F269\"},{\"id\":\"F270\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Lifetime population risk of ovarian cancer\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 35-year-old woman with no family history of breast or ovarian cancer asks about her lifetime risk of ovarian cancer. Which estimate is closest for the general population?\",\"o\":[\"5 - 6%\",\"7 - 8%\",\"3 - 4%\",\"1 - 2%\",\"< 1%\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A lifetime risk of 5–6% is several-fold above population risk and would imply a substantial inherited or other risk burden.\",\"A risk of 7–8% is far above baseline population incidence and is not appropriate for an average-risk woman.\",\"A risk of 3–4% still overestimates population lifetime risk of ovarian cancer.\",\"The average woman's lifetime ovarian-cancer risk is approximately 1–2%, commonly quoted around 1.1–1.3% depending on the population.\",\"A lifetime risk below 1% underestimates most contemporary population estimates.\"],\"e\":[\"Ovarian cancer is uncommon in the general population, with an average lifetime risk of roughly 1–2%.\",\"A woman without a suggestive personal or family history remains in the population-risk range, whereas BRCA1/2 and Lynch syndrome can raise risk substantially.\",\"Hereditary defects in DNA repair permit accumulation of genomic injury, explaining the much higher malignancy risk in pathogenic-variant carriers than in the general population.\"],\"tk\":\"Recall that the general-population lifetime risk of ovarian cancer is about 1–2%.\",\"pr\":\"The absence of a family history does not completely exclude hereditary cancer because small families, paternal transmission, adoption, or de novo variants can obscure pedigree clues.\",\"tp\":\"Take a three-generation history focused on ovarian, breast, pancreatic, prostate, colorectal, and endometrial cancers before deciding whether genetic assessment is indicated.\",\"cn\":\"The general-population lifetime risk of ovarian cancer is about 1.4%, rising with BRCA1 (45%), BRCA2 (27%) and Lynch syndrome.\",\"sl\":\"General risk to age 74 is 1.4%; 10% are familial. Background risk 1.4%; BRCA1 45%, BRCA2 27%, Lynch syndrome 6-20%; one first-degree relative 4%, two 12%.\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[2,14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F270\"},{\"id\":\"F271\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Risk prediction in shoulder dystocia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about shoulder dystocia is true?\",\"o\":[\"The incidence is about 1 in 1000 deliveries\",\"Maternal hypertension is a risk factor\",\"Can be prevented in the majority of cases\",\"The obstruction is at the pelvic outlet\",\"Many affected babies are of normal birth weight\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Shoulder dystocia occurs more often than 1 in 1000 births, although reported incidence varies with birthweight and diagnostic practice.\",\"The strongest recognized maternal risk factors include diabetes, obesity, prior shoulder dystocia, and fetal macrosomia rather than hypertension alone.\",\"Most cases cannot be predicted or prevented reliably, because many occur without major antenatal risk factors.\",\"The obstruction usually occurs when the anterior fetal shoulder impacts behind the maternal symphysis pubis, not simply at the pelvic outlet.\",\"A substantial proportion of shoulder dystocia occurs in infants of normal birth weight, so absence of macrosomia cannot exclude the emergency.\"],\"e\":[\"Shoulder dystocia is an unpredictable obstetric emergency in which the shoulders fail to deliver with gentle downward traction after the head is born.\",\"Although macrosomia and diabetes increase risk, many episodes involve non-macrosomic infants, limiting the value of risk-factor screening alone.\",\"The anterior shoulder commonly becomes impacted behind the pubic symphysis, so maneuvers such as McRoberts positioning and suprapubic pressure alter pelvic geometry or shoulder position.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize that shoulder dystocia is often unpredictable and can occur in normal-birth-weight infants.\",\"pr\":\"Fundal pressure should be avoided during shoulder dystocia because it can worsen impaction and increase fetal or uterine injury.\",\"tp\":\"When shoulder dystocia occurs, call for help, note the time, stop routine traction, perform McRoberts plus suprapubic pressure, and progress through a rehearsed maneuver sequence.\",\"cn\":\"Shoulder dystocia is largely unpredictable and many affected babies are of normal birth weight.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F271\"},{\"id\":\"F272\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Asymmetric fetal growth restriction\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding fetal growth restriction, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"With asymmetric growth restriction, the head-to-abdominal circumference ratio is normal\",\"The abdominal circumference is the single most effective parameter for predicting intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)\",\"When asymmetric growth restriction occurs the brain is preferentially spared at the expense of abdominal viscera\",\"Two types of fetal growth restriction have been described: symmetric and asymmetric\",\"Serial uterine fundal height measurements as the primary screening tool for IUGR\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"In asymmetric growth restriction, abdominal growth is disproportionately reduced while head growth is relatively spared, so the head-to-abdominal circumference ratio rises rather than remaining normal.\",\"The abdominal circumference is highly sensitive to impaired fetal growth because liver size and subcutaneous fat fall early with placental insufficiency.\",\"Chronic placental insufficiency can produce brain-sparing, preserving cerebral growth at the expense of liver and abdominal soft tissue.\",\"Fetal growth restriction has traditionally been described as symmetric or asymmetric, although modern evaluation emphasizes etiology, centiles, growth velocity, and Doppler findings.\",\"Serial symphysis-fundal height measurement is a common primary screening method for fetal growth problems in low-risk singleton pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Placental insufficiency often causes asymmetric fetal growth restriction, with preferential reduction of the abdominal circumference.\",\"Redistribution of fetal cardiac output preserves the brain and heart, creating relative head sparing with a smaller abdomen.\",\"Reduced hepatic perfusion and glycogen deposition shrink liver size, while altered adipose accretion further decreases the abdominal circumference.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize head sparing and disproportionate abdominal growth reduction as classic features of asymmetric FGR.\",\"pr\":\"Umbilical-artery Doppler abnormalities reflect increasing placental vascular resistance and help stratify surveillance and timing of birth in growth-restricted fetuses.\",\"tp\":\"When growth restriction is suspected, review dating accuracy, serial biometry, amniotic fluid, umbilical-artery Doppler, maternal disease, and fetal anatomy rather than relying on a single estimated weight.\",\"cn\":\"In asymmetric fetal growth restriction (FGR) the head is spared and the abdomen is small, so the head-to-abdominal circumference ratio is raised.\",\"sl\":\"Asymmetric (about 70 to 80%): abdominal circumference (AC) falls first (reduced liver glycogen) while the head is spared by blood-flow redistribution, typical of placental insufficiency; the head circumference to abdominal circumference (HC:AC) ratio stays high.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F272\"},{\"id\":\"F273\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":1,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Gravidity and parity notation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant woman has had three term live births, one preterm birth at 32 weeks whose infant died at 4 weeks, and one miscarriage at 14 weeks. Including the current pregnancy, how should her gravida/para status be recorded?\",\"o\":[\"G5 P4+0\",\"G6 P3+2\",\"G5 P3+1\",\"G6 P5+0\",\"G6 P4+1\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"G5 P4+0 undercounts the current pregnancy and omits the prior miscarriage from gravidity.\",\"G6 P3+2 incorrectly classifies the 32-week preterm birth as a pregnancy loss rather than a prior birth.\",\"G5 P3+1 undercounts total pregnancies and prior births, given the current pregnancy and 32-week delivery.\",\"G6 P5+0 incorrectly counts the 14-week miscarriage as a prior birth.\",\"She is G6 P4+1: six pregnancies total, four births beyond the viability threshold, and one miscarriage before viability.\"],\"e\":[\"Gravidity counts all pregnancies including the current one, regardless of outcome.\",\"This patient has five previous pregnancies plus the current pregnancy, while her three term births and one 32-week birth make four prior viable deliveries, with one 14-week loss.\",\"Neonatal survival does not alter parity; parity records the gestational age at delivery, so the infant who died after preterm birth still contributes to para.\"],\"tk\":\"Calculate gravidity and parity from pregnancy number and gestational age at delivery, not neonatal survival.\",\"pr\":\"A preterm infant who later dies still counts toward parity, whereas an early miscarriage contributes to gravidity and the pregnancy-loss component.\",\"tp\":\"Write the obstetric history chronologically and assign each pregnancy as term/preterm birth, loss, or current pregnancy before converting it to local G/P notation.\",\"cn\":\"Gravidity includes the current pregnancy; parity counts births after viability (24 weeks in Jordan) regardless of neonatal survival.\",\"sl\":\"Gravidity is the total number of pregnancies irrespective of outcome including the current pregnancy; parity is the total number of previous pregnancies carried to viability (24 weeks in Jordan); miscarriage is loss before viability (+).\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F273\"},{\"id\":\"F274\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\",\"Screening\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Fetal-weight discordance in twin pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about twin pregnancy is accurate?\",\"o\":[\"Polyhydramnios is less common in twin pregnancy than in singleton pregnancy\",\"A breech second twin always requires cesarean delivery\",\"An abnormal fetal heart pattern in the second twin should routinely be assessed by fetal scalp blood sampling\",\"Twin gestation itself substantially increases the fetal risk of trisomy 21 independent of maternal age and zygosity\",\"Estimated fetal-weight discordance of 20% or more warrants increased surveillance with Doppler assessment\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Twin gestation has increased risks of amniotic-fluid abnormalities, including polyhydramnios in complications such as twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome.\",\"A non-cephalic second twin does not automatically mandate cesarean birth; breech extraction or internal podalic version can be appropriate in skilled hands after vaginal birth of a cephalic first twin.\",\"Concerning fetal status in the second twin requires rapid clinical assessment and delivery planning; routine scalp blood sampling is not the standard response.\",\"Twin gestation itself does not create an independent large trisomy-21 risk; screening interpretation depends chiefly on maternal age and zygosity and on fetus-specific screening results.\",\"An estimated fetal-weight discordance of at least 20% should trigger closer ultrasound surveillance, including umbilical-artery Doppler assessment for each fetus.\"],\"e\":[\"Twin surveillance must detect complications related to growth discordance, chorionicity, and placental sharing.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends at least weekly monitoring with umbilical-artery Dopplers when EFW discordance reaches 20% or more or either fetus is below the 10th centile.\",\"Unequal placental territory or vascular sharing can produce divergent nutrient and oxygen delivery, leading to selective fetal growth restriction and progressive Doppler abnormalities.\"],\"tk\":\"Escalate twin surveillance when estimated fetal-weight discordance reaches 20% or more.\",\"pr\":\"An EFW discordance of 25% or more plus one fetus below the 10th centile is a clinically important marker of selective fetal growth restriction and warrants specialist fetal-medicine input.\",\"tp\":\"For twins, calculate discordance as (larger EFW − smaller EFW) ÷ larger EFW × 100 and trend it rather than comparing isolated weights.\",\"cn\":\"In twins, estimated fetal weight (EFW) discordance of 20% or more, or any EFW below the 10th centile, needs increased surveillance.\",\"sl\":\"EFW discordance (%) = (EFW larger minus EFW smaller) divided by EFW larger x 100; 20% or more and/or any EFW below the 10th centile means increased surveillance (NICE, National Institute for Health and Care Excellence); umbilical artery Doppler is done at each growth scan.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[21,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F274\"},{\"id\":\"F275\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Appendiceal displacement during pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Acute appendicitis in pregnancy: which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"The usual symptoms may be less apparent during pregnancy\",\"The enlarging uterus displaces the appendix superiorly and medially as pregnancy progresses\",\"Right lower quadrant pain is still the most common presentation\",\"The incidence is constant throughout the three trimesters\",\"The increased white blood cell count is seen in normal pregnancy\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pregnancy can make appendicitis symptoms less typical, particularly as uterine enlargement alters abdominal anatomy and physiologic leukocytosis complicates interpretation.\",\"As the uterus enlarges, the appendix tends to move superiorly and laterally, not superiorly and medially.\",\"Despite anatomic shift, right lower-quadrant pain remains common and appendicitis should remain in the differential throughout pregnancy.\",\"Appendicitis can occur in any trimester, without a reliable trimester in which the diagnosis can be safely dismissed.\",\"Pregnancy normally produces physiologic leukocytosis, reducing the specificity of an elevated white-cell count for appendiceal inflammation.\"],\"e\":[\"Appendicitis is the most common non-obstetric surgical emergency of pregnancy and can present with altered abdominal localization.\",\"The gravid uterus displaces the appendix generally upward and laterally, so pain may move away from McBurney's point as gestation advances.\",\"Diagnostic delay increases the chance of perforation, which raises maternal sepsis, preterm birth, and fetal loss.\"],\"tk\":\"Remember that the gravid uterus tends to displace the appendix superiorly and laterally, while appendicitis remains possible in every trimester.\",\"pr\":\"Ultrasound is often the first imaging test, but magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) without gadolinium is useful when ultrasound is nondiagnostic and clinical suspicion persists.\",\"tp\":\"Do not attribute persistent focal abdominal pain solely to pregnancy; obtain serial examination, surgical input, and pregnancy-appropriate imaging when appendicitis remains plausible.\",\"cn\":\"The gravid uterus classically displaces the appendix upwards and laterally, not medially; right lower quadrant pain remains commonest.\",\"sl\":\"Classic teaching: appendix displaced upwards and laterally with uterine growth (Baer, 1932); right lower quadrant (RLQ) pain is still the commonest presentation. White cell count (WCC) is normally raised in pregnancy, so it is less discriminating.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[44]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F275\"},{\"id\":\"F276\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Initial evaluation of adolescent heavy menstrual bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 14-year-old girl has persistent heavy menstrual bleeding without a pelvic mass or other red-flag symptoms. Which is not an essential initial investigation or step?\",\"o\":[\"Full blood count\",\"Menstrual diary keeping\",\"Routine pelvic ultrasound scan\",\"Coagulation studies if bleeding has been present since menarche or is refractory / there is a personal or family bleeding history.\",\"Assessment for anemia and bleeding impact\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A full blood count is required to detect anemia and estimate the hematologic impact of ongoing heavy bleeding.\",\"A menstrual diary documents bleeding pattern over time, including duration, flooding, clot passage, and treatment response.\",\"Routine pelvic ultrasound is low-yield without pain, a mass, or structural concern in an adolescent whose history suggests nonstructural heavy menstrual bleeding.\",\"Coagulation testing is appropriate when heavy bleeding began at menarche or when personal or family history suggests an inherited bleeding disorder.\",\"Initial evaluation should explicitly assess anemia symptoms and functional impact, including fatigue, dizziness, school absence, and exercise intolerance.\"],\"e\":[\"Heavy menstrual bleeding in adolescents is commonly related to anovulatory cycles, but inherited bleeding disorders must be considered.\",\"Initial workup centers on complete blood count (CBC), bleeding history, pregnancy assessment when relevant, and selective coagulation testing rather than routine structural imaging.\",\"Structural uterine disease is uncommon soon after menarche, so ultrasound has lower yield unless pain, a mass, treatment failure, or another anatomic concern emerges.\"],\"tk\":\"Prioritize hematologic and bleeding-disorder assessment over routine pelvic imaging in uncomplicated adolescent heavy menstrual bleeding.\",\"pr\":\"Heavy bleeding present since menarche plus easy bruising, epistaxis, surgical bleeding, or family history should raise suspicion for von Willebrand disease or another coagulopathy.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about flooding, hourly product changes, clots, syncope, bruising, epistaxis, and family bleeding history before deciding which coagulation tests are needed.\",\"cn\":\"In adolescent heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB), check full blood count (FBC) and exclude a bleeding disorder; routine pelvic ultrasound is not needed.\",\"sl\":\"Case: 16-year-old with heavy periods since menarche, epistaxis and family bruising; must exclude a bleeding disorder (von Willebrand) with full blood count (FBC), ferritin, coagulation studies and haematology referral. Investigations list a coagulation screen as optional and transvaginal ultrasound (TVS) for structural causes.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[13,19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F276\"},{\"id\":\"F277\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":9,\"L\":7,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Vaginal epithelium and pelvic vascular anatomy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding female pelvic anatomy, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Vaginal epithelium is keratinized stratified squamous\",\"The venous drainage from the right ovary is directly into the inferior vena cava\",\"The venous drainage from the left ovary is into the left renal vein\",\"The blood supply to the ovaries is provided by the long ovarian arteries, which arise from the abdominal aorta immediately below the renal arteries.\",\"The cervical canal is lined by simple columnar epithelium\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The vagina is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, not keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.\",\"The right ovarian vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava, paralleling the venous anatomy of the right gonad.\",\"The left ovarian vein drains into the left renal vein, creating an asymmetric venous drainage pattern.\",\"The ovarian arteries arise from the abdominal aorta below the renal arteries and travel in the suspensory ligaments of the ovaries.\",\"The endocervical canal is lined by mucin-secreting simple columnar epithelium, which meets squamous ectocervical epithelium at the transformation zone.\"],\"e\":[\"The lower female genital tract has distinct epithelial zones that reflect mechanical and secretory functions.\",\"Vaginal mucosa is nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, while the endocervix is simple columnar and mucus-producing.\",\"Estrogen promotes glycogen accumulation in vaginal epithelial cells; lactobacilli metabolize this substrate and help maintain an acidic vaginal environment.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish nonkeratinized vaginal squamous epithelium from endocervical simple columnar epithelium.\",\"pr\":\"The cervical transformation zone is where columnar epithelium undergoes squamous metaplasia and where most HPV-related precancerous lesions arise.\",\"tp\":\"During pelvic surgery, remember the right ovarian vein drains to the inferior vena cava (IVC) and the left to the left renal vein, an asymmetry relevant to imaging and thrombosis.\",\"cn\":\"Vaginal epithelium is non-keratinised stratified squamous; the endocervical canal is lined by simple columnar epithelium.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F277\"},{\"id\":\"F278\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":9,\"L\":44,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Anovulation and endometrial hyperplasia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 38-year-old woman with intermenstrual bleeding has an endometrial biopsy showing simple endometrial hyperplasia. Which condition is characteristically associated with this finding?\",\"o\":[\"Hyperprolactinemia\",\"Hypothyroidism\",\"Vagina adenosis\",\"Anovulatory cycles\",\"Hyperthyroidism\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hyperprolactinemia can cause secondary anovulation; endometrial hyperplasia develops from prolonged estrogen exposure without adequate progesterone opposition.\",\"Hypothyroidism may disturb menstrual cycles, but hyperplasia is driven principally by prolonged unopposed estrogen exposure.\",\"Vaginal adenosis is glandular epithelium within the vagina and is unrelated to endometrial proliferative hyperplasia.\",\"Chronic anovulatory cycles provide no cyclic luteal progesterone opposition, allowing sustained estrogen-driven endometrial proliferation.\",\"Hyperthyroidism can alter cycles, usually through ovulatory disturbance rather than direct endometrial stimulation.\"],\"e\":[\"Endometrial hyperplasia develops when endometrial glands receive prolonged estrogen stimulation without adequate progesterone opposition.\",\"Chronic anovulation leaves no corpus luteum and therefore no normal luteal progesterone, making anovulatory cycles a classic clinical setting.\",\"Persistent proliferative signaling increases gland-to-stroma ratio and, when atypia develops, raises the probability of endometrioid carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Connect chronic anovulation with unopposed estrogen and endometrial hyperplasia.\",\"pr\":\"Hyperplasia with atypia/EIN carries a much higher risk of concurrent or future endometrial carcinoma than hyperplasia without atypia.\",\"tp\":\"In reproductive-age patients with recurrent anovulation, provide endometrial protection with cyclic progestogen, combined hormonal contraception, or a levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) when appropriate.\",\"cn\":\"Anovulatory cycles cause unopposed oestrogen exposure, which leads to endometrial hyperplasia.\",\"sl\":\"Anovulatory cycles: unopposed oestrogen gives a thick, fragile endometrium; long-term risk is endometrial hyperplasia and cancer. If anovulation is longstanding, endometrial biopsy should be considered to rule out hyperplasia or carcinoma.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_1\",[6]],[\"L29_0\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F118\"},{\"id\":\"F279\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":6,\"L\":44,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Medical treatment and biology of uterine fibroids\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 31-year-old premenopausal woman with uterine fibroids asks about the condition. Which statement is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Cyclical progesterone can’t be used in pre-menopausal ladies to treat their abnormal uterine bleeding\",\"It’s seen less frequently in parous ladies\",\"Mechanical factor (reduces uterine contractility) it’s a mechanism of abnormal uterine bleeding\",\"The incidence of this uterine tumor is about 30%\",\"It’s a hormone-dependent tumor, it needs Estrogen and progesterone to grow\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cyclical oral progestogens can be used in selected premenopausal women to reduce heavy menstrual bleeding, although they do not remove the fibroid itself.\",\"Fibroids are seen less often in parous women, while nulliparity is associated with greater risk.\",\"Submucosal and intramural fibroids can impair myometrial contractility and uterine hemostasis, contributing to heavy menstrual bleeding.\",\"Uterine leiomyomas are common, with prevalence rising across reproductive age and estimates around 20–30% or higher depending on detection method and population.\",\"Leiomyomas are steroid-hormone responsive, with growth influenced by both estrogen and progesterone and regression common after menopause.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine leiomyomas are benign smooth-muscle tumors whose symptoms depend on size, number, and location.\",\"Medical therapy can control heavy bleeding even when the fibroid remains in place; options may include progestogens, levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) in suitable cavities, tranexamic acid, and other hormonal strategies.\",\"Estrogen and progesterone promote leiomyoma-cell growth and extracellular matrix production, explaining reproductive-age enlargement and typical postmenopausal regression.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize that medical therapy can reduce fibroid-related heavy menstrual bleeding even though it does not excise the fibroid.\",\"pr\":\"A submucosal fibroid is disproportionately associated with heavy bleeding and impaired fertility because it distorts the endometrial cavity.\",\"tp\":\"Before choosing treatment, document cavity distortion, fibroid size/location, anemia, pressure symptoms, and future fertility wishes.\",\"cn\":\"Cyclical progestogen can be used to reduce abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) in premenopausal women, including those with fibroids.\",\"sl\":\"Cyclical progesterone for 21 days of the cycle (day 5 to 26) results in a significant reduction in menstrual blood loss; cyclical oral progestogen is listed as an other medical option, though progestogen alone often fails. Uterine fibroids cause 20-30% of heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB).\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[42,62]],[\"L33_1\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F279\"},{\"id\":\"F280\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Types and epidemiology of breech presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding breech presentation, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"The incidence of breech presentation is 4% of all deliveries\",\"Before 28 weeks, approximately 25% of fetuses are presenting as a breech\",\"At term, 65% of breech fetuses are frank\",\"The major factor predisposing to breech presentation is prematurity\",\"A complete breech has both thighs flexed and one or both knees extended\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Breech presentation occurs in roughly 3–4% of term deliveries, although the incidence is much higher earlier in gestation.\",\"Before 28 weeks, breech presentation is common because the fetus has substantial mobility; rates around 20–25% are plausible.\",\"Frank breech, with flexed hips and extended knees, is the most common breech configuration at term.\",\"Prematurity is a major predisposing factor because many fetuses have not yet undergone spontaneous cephalic version.\",\"A complete breech has both hips and knees flexed; extension of one or both knees describes an incomplete or footling configuration.\"],\"e\":[\"Breech presentation is categorized by the attitude of the fetal hips and knees.\",\"Frank breech has flexed hips and extended knees, complete breech has both hips and knees flexed, and footling/incomplete breech includes an extended lower limb.\",\"Earlier gestations have more uterine space and a higher breech frequency, while spontaneous version to cephalic presentation becomes more common as term approaches.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate frank, complete, and footling breech presentations by hip and knee position.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent breech at term is associated with cord prolapse, head entrapment, and birth trauma, particularly in non-frank configurations.\",\"tp\":\"For a term breech fetus, confirm presentation, estimated weight, head attitude, placental location, anomalies, and eligibility for external cephalic version before finalizing the birth plan.\",\"cn\":\"A complete breech has both hips and both knees flexed; one or both hips extended defines incomplete (footling) breech.\",\"sl\":\"Breech is 30% at 28 weeks and 3-4% of term pregnancies in labour. Frank breech (50-70%): hips flexed, knees extended; complete breech (5-10%): hips and knees flexed; inc";